\title{Symplectic geometric aspects of the Schwarzschild solution}

\author{Romero Solha}\affiliation{email: romerosolha@gmail.com.}\date{\today}
\begin{abstract}
This article details a construction of a symplectic structure for the Schwarzschild solution, and discusses its geometric quantisation. Said structure is defined on the manifold itself, not on its cotangent bundle.
\end{abstract}
\maketitle

%#########################################################################

\section{Schwarzschild spacetime}

Considering $Q=\CR^3-\{0\}$, on $M=Q\times\CR$ it is possible to define a lorentzian structure $\mathrm{g}$. Using linear coordinates $x,y,z,t\in C^\infty(\CR^4)$, their corresponding spherical coordinates satisfy
\begin{equation*}
\left\{\begin{array}{l}
x=r\sin(u)\cos(v) \\
y=r\sin(u)\sin(v) \\
z=r\cos(u) \\
\end{array}\right . \ ,
\end{equation*}and, for a given real number $m$,
\begin{align*}
\mathrm{g}&=\left(1-\frac{2m}{r}\right)^{-1}\ud r\otimes\ud r  \nonumber \\
&\quad+r^2\ud u\otimes\ud u+r^2\sin(u)^2\ud v\otimes\ud v \nonumber \\
&\quad-\left(1-\frac{2m}{r}\right)\ud t\otimes\ud t
\end{align*}is a particular solution of Einstein's equation obtained by Schwarzschild in 1916 \cite{Wald}.

By defining the function
\begin{equation*}
\phi=\ln\left(1-\frac{2m}{r}\right)^{\tfrac{1}{2}}
\end{equation*}one can rewrite the lorentzian structure as
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{g}_0-\e^{2\phi}\ud t\otimes\ud t \ ,
\end{equation*}where $\mathrm{g}_0$ is a riemannian structure on $Q$. This is in accordance to \cite{BerSan}, since the Schwarzschild solution is an example of a $4$-dimensional lorentzian globally hyperbolic manifold \cite{Wald}.

%#########################################################################

\section{Gravitational vector field}

The spacelike vector field
\begin{equation*}
R=-\frac{m}{r^2}\dep{}{r}
\end{equation*}
is the gradient of $-\phi$, i.e.
\begin{equation*}
-\ud\phi=\imath_R\mathrm{g}=-\frac{m}{r^2}\left(1-\frac{2m}{r}\right)^{-1}\ud r \ ,
\end{equation*}whilst the timelike vector field
\begin{equation*}
X=-\left(1-\frac{2m}{r}\right)^{-\tfrac{1}{2}}\dep{}{t}
\end{equation*}is unitary and orthogonal to $R$.

From the volume form
\begin{equation*}
\star 1=r^2\sin(u)\ud u\wedge\ud v\wedge\ud r\wedge\ud t \ ,
\end{equation*}it is possible to define the $2$-form
\begin{align*}
\omega&=-\frac{1}{4\pi}\imath_R\imath_X\star 1 \nonumber \\
&=\frac{m}{4\pi}\left(1-\frac{2m}{r}\right)^{-\tfrac{1}{2}}\sin(u)\ud u\wedge\ud v \ ,
\end{align*}which is both degenerate and not closed; for $\omega\wedge\omega=0$ and
\begin{align*}
\ud\omega&=-\frac{\mathrm{g}(R,R)}{4\pi\e^\phi}r^2\sin(u)\ud u\wedge\ud v\wedge\ud r \nonumber \\
&=-\ud\phi\wedge\omega
\end{align*}provides a volume form on $Q$.

%#########################################################################

\section{Symplectic foliation}

Although $\omega$ cannot define a symplectic structure \cite{Arnold}, together with $-\ud\phi$ they define a symplectic foliation on $Q$. Unfortunately, there is no consensus in the literature regarding the nomenclature of this geometric structure. Some authors call it cosymplectic, others might treat it as a symplectic vector bundle or a Poisson manifold \cite{GMP11}.

Because $-\ud\phi$ is an exact $1$-form (in particular it is closed), its kernel when restricted to the $3$-dimensional manifold $Q$ defines an integrable distribution (the orthogonal complement of $R$) for which its leaves are symplectic with respect to the $2$-form $\omega$. The leaves are $2$-dimensional (in this case they are the concentric spheres with centre at the origin); therefore, $\omega$ restricted to them must be closed. The nondegeneracy of the restriction of $\omega$ is justified by $-\ud\phi\wedge\omega$ defining a volume form on $Q$.

%#########################################################################

\section{Symplectic structure}

Here it is detailed how to obtain (via its lorentzian structure) a symplectic $2$-form on the manifold $M$ itself.

It is well known that the cotangent bundle of any manifold admits a symplectic structure \cite{Arnold}. It is also a straightforward corollary of the results in \cite{Giroux} to obtain a symplectic structure on any orientable globally hyperbolic $4$-dimensional lorentzian manifold, such as $M$. However, these constructions do not use (or are related to) a lorentzian structure.

Although the Hodge dual of $\omega$ is the exact $2$-form
\begin{equation*}
\star\omega=\frac{m}{4\pi r^2}\left(1-\frac{2m}{r}\right)^{-\tfrac{1}{2}}\ud r\wedge\ud t=\ud\left(\frac{\e^\phi\ud t}{4\pi}\right)
\end{equation*}it also cannot be symplectic, because $\star\omega\wedge\star\omega=0$ (it is degenerate). Nevertheless the following holds.

\begin{theorem}
The $2$-form
\begin{equation*}
\varpi=\e^\phi\omega+\star\omega
\end{equation*}provides a symplectic structure to $M$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since
\begin{equation*}
\ud\omega=-\ud\phi\wedge\omega \ ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\ud\e^\phi\omega=\e^\phi\ud\phi\wedge\omega+\e^\phi\ud\omega=0
\end{equation*}and
\begin{align*}
\varpi\wedge\varpi&=(\e^\phi\omega+\star\omega)\wedge(\e^\phi\omega+\star\omega) \nonumber \\
&=2\e^\phi\omega\wedge\star\omega=\frac{2\e^\phi\mathrm{g}(R,R)}{(4\pi)^2}\star 1 \ .
\end{align*}
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This result is true for any orientable globally hyperbolic $4$-dimensional lorentzian manifold such that $\ud\omega=-\ud\phi\wedge\omega$.
\end{remark}

The hamiltonian vector field $H_f$ of a function $f\in C^\infty(M)$ is the unique solution of
\begin{equation*}
\imath_{H_f}\varpi=-\ud f
\end{equation*}\cite{Arnold}. And for the (spherical) coordinate functions they are:
\begin{equation*}
H_u=\frac{4\pi}{m\sin(u)}\dep{}{v} \ ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
H_v=-\frac{4\pi}{m\sin(u)}\dep{}{u} \ ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
H_r=\frac{4\pi r^2}{m}\left(1-\frac{2m}{r}\right)^{\tfrac{1}{2}}\dep{}{t} \ ,
\end{equation*}and
\begin{equation*}
H_t=-\frac{4\pi r^2}{m}\left(1-\frac{2m}{r}\right)^{\tfrac{1}{2}}\dep{}{r} \ .
\end{equation*}Therefore, their Poisson brackets are:
\begin{equation*}
\{u,v\}=\varpi(H_u,H_v)=\frac{4\pi}{m\sin(u)} \ ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
\{u,r\}=\{u,t\}=\{v,r\}=\{v,t\}=0 \ ,
\end{equation*}and
\begin{equation*}
\{r,t\}=\varpi(H_r,H_t)=\frac{4\pi r^2}{m}\left(1-\frac{2m}{r}\right)^{\tfrac{1}{2}} \ .
\end{equation*}

%#########################################################################

\section{Geometric quantisation}

Before applying the machinery of geometric quantisation \cite{Kostant} to the symplectic manifold $(M,\varpi)$, one must first determine if the class of $\varpi$ is integral. Due to the simplicity of the topology of $M$, it is enought to consider any $2$-dimensional compact, without boundary, and oriented submanifold $\Sigma\subset Q$ such that $[\Sigma]\in H_2(\CR^3)$ is trivial. For those surfaces,
\begin{equation*}
\int_{\Sigma}\varpi=\int_{\Sigma}\e^\phi\omega+\int_{\Sigma}\star\omega=\int_{\Sigma}\e^\phi\omega=m \ .
\end{equation*}

At this point one reaches a conundrum. Either $\varpi/m$ is chosen to be the curvature of the prequantum line bundle, or $\varpi$ is. The former avoids dealing with quantisation conditions, though there is a risk it is not aplicable to other examples but the Schwarzschild solution. The latter comes with a bold, yet falsifiable, claim regarding $m$: it must be an integral multiple of a fundamental value.

Here the prequantum line bundle will be a hermitian complex line bundle $\mathscr{L}$ with connexion $\nabla^\varpi$ satisfying
\begin{equation*}
\mathrm{curv}(\nabla^\varpi)=-\frac{\sqrt{-1}}{m}\varpi \ .
\end{equation*}Polarisations will not be considered, since the symplectic structure is defined on $M$ (as oppose to its cotangent bundle): the Hilbert space $\mathscr{H}$ will be formed by sections of the prequantum line bundle which are square integrable (with respect to the volume induced by $\varpi$), $L^2(\Gamma(\mathscr{L}))$.

Following geometric quantisation \cite{Kostant}, to each function $f\in C^\infty(M)$, one associates the linear operator
\begin{equation*}
\hat{f}=m\nabla^\varpi_{H_f}-f
\end{equation*}acting on $\mathscr{H}$, and if $h\in C^\infty(M)$ is another function
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{\{f,h\}}=-\frac{\sqrt{-1}}{m}[\hat{f},\hat{h}] \ .
\end{equation*}Thus, the commutators of the linear operators associated to the (spherical) coordinate functions are:
\begin{equation*}
[\hat{u},\hat{v}]=4\pi\sqrt{-1}\widehat{\sin(u)^{-1}} \ ,
\end{equation*}
\begin{equation*}
[\hat{u},\hat{r}]=[\hat{u},\hat{t}]=[\hat{v},\hat{r}]=[\hat{v},\hat{t}]=0 \ ,
\end{equation*}and
\begin{equation*}
[\hat{u},\hat{v}]=4\pi\sqrt{-1}\widehat{r^2\left(1-\frac{2m}{r}\right)^{\tfrac{1}{2}}} \ .
\end{equation*}

The coordinate function $r$ is particularly interesting because from it one can build a ($1$-dimensional) lenght function as $r$ itself representing the distance from the origin, a ($2$-dimensional) area function as $4\pi r^2$ representing the area of a sphere of radius $r$ centred at the origin, and a ($3$-dimensional) volume function as $4\pi r^3/3$ representing the volume of a sphere of radius $r$ centred at the origin. Their linear operators are related by
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{4\pi r^2}=4\pi r\hat{r}+m\nabla^\varpi_{H_r}
\end{equation*}and
\begin{equation*}
\widehat{\frac{4\pi}{3}r^3}=\frac{4\pi}{3}r^2\hat{r}+2m\nabla^\varpi_{H_r}  \ .
\end{equation*}

Unitary eigenvectors $\psi\in\mathscr{H}$ of the radial distance operator are solutions of
\begin{equation*}
\ell\psi=\hat{r}\psi=m\nabla^\varpi_{H_r}\psi-r\psi \ .
\end{equation*}The study of the Hilbert space $\mathscr{H}$ and the spectrum of the operator $\hat{r}$ will be concluded elsewhere.

