\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}

There has been a large amount of work in the literature on various adjustments to `naive' derivative prices, e.g.~ranging from early work presented in \cite{gatheral_2006}, through \cite{piterbarg_2010}, \cite{burgard_2013} and \cite{green_2014} to, more recently, \cite{burnett_2021a}. These articles have demonstrated the possibilities that are opened by accommodating sophisticated effects (be it volatilities, funding or hedging costs) via adjustments to well-understood base calculations. What has perhaps not been explicitly addressed is the relation of each of these adjustments to the others, placing them all within a unified view of the landscape. That is the primary purpose of this article.

The impetus for this unification was the consideration of adjustments to XVA prices that reflect volatility risk (which we will introduce in Section \ref{sec:meta}), relating to XVA model risk and \cite{benezet_2024} more generally. XVAs are themselves adjustments, generally with relatively exotic model-dependent representations. As such, concretely understanding \emph{model}-related \emph{adjustments} to XVA requires an understanding of some unexplored intersections of past adjustment-related work.\footnote{The adjustment \emph{adjustment} implication in an XVA adjustment is intended. We will use \emph{meta}-adjustment to avoid this. Credit for this prefix goes to Simon O'Callaghan, who originally proposed it for prices or adjustments that are computed via greeks of others.} The provision of a framework suited to handling such risk, via so-called meta-adjustments, is the secondary purpose of this article.

To these ends, in Section \ref{sec:fundamental} we present our \emph{fundamental} representation of pricing adjustments. Then via Sections \ref{sec:examples} and \ref{sec:meta} we demonstrate how this encapsulates past and future, particularly with XVA model risk in mind. In the end, this representation reveals a natural separation of the pricing adjustments universe, into parts (M, D and P) relating to models, discounting, and payoffs.\footnote{This separation is natural given the ingredients in basic pricing rules, e.g.~$\bb{E}[e^{-rT}G(X_T)]$. Notice the \emph{model} $X_t$, \emph{discounting} at rate $r$, and \emph{payoff} $G$, which all stand to be interrogated.} 

Considering a desirable target price $\hat V$ in terms of a convenient base price $V$ plus some adjustment $U$, this separation specifically derives from the associated P\&L bleed $\bleed$ on which $U$ depends intimately. Summarising Equation \eqref{eq:fundamental},
\begin{equation}\label{eq:intro-adjustment}
	U=\bb{E}\left[\int_0^Te^{-\int_0^t\hat R_s ds}\bleed_t dt\right],\quad \bleed = \underbrace{(\hl - \ll)}_{\neq0\Leftrightarrow\rr{\textbf{M}}}V - \underbrace{(\hat R - R)}_{\neq0\Leftrightarrow\rr{\textbf{D}}}V + \underbrace{(\hat F - F)}_{\neq0\Leftrightarrow\rr{\textbf{P}}},
\end{equation}
which is depicted in Figure \ref{fig:venn}. As described in Table \ref{tab:notation}, $\ll$, $R$ and $F$ are the (naive) It\^o generator of a process $X_t$, discount rate, and payoff respectively, all reflected in the base price $V$. Terms with hats are counterparts from $\hat V$. 

\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\centering
	\input{picture}
	\caption{The pricing adjustments universe as suggested by Equation \eqref{eq:intro-adjustment}. Citations show where the historic examples from Section \ref{sec:examples} live.}
	\label{fig:venn}
\end{figure}

The region M of model adjustments in Figure \ref{fig:venn} is arguably the most challenging, so it serves to consider a familiar example from \cite{gatheral_2006}. In Equation (3.4) there, Gatheral writes a call option price $\hat V$ on a stock $S$ in terms of a Black-Scholes price $V$ plus some adjustment $U$, which all together look like
\begin{equation}\label{eq:example-intro}
	\hV = V + U,\quad U=\bb{E}\left[\int_0^T \bleed_t dt\right],\quad \bleed=\frac12\left(\hat \alpha^2 - \alpha^2\right)S^2 \partial_{SS}V.
\end{equation}
The variable $\int_0^T \bleed_t dt$ here represents the (pathwise) P\&L after selling (and delta hedging) the option using the Black-Scholes model with volatility $\alpha$, while $S$ \emph{actually} evolves with volatility $\hat \alpha$. Its representation in terms of the option gamma $\partial_{SS}V$ is often referred to as the \emph{fundamental theorem of derivative trading}. See \cite{ellersgaard_2017} for the history going back to \cite{karoui_1998}, with the experiments of \cite{ahmad_2005} deserving special mention. The associated \emph{pricing adjustment} $U$ was, to the best of our knowledge, first presented in \cite{gatheral_2001}, then refined in \cite{lee_2005}.

In this example, the adjustment specifically reflects Black-Scholes model risk for a call option. What we present in this article both \emph{extends} this model adjustment example to its logical conclusion within an It\^o SDE/parabolic PDE framework, and \emph{unifies} it with the other discounting and payoff adjustments that are typical in XVA. To accompany \cite{gatheral_2006}, for these others we use familiar examples from \cite{piterbarg_2010} and \cite{burgard_2013}.

Implicit in Figure \ref{fig:venn} are diverse applications spanning multiple teams in financial institutions.\footnote{A simple model (e.g.~convexity) adjustment could be handled directly by an origination desk, a payoff adjustment (e.g.~CVA) could be handled by the XVA or capital desk, etc.} For concreteness we will henceforth mostly imagine that a bank sells a derivative to a client at the target price $\hat V$, then distributes its risk management between an origination desk handling $V$ and XVA desk handling $U$. But this should be taken with a pinch of salt, i.e.~idealised abstraction.

Moving to XVA model risk in Section \ref{sec:meta}, $U$ plays the role of $V$ and we call $A=\hat U - U$ a meta-adjustment. Due to the extreme difficulty of computing these accurately, the theory developed thus far serves as a robust framework for constructing signals for adjustments' magnitude and direction.\footnote{In 2001 Gatheral wrote \emph{it is no surprise that this approach doesn't yield any easy results}, essentially regarding $U$ in Equation \eqref{eq:example-intro}. \emph{Easy} would be an understatement for meta-adjustments, depending on future XVA greeks, but this doesn't preclude valuable \emph{results}!} Based around possible P\&L, e.g.~$\int_0^Te^{-\int_0^t\hat R_s ds}\bleed_t dt$ in Equation \eqref{eq:intro-adjustment}, these can be vital towards traders understanding risk \emph{today}, given model upgrades usually take longer.

