\section{Historic adjustment examples}\label{sec:examples}

The goal here is to clarify how our fundamental representation of adjustments from Equation \eqref{eq:fundamental-representation} encapsulates a range of others from the past. With the P\&L bleed breakdown from Equation \eqref{eq:intro-adjustment} in mind, our main focus is on the key \emph{model}, \emph{discounting} and \emph{payoff} adjustment examples shown in Figure \ref{fig:venn}. 

Keep the summary from the end of Section \ref{sec:fundamental} in mind. And since we will have $G^*=0$, our adjustments will reduce to that from Equation \eqref{eq:fundamental}, i.e.
\begin{equation*}
	U=\bb{E}_{t,x}^\hq\left[\int_t^Te^{-\int_t^u\hat R_s ds}\bleed_u du\right],\quad \bleed := (\hl - \ll)V - (\hat R - R)V + (\hat F - F).
\end{equation*}

\subsection{Model: Gatheral (2006)}\label{sec:gatheral}

This is from Chapter 3 of \cite{gatheral_2006}, and was discussed briefly in Section \ref{sec:intro}. 
Here we consider strict model adjustments from Figure \ref{fig:venn}, specifically $\hl\neq\ll$, $\hat R=R=0$, $\hat F=F=0$ and common European payoff $\hat G=G$. So in summary,
\begin{equation*}
	V=\bb{E}_{t,x}^\qq\left[G_T\right], \quad \hat V=\bb{E}_{t,x}^\hq\left[G_T\right],\quad U=\bb{E}_{t,x}^\hq\left[\int_t^T\bleed_u du\right],\quad \bleed = (\hl - \ll)V.
\end{equation*}

So \emph{all} we have to do is specialise the general operator's representation,
\begin{equation*}
	(\hl-\ll)=(\hat\mu-\mu)\cdot\partial_x + \frac12(\hat a - a):\partial_{xx},
\end{equation*}
which is eased by one-factors models here, since then e.g.~$a_{ij}=\rho_{ij}\sigma_{ii}\sigma_{jj}$. We cover local volatility ($n=1$) and stochastic volatility ($n=2$) separately, since our representation provides a different and noteworthy perspective on the latter.

\subsubsection*{Local volatility}

Fix dimensions $d=n=1$, denote a stock price variable $x=S$, process $X_t=S_t$ and functions $\sigma=\sigma(t,S)$, $V=V(t,S)$ etc. Encode driftless Black-Scholes and local volatility dynamics of $S_t$ under $\qq$ and $\hq$ respectively through the functions
\begin{equation*}
	\mu = 0,\quad
	\sigma = \alpha S,\quad
	\hat\mu = 0,\quad
	\hat\sigma= \hat\alpha S,
\end{equation*}
for \emph{constant} Black-Scholes volatility $\alpha>0$, and local volatility \emph{function} $\hat\alpha=\hat\alpha(t,S)$. Now the adjustment $U=\hat V-V$ \emph{purely} reflects volatility risk, i.e.~from
$$
dS_t=\alpha S_t dW_t\quad \xmapsto{\qq\mapsto\hq}\quad dS_t=\hat\alpha_t S_t dW_t.
$$
This yields P\&L bleed $Z = \frac12 (\hat\alpha^2 - \alpha^2)S^2\partial_{SS}V$, so e.g.~$U_0=\hat V_0-V_0$ is given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:local-vol-adj}
	U_0 = \bb{E}_0^\hq\left[\int_0^T \frac12(\hat\alpha_t^2 - \alpha^2)S_t^2(\partial_{SS}V)_t dt\right].
\end{equation}

\subsubsection*{Stochastic volatility}

As in \cite{bergomi_2016} Section 2.4.1, the above generalises to accommodate going from local to stochastic volatility with \emph{any} dimension $n> 1$, and again we get $U_0$ as in Equation \eqref{eq:local-vol-adj}.
%E.g.~consider
%$$
%dS_t=\alpha S_t dW^S_t,\quad \alpha>0\quad \xmapsto{\qq\mapsto\hq}\quad dS_t=\hat\alpha_t S_t dW^S_t,\quad d\hat\alpha_t=\dots
%$$
However then $\hat\alpha$ becomes a \emph{variable} of $\hat V$ in its own right, unlike the \emph{parameter} $\alpha$ of $V$.\footnote{This inconsistent volatility treatment is not in the spirit of this work, since we \emph{explicitly} consider $V$ and $\hat V$ on a common domain $\cc{X}$. Treating $\alpha$ as a variable under $\qq$ instead, with associated ``process'' $d\alpha_t=0$, has tangible consequences. (Think about the associated hedging strategy in Appendix \ref{app:pnl}.) This will come up again in Section \ref{sec:meta}, regarding XVA parameter risk.} Fixing $n=2$ for simplicity, \emph{instead} let $x=(S,\alpha)$ and treat $\alpha$ as a variable of \emph{both} $V$ and $\hat V$, with associated dynamics 
$$
dS_t=\alpha_t S_t dW^S_t,\quad d\alpha_t=0\quad \xmapsto{\qq\mapsto\hq}\quad dS_t=\alpha_t S_t dW^S_t,\quad d\alpha_t=\hat\beta_tdt+\hat\gamma_tdW^\alpha_t,
$$
and common correlation $\hat\rho_{S\alpha}=\rho_{S\alpha}$.\footnote{E.g.~for Heston dynamics choose $\hat\beta$ and $\hat\gamma$ such that $d\alpha^2_t=\kappa(\theta-\alpha^2_t)dt+v\alpha_tdW^\alpha_t$.} Now we explicitly have 2d functions
\begin{equation*}
	\mu =
	\begin{pmatrix}
		0\\
		0
	\end{pmatrix},\quad
	\sigma =
	\begin{pmatrix}
		\alpha S & 0\\
		0 & 0
	\end{pmatrix},\quad
	\hat\mu =
	\begin{pmatrix}
		0\\
		\hat\beta
	\end{pmatrix},\quad
	\hat\sigma=
	\begin{pmatrix}
		\alpha S & 0\\
		0 & \hat\gamma
	\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation*}
and obtain P\&L bleed $Z = \hat\beta\partial_\alpha V + \frac12 \hat\gamma^2\partial_{\alpha\alpha}V + \rho_{S\alpha}\alpha S\hat\gamma\partial_{S\alpha}V$, thus adjustment
\begin{equation*}
	U_0 = \bb{E}_0^\hq\left[\int_0^T \left(\hat\beta_t(\partial_\alpha V)_t + \frac12 \hat\gamma_t^2(\partial_{\alpha\alpha}V)_t + \rho_{S\alpha}\alpha_t S_t\hat\gamma_t(\partial_{S\alpha}V)_t\right) dt\right].
\end{equation*}
Now the gamma term $\partial_{SS}V$ cancels since its coefficient $\hat\sigma^2_{11}-\sigma^2_{11}$ does, and here we have Black-Scholes vega $\partial_\alpha V$, vanna $\partial_{S\alpha}V$ and volga $\partial_{\alpha\alpha}V$ terms instead.\footnote{Implicit in this P\&L bleed $Z$ is a better hedging strategy, compared with Equation \eqref{eq:local-vol-adj}, so we expect a better variance reduction compared with \cite{bergomi_2016} Appendix 8.A.2.}

\subsection{Discounting: Piterbarg (2010)}

Here we consider strict discounting adjustments, starting with $\hl=\ll$, $\hat R\neq R$, $\hat F=F=0$ and $\hat G=G\neq0$ as in \cite{piterbarg_2010}. Then we have prices,
\begin{equation*}
	V=\bb{E}_{t,x}^\qq\left[e^{-\int_t^T R_u du}G_T\right], \quad \hat V=\bb{E}_{t,x}^\qq\left[e^{-\int_t^T \hat R_u du}G_T\right],
\end{equation*}
and we have P\&L bleed $\bleed = -(\hat R - R)V$, so adjustment $U_0 = \hat V_0 - V_0$ given by
\begin{equation}\label{eq:disc-adj}
	U_0=-\bb{E}_0^\qq\left[\int_0^Te^{-\int_0^t \hat R_s ds}(\hat R_t - R_t)V_t dt\right].
\end{equation}
This expression coincides with \cite{piterbarg_2010} Equation (3.2),\footnote{Specifically for the fully collateralised case $C=V$, and using notation $\hat R=r_F$, $R=r_C$. The partially collateralised case is best accommodated as a payoff adjustment (i.e.~a COLVA).} but the scope here is wider given our unconstrained dimension and dynamics of $X_t$, driving everything e.g.~rates $\hat R_t=\hat R(t,X_t)$.\footnote{Indeed, using $\hat R_t=\max(r^1_t, \dots,r^m_t)$ we cover CTD CSAs as in \cite{kemarsky_2025}.} Notice that the form of Equation \eqref{eq:disc-adj} is actually unchanged if we allow exotic $V$ and $\hat V$, provided that $\hat R\neq R$ only, e.g.
\begin{multline*}
	V = \bb{E}_{t,x}^\qq\left[\int_t^T e^{-\int_t^u R_s ds}F_u du + e^{-\int_t^T R_u du} G_T  \right],\\
	\hat V = \bb{E}_{t,x}^\qq\left[\int_t^T e^{-\int_t^u \hat R_s ds} F_u du + e^{-\int_t^T \hat R_u du} G_T  \right].
\end{multline*}
This generalises to the funding invariance principle of \cite{elouerkhaoui_2016} also.


\subsection{Payoff: Burgard \& Kjaer (2013)}\label{sec:burgard}

Here we will recover the CVA formula presented in \cite{burgard_2013} Section 3.3, which as in Figure \ref{fig:venn} lives in the intersection of discounting and payoff adjustments, specifically with $\hl=\ll$, $\hat R\neq R$, $\hat F\neq F=0$ and $\hat G=G\neq0$. Such XVA examples are usually the simplest mathematically.\footnote{Recall \cite{elouerkhaoui_2016}: it is usually just a matter of specifying the exact \emph{payoff}.}


Contrasting the previous examples, for XVA we typically define $V$ and $\hat V$ through PDEs. So specialising the PDEs in Equations \eqref{eq:base-pde} and \eqref{eq:target-pde}, assume
\begin{equation*}
	\partial_t V + \cc{L} V - RV = 0,\quad \partial_t \hat V + \ll \hat V - \hat R\hat V + \hat F=0,\quad \hat V(T,\cdot) = V(T,\cdot) = G,
\end{equation*}
then the PDE in Equation \eqref{eq:adjustment-pde} for the adjustment $U=\hat V-V$ reduces to 
\begin{equation*}
	\partial_t U + \ll U - \hat RU + \hat F = 0,\quad U(T,\cdot) = 0,
\end{equation*}
and from Equation \eqref{eq:succinct-repr} we get the adjustment's stochastic representation,
\begin{equation*}
	U = \bb{E}_{t,x}^\qq\left[\int_t^T e^{-\int_t^u \hat R_sds}\hat F_udu \right].
\end{equation*}
Specialising this further, introduce an interest rate and counterparty hazard rate $x=(r, \lambda, x_3, \dots, x_n)$, assume $R=r$, and that we have discounting difference $\hat R - R = \lambda$ and payoff difference $\hat F -F=\hat F=-\lambda V^+$.\footnote{These differences arise from hedging a loss of $V^+:=\max(V,0)$ due to counterparty default.} Then we obtain
\begin{equation*}
	\partial_t U + \ll U - (r + \lambda)U -\lambda V^+ = 0,\quad U = -\bb{E}_{t,x}^\qq\left[\int_t^T e^{-\int_t^u (r_s + \lambda_s) ds}\lambda_u V^+_udu \right],
\end{equation*}
which coincides with \cite{burgard_2013} Equations (36) and (37).\footnote{Specially in the case of zero recovery $R_C$, funding spread $r_F-r$ and collateral $X$.}

Similarly we can reconcile \emph{strict} payoff adjustments such as KVA from \cite{green_2014} or HVA from \cite{burnett_2021a}. E.g.~write $\hat F=F+f$ for capital or hedging friction cost $f$, with all else equal, to obtain $U = \bb{E}_{t,x}^\qq[\int_t^T e^{-\int_t^u R_sds} f_udu ]$.


