\section{Meta-adjustments}\label{sec:meta}

Finally we return to XVA model risk, which in part necessitated this work, and ultimately revealed the pricing adjustments universe as depicted in Figure \ref{fig:venn}. The goal here is to merely introduce something novel which comes of this work, rather than venture off-piste into what is a brutally challenging topic.

To this end, let $U$ represent some base XVA price, then let $A$ denote a meta-adjustment,\footnote{Recall that meta-adjustment is just terminology to avoid implicitly using adjustment \emph{adjustment}. HVA on XVA as presented in \cite{burnett_2021b} provides an example.} that takes us to some target XVA price, $\hat U = U + A$. XVA are of course already adjustments (to some price $V$), conventionally residing in the collections of discounting ($\hat R\neq R$) and/or payoff adjustments ($\hat F\neq F$) in Figure \ref{fig:venn}, but \emph{not} model adjustments ($\hl \neq \ll$). Focusing on novelties, we consider here some \emph{strict} model adjustments to XVA, i.e.~with $\hat R=R$, $\hat F=F$ and $\hl\neq\ll$. So $A$ lives alongside \cite{gatheral_2006} in Figure \ref{fig:venn}, while $\hat U$ lives in the very centre.

Keeping our CVA example from Section \ref{sec:burgard} in mind, we suppose that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:meta-prices}
	U=\bb{E}_{t,x}^\qq\left[\int_t^Te^{-\int_t^u R_s ds}F_u du\right],\quad \hat U=\bb{E}_{t,x}^\hq\left[\int_t^Te^{-\int_t^u R_s ds} F_u du\right],
\end{equation}
so that $U$ and $\hat U$ differ through the dynamics of $X_t$ only. Now adapting Equation \eqref{eq:fundamental},\footnote{E.g.~by simply changing notation $(V,U)\mapsto(U,A)$. For convenience, we recycle notation $\sigma$, $F$, $\qq$ etc.~from $V$ to define $U$ here, since we will not need to focus on these details of $V$.} our fundamental representation of the \emph{meta}-adjustment $A=\hat U - U$ is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:fundamental-meta}
	A=\bb{E}_{t,x}^\hq\left[\int_t^Te^{-\int_t^u R_s ds}\bleed_u du\right],\quad \bleed := (\hl - \ll)U.
\end{equation}
Here $\bleed$ reflects our P\&L bleed function as usual, now specifically deriving from some XVA model misspecification, i.e.~the use of $X_t$ under $\qq$, with $\qq\not\sim\hq\sim\hp$ for real-world measure $\hp$. Since the compact Equation \eqref{eq:fundamental-meta} is prone to disguising complexities, recall that in full, evaluated at $t=0$, this reads
\begin{equation}\label{eq:3}
	A_0=\bb{E}_0^\hq\left[\int_0^Te^{-\int_0^t R_s ds}\left( (\hat \mu - \mu)_t\cdot (\partial_x U)_t + \frac12 (\hat a - a)_t:(\partial_{xx}U)_t\right) dt\right].
\end{equation}

Depending on $O(n^2)$ future XVA greeks, computing such meta-adjustments accurately presents a monstrous challenge, and traders will firstly be interested in indicative signals $\tilde A\approx A$ for this adjustment's magnitude and direction.\footnote{Indeed, good XVA traders will have developed some such signals, e.g.~based on today's P\&L bleed $\bleed_0$ and the reasonable assumption that $\bleed_t\to0$ as $t\to T$ for XVA, as in Figure \ref{fig:plot}.} Related, traders will be interested in estimating their expected P\&L if they were to not make and hedge such an adjustment, which we have shown in Appendix \ref{app:pnl} is simply given by Equation \eqref{eq:fundamental-meta} but replacing $\hq$ by the real-world $\hp$.\footnote{Note that evaluating the P\&L bleed \emph{process} $\bleed_t=\bleed(t,X_t)$ under $\hp\sim\hq$ means $X_t$ has real drift $\hat\mu^*\neq\hat\mu$, while the \emph{function} $\bleed=\bleed(t,x)$ retains dependence on risk-neutral $\hat\mu$ via $\hl$.}

\subsection*{Toy example}

Now we attempt to give an essentially minimal extension of Gatheral's adjustment, summarised in Equation \eqref{eq:example-intro}, to such a \emph{meta}-adjustment. To this end, rather than consider Black-Scholes volatility risk on a call option with price $V$, we consider instead Ho-Lee \emph{hazard rate volatility risk} on this option's CVA $U$.\footnote{This Gaussian hazard rate model, allowing $\lambda_t<0$, is clearly unrealistic, but is ideal for this example. In particular, it facilitates a straightforward calibration of $\lambda_t$ to a toy survival curve $P_0(T)=e^{-\lambda_0 T}$ under both $\qq$ and $\hq$. I.e.~$\bb{E}^\hq_0[e^{-\int_0^T\lambda_tdt}]=\bb{E}^\qq_0[e^{-\int_0^T\lambda_tdt}] =P_0(T)$.}

Now denote hazard rate and stock price variables $x=(\lambda,S)$, processes $X_t=(\lambda_t,S_t)$ and functions $V=V(t,S)$, $U=U(t,\lambda,S)$ etc. Specifically let the adjustment $U$ in Equation \eqref{eq:meta-prices} be a toy version of the CVA from Section \ref{sec:burgard},
\begin{equation*}
	U=-\bb{E}_{t,x}^\qq\left[\int_t^Te^{-\int_t^u \lambda_s ds}\lambda_u V^+_u du\right]\quad \text{i.e.}\quad R=\lambda,\quad F=-\lambda V^+.
\end{equation*}
As in Equation \eqref{eq:meta-prices}, our target CVA $\hat U$ looks identical to $U$, except for being evaluated under $\hq$. Recalling that e.g.~$dX_t=\mu_tdt+\sigma_tdW_t$, we encode (Ho-Lee and Black-Scholes) dynamics of $\lambda_t$ and $S_t$ under $\qq$ and $\hq$ through the functions
\begin{equation*}
	\mu =
	\begin{pmatrix}
		\sigma_\lambda^2t\\
		0
	\end{pmatrix},\quad
	\sigma =
	\begin{pmatrix}
		\sigma_\lambda & 0\\
		0 & \sigma_S S
	\end{pmatrix},\quad
	\hat\mu =
	\begin{pmatrix}
		\hat \sigma_\lambda^2 t\\
		0
	\end{pmatrix},\quad
	\hat\sigma=
	\begin{pmatrix}
		\hat \sigma_\lambda & 0\\
		0 & \sigma_S S
	\end{pmatrix},
\end{equation*}
for constants $\sigma_\lambda,\hat\sigma_\lambda,\sigma_S>0$, along with correlations $\hat\rho=\rho$. Notice that since $dS_t=\sigma_S S_tdW^S_t$ under both $\qq$ and $\hq$, our meta-adjustment $A=\hat U-U$ \emph{purely} captures the impact from hazard rate volatility risk, summarised by the mapping
$$
d\lambda_t=\sigma_\lambda^2 t dt + \sigma_\lambda dW^\lambda_t\quad \xmapsto{\qq\mapsto\hq}\quad d\lambda_t=\hat\sigma_\lambda^2 t dt + \hat\sigma_\lambda dW^\lambda_t.
$$
Putting this into the meta-adjustment representation in Equation \eqref{eq:3}, we have
\begin{equation}\label{eq:meta-adjustment-full}
	A_0 = \bb{E}_0^\hq\left[\int_0^Te^{-\int_0^t \lambda_s ds}Z_t dt\right],
\end{equation}
with P\&L bleed given by
$$
	Z_t = (\hat\sigma_\lambda^2 - \sigma_\lambda^2)t(\partial_\lambda U)_t + \frac12 (\hat\sigma_\lambda^2 - \sigma_\lambda^2)(\partial_{\lambda\lambda}U)_t + \rho_{\lambda S}(\hat \sigma_\lambda - \sigma_\lambda)\sigma_S S_t(\partial_{\lambda S}U)_t.
$$

This is a particularly interesting meta-adjustment example because, unlike in Gatheral's simpler Equation \eqref{eq:example-intro}, all CVA greeks $\partial_\lambda U$, $\partial_{\lambda\lambda} U$, $\partial_{\lambda S} U$ here are continuous as $\sigma_\lambda\to 0$.\footnote{I.e.~in Gatheral's example, the option gamma $\partial_{SS}V$ goes to a Dirac measure as $\sigma_S\to0$.} Indeed, on this boundary where $\sigma_\lambda=0$, $U$ is given by 
$$
U = -\left(1-e^{-\lambda(T-t)}\right)V,\quad V = C\left(S,K,\sigma_S^2(T-t)\right),
$$
for Black-Scholes call price $C$,\footnote{Recall $C(S,K,v) = S N(d_+) - K N(d_-)$ for normal CDF $N$ and $d_\pm=\frac{\log(S/K)}{\sqrt{v}} \pm \frac{\sqrt{v}}{2}$.} and greeks follow by differentiation. Since $\lambda$ is then essentially a parameter under $\qq$, with ``process'' $d\lambda_t=0$, this suggests that meta-adjustments could be used to signal XVA \emph{parameter} risk more generally. That is, used to estimate the impact of volatility being introduced into XVA model parameters, e.g.~reversion speeds, basis spreads, other volatilities.

\clearpage

\begin{figure}[htbp]
	\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{plot}
	\caption{Summarising Equation \eqref{eq:meta-adjustment-full} as $A_0=\bb{E}[\rr{P\&L}_T]$, here we show paths of P\&L as in the classic \cite{ahmad_2005} Figure 3. The expectation of these paths leads to the CVA meta-adjustment $A_0 =-0.39$ as $t\to T=3$.}
	\label{fig:plot}
\end{figure}

This boundary case of $\sigma_\lambda=0$ can be validated succinctly, e.g.~avoiding advanced numerical methods for simulating future P\&L bleeds via CVA greeks. As in Figure \ref{fig:plot}, this leads to the meta-adjustment $A_0$ via Equation \eqref{eq:meta-adjustment-full}. With inputs $\rho_{\lambda S}=0.9$, $\hat\sigma_\lambda=0.01$, $\sigma_S=0.2$, $\lambda_0=0.05$, $S_0=100$, $K=100$ and $T=3$, we obtain call price $V_0=13.75$, base CVA $U_0=-1.92$ and CVA meta-adjustment $A_0=-0.39$, using 100,000 paths and 1,000 time steps. Evaluating the target CVA directly confirms $\hat U_0=U_0 + A_0$, although doing so via the meta-adjustment $A_0$ provides a significant variance reduction, as noted in Section \ref{sec:gatheral}.


