\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

The proliferation of subscription-based services has become a defining feature of the modern economy. From streaming entertainment (Netflix, Spotify) and software as a service (Adobe Creative Cloud, Microsoft 365) to news media and e-commerce (Amazon Prime), the automatic renewal model is ubiquitous. A central approach in the customer acquisition and growth strategy toolkit for these services is an introductory trial offer, typically a free or low-cost period, after which the consumer is automatically enrolled in a recurring payment plan (commonly referred to as the ``negative option'' model by regulatory bodies). The subscription mode accounts for nearly half the share of mobile apps revenue, despite the small share of subscription-based apps. The global subscription e-commerce market has grown into a multi-billion-dollar industry, underscoring the strategic importance of understanding the design of these contracts.


\begin{figure}
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width= 0.8 \linewidth]{figures/subscriptionstats.png}
    \caption{Distribution of subscription-based mobile apps and their revenue worldwide as of August 2024 (Source: Statista).}
    \label{fig:substats}
\end{figure}

The dominant theoretical explanation for trial offers in economics and marketing is rooted in the problem of experience goods \citep{VillasBoas2006}. In this view, consumers are uncertain about their valuation for a new product. A free trial serves as a mechanism to resolve this uncertainty, allowing consumers to learn their personal fit and make an informed purchase decision. The firm offers the trial to signal quality and persuade high-valuation consumers to adopt, balancing the cost of the trial against the future stream of revenue from converted subscribers. The optimal length of such a trial is thus determined by the time required for this learning process to conclude.

While the learning motive is undeniably important, it provides an incomplete picture of the subscription landscape. We observe trial periods that seem excessively long relative to any plausible learning period. A consumer can likely assess a music streaming service's library and interface within a few days, yet 30-day, 60-day, or even longer trials are standard practice. Furthermore, a substantial body of evidence points to consumer inertia, forgetfulness, and procrastination as significant drivers of subscription retention. Surveys consistently reveal that a large number of consumers pay for subscriptions they no longer use, with forgetfulness cited as a primary reason. This suggests that trial periods may serve a dual purpose: one of facilitating learning, and another, perhaps less benign, of leveraging consumer inattention.

This paper develops a formal theory of subscription contract design that isolates and explores this second channel. We propose a model where the trial length's primary strategic function is to modulate consumer attention. To achieve a clean separation from the learning motive, we assume consumers know their valuation for the service from the outset. The central friction in our model is not uncertainty about value, but the cognitive cost of future actions. A consumer who signs up for a trial and finds the service is not worth the renewal price must remember to perform a specific action, cancellation, by a specific deadline. This act of timely recall is not costless; it requires cognitive resources.

We formalize this friction using the rational inattention framework pioneered by \citet{Sims2003} and adapted for discrete choice by \citet{MatejkaMcKay2015}. In our model, a consumer who wishes to cancel can exert mental effort to increase the probability of successfully doing so. The cost of this effort is governed by their "attention sensitivity." Our key behavioral assumption, grounded in the psychology of prospective memory, is that this attention sensitivity decays with the length of the trial period. A task due tomorrow is more salient and easier to manage than a task due three months from now.

This simple and intuitive assumption generates a novel and fundamental trade-off for a profit-maximizing firm. On one hand, extending the trial period causes consumer attention to decay. This makes it more costly for low-valuation consumers to ensure they cancel, leading to a higher rate of "accidental" renewals. This generates what we term \textit{inattentive revenue}, a stream of profit derived purely from consumer forgetfulness. This creates a powerful incentive for the firm to lengthen the trial period, an incentive entirely absent from standard learning models.

On the other hand, the consumers in our model are fully rational and sophisticated. They are aware of their own cognitive limitations and anticipate the challenges of managing a subscription with a distant deadline. They understand that a longer trial increases the expected cost of monitoring and the risk of a costly mistake. This prospective cost lowers their ex-ante expected utility from signing up for the contract in the first place. We label this effect the \textit{IR-Slack}. It acts as a countervailing force, making the firm's offer less attractive and providing a strong incentive to shorten the trial period.

The optimal contract, comprising a trial length ($T$) and a renewal price ($P$), is designed to resolve this tension. Our first major result demonstrates that this trade-off naturally gives rise to a finite, interior optimal trial length. The model thus explains why firms offer trials that are longer than necessary for learning but are not infinitely long.

We then deploy this framework to generate sharp, testable predictions about the structure of subscription contracts. By imposing standard, empirically-grounded assumptions on the distribution of consumer valuations, we find that trial length and renewal price are strategic complements. A firm that lengthens its trial period to enhance its inattentive revenue stream will also find it optimal to increase its renewal price to extract more value from this channel. This prediction provides a clear point of departure from learning-based models, where the relationship between trial duration and price is ambiguous.

Our framework also serves as a powerful tool for analyzing public policy. Growing regulatory concern about "dark patterns" and exploitative business practices has led to proposals like "click-to-cancel" laws. We model such a policy as an exogenous shock that makes attention effectively cheaper for all consumers. We show that this forces firms to compete less on the inattention margin, leading to shorter optimal trial lengths. The effect on price, however, depends directly on the price elasticity of demand from the firm's base of willing subscribers. This result highlights the potential for well-intentioned regulations to have unintended consequences, underscoring the need for careful, theory-informed policy design.

Finally, we extend our baseline model to consider paid trials. We show that the introductory price and the trial length act as strategic substitutes. An introductory price functions as a screening device, selecting for more committed or higher-valuation consumers. When a firm uses this tool, it diminishes the pool of low-valuation consumers who are the source of inattentive revenue, thereby diluting the incentive to use a long trial. This "dilution principle" illustrates how different contract features can substitute for one another in a firm's strategy to manage consumer behavior.

This paper contributes to three distinct streams of literature. First, we contribute to the rational inattention literature by endogenizing the cost of information; the firm's strategic choice of trial length determines the consumer's cognitive environment. Second, we advance the behavioral industrial organization literature on exploitation contracts by modeling a market with sophisticated consumers who are aware of their cognitive frictions, providing a new micro-foundation for consumer inertia. Third, we offer a new, complementary theory for the economics of subscriptions and marketing strategy, focusing on attention rather than learning as a primary driver of contract design.

The paper proceeds as follows. \Cref{sec:lit} provides a detailed review of the related literature. \Cref{sec:model} formally lays out the model of the consumer's and firm's problems. \Cref{sec:general} presents our main results that hold for a general class of information cost functions. \Cref{sec:shannon} specializes the model to the widely used Shannon entropy cost structure and imposes regularity conditions to derive sharper predictions. \Cref{sec:extension} analyzes the extension to paid trials. \Cref{sec:policy} synthesizes the findings into a discussion of managerial and policy implications. \Cref{sec:conclusion} concludes and outlines avenues for future research.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Related Literature}\label{sec:lit}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

This research is positioned at the intersection of three influential streams of literature: the economic theory of rational inattention, behavioral industrial organization with a focus on contract design, and the marketing and information systems (IS) literature on digital subscriptions and customer management. We synthesize insights from each to build our theory of contract design based on attentional friction.

\subsection{Rational Inattention and Bounded Rationality in Choice}

The theory of rational inattention, introduced by Nobel laureate \citet{Sims1998, Sims2003}, provides a rigorous micro-foundation for information frictions. It posits that agents are not perfectly informed, not because information is unavailable, but because processing it is cognitively costly. Agents must therefore optimally allocate their finite attention. Our work uses the tractable discrete-choice formulation of \citet{MatejkaMcKay2015}, where Shannon entropy models the cost of distinguishing between alternatives, leading to a generalized logit choice model.

While much of the early work in rational inattention focused on macroeconomics, its principles are increasingly applied to microeconomic and marketing contexts. For instance, it provides a theoretical underpinning for why consumers might not react to small price changes or may exhibit inertia in their choices \citep{MackowiakWiederholt2009}. Our primary contribution to this literature is to endogenize the cost of attention. In most RI models, the agent's information processing capacity (our $\lambda_0$) is an exogenous parameter. In our model, the effective attention sensitivity $\lambda(T)$ is a direct function of a firm's strategic choice: the trial length $T$. This connects our work to a growing interest in how market environments and firm strategies can shape consumer cognition. For example, \citet{deClippel2014} study how firms might design complex products to exploit consumers with limited attention. Our model provides a new mechanism, temporal distance, through which firms can strategically influence the cognitive burden on consumers. By making the consumer's attention capacity an outcome of the firm's contract design, we create a feedback loop between market structure and cognitive constraints, allowing us to study how firms might strategically manipulate the informational environment to their advantage.

\subsection{Behavioral IO and Exploitation Contracts}

Our paper is situated within the field of behavioral industrial organization, which examines market outcomes when firms interact with consumers who exhibit psychological biases. A significant strand of this literature focuses on the design of "exploitation contracts" that generate profit from consumer mistakes. A seminal contribution is \citet{GabaixLaibson2006}, who study markets with shrouded attributes. They show that firms may choose to hide the price of add-ons (e.g., printer ink) to exploit "myopic" consumers. Our model shares the theme of firms profiting from a secondary, often overlooked, aspect of a product, in our case, the cost of cancellation.

However, our conception of the consumer is different and aligns more closely with recent behavioral models that assume consumer sophistication. Rather than positing a fraction of naive consumers, all consumers in our model are sophisticated and forward-looking. They are "rationally inattentive," not naively forgetful. They are fully aware of their own cognitive limitations and anticipate that a long trial will make cancellation more difficult. This anticipation is precisely what generates the "IR-Slack," the force that disciplines the firm's incentive to exploit inattention.

In this respect, our work is closer in spirit to models of contracting with sophisticated but time-inconsistent agents, such as those with present-biased preferences \citep{DellaVignaMalmendier2004, HeidhuesKoszegibotond2017}. \citet{DellaVignaMalmendier2004} analyze contracts for gyms and credit cards, showing that firms will optimally design contracts to take advantage of consumers' self-control problems, even when consumers are sophisticated. Our paper provides a parallel analysis where the friction is inattention rather than present bias. We thus contribute to this literature by proposing a new, attention-based mechanism for consumer inertia and showing how it can be embedded within a contract design problem with sophisticated agents. This approach also resonates with the marketing literature on "sludge," which examines how firms can increase cognitive friction to guide consumer choices, often to the consumer's detriment \citep{thaler2018nudge}.

Our theoretical framework finds strong empirical support in a recent large-scale field experiment by \citet{Miller2023}. In a study with over two million newspaper readers, they experimentally vary the presence of an automatic renewal clause. Their findings validate the two central, opposing forces in our model. First, they document powerful inertia, finding that a large fraction of consumers who are defaulted into a paid subscription remain subscribed while exhibiting little to no usage, which corresponds to the \textit{inattentive revenue} channel we model. Second, and critically, they find strong evidence of consumer sophistication, showing that initial take-up of auto-renewal offers is 24\%--36\% lower than for equivalent auto-cancel offers. This provides a direct empirical measurement of the choice-based deterrence that we label the \textit{IR-Slack}. Their estimate that a majority of inert consumers are sophisticated and anticipate their future inertia lends crucial support to our decision to model consumers as rational agents who are aware of their own cognitive frictions, rather than as naively myopic agents.

\subsection{Marketing Strategy and Subscription Models}

There is a vast literature in marketing and related business fields on pricing and promotion strategies for digital goods. The classic explanation for free trials is to facilitate consumer learning and reduce perceived risk for experience goods \citep{VillasBoas2006}. In this context, trials are a form of promotional spending to drive adoption. For instance, \citet{Narayan2011} study how seeding strategies and free trials can be used to launch new products in the presence of network effects. Our model provides a complementary, attention-based rationale that can explain trial lengths that extend beyond any plausible learning period. These two mechanisms, learning and attention, are not mutually exclusive. A complete model of subscription trials would likely include both, but our contribution is to isolate the attention channel and derive its unique strategic implications.

Our work also connects to the extensive literature on customer relationship management (CRM) and customer inertia. It is well-documented that acquiring a new customer is often more expensive than retaining an existing one, leading firms to focus on maximizing customer lifetime value (CLV) \citep{Gupta2004}. Firms use various strategies to increase switching costs and lock in their customer base. While much of this literature focuses on contractual or technological lock-in \citep{Farrell1989}, our model provides a specific, micro-founded mechanism for a form of psychological lock-in: the inertia that arises from the cognitive effort required to terminate a relationship. The firm's choice of trial length can be seen as a strategic decision to set the level of this "cognitive switching cost." This adds a new dimension to the study of customer retention, suggesting that retention can be "engineered" not just through loyalty programs but through the careful design of contractual friction.

The context of our model, digital subscriptions, is central to the modern marketing, MIS, and platform economics literature. Research in this area has explored various facets of the freemium and subscription models, including conversion from free to paid tiers \citep{Kumar2014}, the impact of network effects \citep{Sundararajan2004}, and the role of user engagement in retention. Our work contributes to this stream by focusing on a previously under-explored aspect of the model: the automatic renewal feature and the role of trial length in leveraging consumer inattention. This connects to empirical findings on the power of defaults and status quo bias in digital settings \citep{Johnson2002}. Our model provides a rational, choice-theoretic explanation for why such defaults are so powerful: overcoming them requires costly cognitive effort.

Finally, our analysis of "click-to-cancel" regulations and other policy interventions speaks to the growing literature on "dark patterns" in user interface design \citep{Luguri2021}. These are design choices that steer users toward outcomes that benefit the firm but not necessarily the user. A long trial period paired with a complex, multi-step cancellation process can be viewed as a contractual dark pattern. Our model provides a formal economic framework for analyzing the incentives behind such practices and for evaluating the market-wide consequences of policies designed to curb them. By formalizing the trade-offs involved, we can move beyond a purely descriptive account of these practices to a predictive analysis of how firms will respond to regulation, providing a valuable tool for both managers and policymakers in the digital economy.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section{Model}\label{sec:model}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

We develop a model of a monopoly subscription platform to analyze how a firm designs its introductory contract, comprising a trial length and a post-trial price, for a market of consumers who are fully rational but subject to attentional frictions. Our framework isolates the role of trial length as a strategic tool for managing consumer attention, distinct from its traditional role in facilitating learning. The model is structured to be rich enough to capture the essential behavioral trade-offs while remaining tractable enough to yield sharp, testable predictions.

\subsection{Primitives and Timeline}\label{sec:primitives}

\paragraph{Consumers and Valuations.} We consider a market with a unit mass of consumers, each characterized by a per-period valuation $v$ for the platform's service. This valuation is the consumer's private information and is drawn from a cumulative distribution function $F(v)$ with continuous density $f(v)>0$ on the support $[0,1]$. A crucial modeling choice, which represents a clear departure from the dominant literature on trial offers, is that consumers know their valuation $v$ from the outset. This assumption allows us to cleanly isolate the role of attention from the role of learning. In a learning model \citep{VillasBoas2006}, the trial period's primary function is to allow consumers to resolve uncertainty about $v$. By assuming $v$ is known, we create a setting where, from a purely informational perspective, no trial period is necessary. The existence of a non-zero optimal trial length in our model will therefore be attributable solely to attentional frictions, providing a complementary and previously unformalized rationale for this ubiquitous marketing tool.

\paragraph{The Firm and the Contract.} A monopolist firm offers a subscription contract defined by a pair $(T,P)$, where $T \in \mathbb{N}$ is the length of the free trial period (e.g., in days or weeks), and $P \in (0,1]$ is the price charged automatically upon renewal at the end of the trial. The firm's marginal cost of production is normalized to zero, a common approximation for digital goods. We ignore time discounting within the initial subscription cycle for simplicity. The monopoly setting allows us to focus purely on the firm's contract design incentives without the confounding effects of competition, representing markets for highly differentiated products (e.g., specialized SaaS like Salesforce) or platforms with dominant market positions (e.g., Adobe Creative Cloud).

\paragraph{Sequence of Events.} The strategic interaction unfolds over a clear sequence of stages, which mirrors the typical customer journey for a subscription service:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.8em]
    \item \textbf{Stage 0 (Contract Announcement):} The firm chooses and publicly commits to a contract $(T,P)$. This commitment is essential; the firm cannot change the terms ex-post for consumers who have signed up.
    \item \textbf{Stage 1 (Sign-up Decision):} Each consumer observes the contract terms $(T,P)$. Knowing their own valuation $v$, they form expectations about the future costs and benefits of the contract, including the cognitive burden of managing the subscription. Based on this forward-looking assessment, they decide whether to sign up.
    \item \textbf{Stage 2 (Monitoring Decision):} During the trial period, a consumer with a low valuation ($v<P$) who intends to cancel must decide on a monitoring strategy. This is an abstract choice representing the level of costly cognitive effort they will exert to set up a "reminder" to cancel the subscription before it renews. This could involve literally setting a calendar alert, placing a sticky note on a monitor, or simply trying to keep the task "top of mind."
    \item \textbf{Stage 3 (Renewal):} At the end of the trial, the subscription automatically renews at price $P$ unless the consumer has successfully executed a cancellation action.
\end{itemize}

