\section*{Appendix A: Proofs of General Results}

This appendix provides detailed mathematical proofs for the lemmas and propositions of results without the Shannon cost functional form assumption.

\subsection*{A.1  Proof of Lemma \ref{lem:qstar} (Optimal monitoring)}

For any consumer whose valuation is below the renewal price, \(v<P\),
the cancellation reminder decision is

\[
\min_{q\in[0,1]}
\;L(q;P,\lambda)
\;=\;
(1-q)P \;+\; \frac{1}{\lambda}\,H(q),
\qquad
H(q)=q\ln q + (1-q)\ln(1-q),
\tag{A.1}\label{eq:A.1}
\]

with cost parameter \(\lambda=\lambda(T)=\lambda_0/(1+\beta T)\).

First, we compute the first and second derivatives of the entropy term:

\[
H'(q)=\ln\!\Bigl(\tfrac{q}{1-q}\Bigr),\qquad
H''(q)=\frac{1}{q(1-q)}>0
\quad\text{for }q\in(0,1).
\]

Hence \(H(q)\) is strictly convex; scaling by the positive constant
\(1/\lambda\) preserves convexity.
The linear loss \((1-q)P\) is also convex, so the entire objective
\(L(q;P,\lambda)\) is \emph{strictly convex} on \((0,1)\).

Therefore any stationary point is the \emph{unique} global minimizer.

Next, the derivative of \(L\) is

\[
\frac{\partial L}{\partial q}
= -P + \frac{1}{\lambda}\,H'(q)
= -P + \frac{1}{\lambda}\,
                  \ln\!\Bigl(\tfrac{q}{1-q}\Bigr).
\]

Setting \(\partial L/\partial q=0\) yields

\[
\ln\!\Bigl(\tfrac{q}{1-q}\Bigr)=\lambda P
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\frac{q}{1-q}=e^{\lambda P}
\;\Longrightarrow\;
q^{*}(P,\lambda)=\frac{1}{1+e^{-\lambda P}}.
\tag{A.2}\label{eq:A.2}
\]

Because \(L\) is strictly convex and \(q^{*}\in(0,1)\),
expression \eqref{eq:A.2} is the unique minimizer. Next, define \(q^{*}\equiv q^{*}(P,\lambda)\).
Using \(q^{*}(1-q^{*})=e^{-\lambda P}/(1+e^{-\lambda P})^{2}\):

\[
\frac{\partial q^{*}}{\partial P}
  =\lambda q^{*}(1-q^{*})>0,
\qquad
\frac{\partial q^{*}}{\partial \lambda}
  =P\,q^{*}(1-q^{*})>0.
\tag{A.3}\label{eq:A.3}
\]

\paragraph{Sensitivity to trial length \(T\).}
Since \(\lambda(T)=\lambda_0/(1+\beta T)\) with \(\beta>0\),

\[
\frac{\partial q^{*}}{\partial T}
  =\frac{\partial q^{*}}{\partial\lambda}\;
     \frac{d\lambda}{dT}
  =P\,q^{*}(1-q^{*})\;\Bigl(-\frac{\beta\lambda_0}{(1+\beta T)^{2}}\Bigr)
  <0.
\tag{A.4}\label{eq:A.4}
\]


\paragraph{Boundary checks.}
At \(q=0\) the derivative of \(L\) is \( -P-\infty<0\); at \(q=1\) it is
\( -P+\infty>0\).  
Hence the unique stationary point in \((0,1)\) is indeed a minimum, and no corner solution can dominate it.

Equations \eqref{eq:A.2}–\eqref{eq:A.4} establish
\[
q^{*}(P,\lambda)
      =\frac{1}{1+\exp(-\lambda P)},\quad
\partial_P q^{*}>0,\;
\partial_\lambda q^{*}>0,\;
\partial_T q^{*}<0,
\]
thus completing the proof. \qed
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection*{A.2. Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:F1} (Attention vs. Trial length)}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

For a fixed renewal price \(P>0\) recall that  
\(q^{*}(P,\lambda)=\bigl[1+\exp(-\lambda P)\bigr]^{-1}\)  
from Lemma \ref{lem:qstar} and that
\(\lambda(T)=\lambda_{0}/(1+\beta T)\) with \(\beta>0\)
(MA‑\ref{MA3}).  By the chain rule
\[
\frac{\partial q^{*}}{\partial T}
  \;=\;
  \frac{\partial q^{*}}{\partial\lambda}\;
  \frac{d\lambda}{dT}.
\tag{A.2.1}\label{eq:A.2.1}
\]

\paragraph{Step 1. Sign of \(\partial q^{*}/\partial\lambda\).}
Differentiate the closed‑form expression:

\[
\frac{\partial q^{*}}{\partial\lambda}
   =  \frac{\partial}{\partial\lambda}
      \Bigl[1+\exp(-\lambda P)\Bigr]^{-1}
   =  P\,q^{*}(1-q^{*}) \;>\;0,
\tag{A.2.2}
\]

because \(q^{*}\in(0,1)\) for any \(\lambda,P>0\).

\paragraph{Step 2. Sign of \(d\lambda/dT\).}
From \(\lambda(T)=\lambda_{0}(1+\beta T)^{-1}\):

\[
\frac{d\lambda}{dT}
   = -\frac{\beta\lambda_{0}}{(1+\beta T)^{2}}
   \;<\;0
\quad\text{for }T\ge0,\;\beta>0.
\tag{A.2.3}
\]

\paragraph{Step 3. Combine signs.}
Multiplying \eqref{eq:A.2.2} and \eqref{eq:A.2.3} inside
\eqref{eq:A.2.1} yields

\[
\boxed{\;
  \dfrac{\partial q^{*}(P,\lambda(T))}{\partial T} < 0
  \;}
\qquad\text{for all }P>0,\;T\ge0,\;\beta>0 .
\]

Strict inequality holds because both factors are strictly non‑zero.
\hfill\(\blacksquare\)

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection*{A.3. Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:F2} (Monotonicity of inattentive revenue)}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Fix \(P>0\).
Definition \eqref{eq:IR} gives
\[
\IR(T,P)
   = P\int_{0}^{P}
       \bigl[1-q^{*}(P,\lambda(T))\bigr]\,f(v)\,dv
   = P\,F(P)\,\bigl[1-q^{*}(P,\lambda(T))\bigr],
\tag{A.3.1}\label{eq:A.3.1}
\]
because \(q^{*}\) does not depend on \(v\) for \(v<P\).

\paragraph{Step1. Differentiate w.r.t.\ \(T\).}
Using \eqref{eq:A.3.1} and the product rule:

\[
\frac{\partial \IR}{\partial T}
   = P\,F(P)\;
     \Bigl[-\,\frac{\partial q^{*}}{\partial T}\Bigr].
\tag{A.3.2}\label{eq:A.3.2}
\]

\paragraph{Step 2. Sign of the derivative.}
From Proposition \ref{prop:F1},
\(\partial q^{*}/\partial T < 0\);
hence \(-\partial q^{*}/\partial T > 0\).

* If \(F(P)>0\) (i.e.\ there exists a positive mass of \(v<P\)),
  the factors \(P\), \(F(P)\), and
  \(-\partial q^{*}/\partial T\) are all strictly positive, so

  \[
  \boxed{\;
    \frac{\partial \IR}{\partial T} > 0
    \quad(\text{strict monotonicity})
    \;}
  \]

* If \(F(P)=0\) (no consumer has \(v<P\)), then \(\IR\equiv0\) and the derivative is zero-yielding weak monotonicity.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
Inattentive revenue is (weakly) increasing in trial length \(T\),
and strictly increasing whenever a strictly positive share of consumers
would prefer to cancel at price \(P\). \qed
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection*{A.4. Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:F3} (Interior optimal $T^{*}$)}


Throughout this proof $P>0$ is fixed and Assumptions \textbf{MA‑1},
\textbf{MA‑3} (\(\beta>0\)) and \textbf{C1}(i) (\(f(0)>0\))
hold.

\paragraph{Derivative of profit with respect to $T$.}

From \eqref{eq:profit},
\[
\frac{\partial\Pi}{\partial T}(T,P)
   = P\,\frac{\partial\IR}{\partial T}(T,P)
     \;-\;\IRSlack(T,P),
\tag{A.4.1}\label{eq:A.4.1}
\] exactly as stated in the proposition.


\paragraph{Sign near $T=0$.}
Using \eqref{eq:IR} and Lemma \ref{lem:qstar},
\(
\partial q^{*}/\partial T
     = -\beta\lambda_{0}P\,q^{*}(1-q^{*})/(1+\beta T)^{2}.
\)
Hence, at \(T=0\)

\[
\left.\frac{\partial \IR}{\partial T}\right|_{T=0}
   = P\int_{0}^{P}
       \!\!\Bigl[-\frac{\partial q^{*}}{\partial T}\Bigr]_{T=0}\,f(v)\,dv
   = \beta\lambda_{0} P^{2}
     q^{*}_{0}\bigl(1-q^{*}_{0}\bigr)\,f(0) \;+\;O(P^{3}),
\tag{A.4.2}\label{eq:A.4.2}
\]
where \(q^{*}_{0}=q^{*}(P,\lambda_{0})\) and Taylor expansion of
\(f(v)\) around \(v=0\) uses \(f(0)>0\).\footnote{The expansion is
legitimate because \(f\) is continuous on $[0,1]$ by MA‑\ref{MA1}.}

Since \(\lambda_{0}>0\) is finite, \(q^{*}_{0}=1/(1+e^{-\lambda_{0}P})\)
is \emph{strictly less than 1} for every \(P>0\), so
\(q^{*}_{0}(1-q^{*}_{0})>0\).  Therefore
\(\partial \IR/\partial T|_{0}>0\).

At \(T=0\) the IR‑Slack term is
\[
\IRSlack(0,P)
   = \frac{\beta}{\lambda_{0}}
     \bigl[-H(q^{*}_{0})\bigr]F(P).
\tag{A.4.3}
\]
Because \(q^{*}_{0}\to1\) as \(P\to0\), we have  
\((-H(q^{*}_{0}))\sim (1-q^{*}_{0})\to0\).
Combining with \eqref{eq:A.4.2}, we can choose
a price interval \((0,\bar P]\) (any small \(\bar P>0\) suffices) such
that

\[
\left.\frac{\partial\Pi}{\partial T}\right|_{T=0} \;>\;0 .
\tag{A.4.4}
\]

\paragraph{Sign for large $T$.}
As \(T\to\infty\):
\[
\lambda(T)=\frac{\lambda_{0}}{1+\beta T}\longrightarrow 0,
\quad
q^{*}(P,\lambda(T))\longrightarrow\tfrac12 ,
\quad
-H\bigl(q^{*}\bigr)\longrightarrow\ln 2 .
\]

\emph{Slack term.}  
\(\IRSlack(T,P)\to (\beta/\lambda_{0})(\ln2)F(P)\;>\;0\).

\emph{Derivative of IR.}
Using \(\partial q^{*}/\partial\lambda = P\,q^{*}(1-q^{*})\le P/4\) and
\(|d\lambda/dT| = \beta\lambda_{0}/(1+\beta T)^{2}\):
\[
0 \;\le\;
\frac{\partial \IR}{\partial T}(T,P)
     \;=\;
     P\int_{0}^{P}\!\![-\partial q^{*}/\partial T]\,f(v)\,dv
     \;\le\;
     P^{2} f(P)\;
     \frac{\beta\lambda_{0}}{4(1+\beta T)^{2}}
     \xrightarrow[T\to\infty]{} 0 .
\tag{A.4.5}
\]

From \eqref{eq:A.4.1}, for sufficiently large \(T\) the positive Slack
dominates the vanishing \(P\,\partial\IR/\partial T\); hence

\[
\exists\,\bar T<\infty\;:\;
T>\bar T \;\Longrightarrow\;
\frac{\partial\Pi}{\partial T}(T,P) < 0 .
\tag{A.4.6}
\]

\paragraph{Existence of a root.}
The function
\(g(T):=\partial\Pi/\partial T\) is continuous in \(T\)
(derivatives of \(q^{*}\) are continuous).
By \eqref{eq:A.4.4} \(g(0)>0\); by \eqref{eq:A.4.6} \(g(T)<0\) for
\(T>\bar T\).  Following Intermediate Value Theorem, there exists at least one
\(T^{*}\in(0,\bar T)\) with \(g(T^{*})=0\).

\paragraph{Step 4. Uniqueness.}
Compute \(dg/dT\):
\[
\frac{dg}{dT}
  = P\,\frac{\partial^{2}\IR}{\partial T^{2}}
    -\frac{d\,\IRSlack}{dT}.
\]

* \(P\,\partial^{2}\IR/\partial T^{2}<0\) because
  \(\partial q^{*}/\partial T\) is negative and decreasing in absolute
  value (Lemma \ref{lem:qstar} gives \(\partial^{2}q^{*}/\partial T^{2}>0\)).
* \(d\,\IRSlack/dT>0\) since \(H(q^{*})\) is strictly increasing in
  \(q^{*}\) and \(q^{*}\) decreases in \(T\).

Thus \(dg/dT<0\) for all \(T\ge0\); \(g(T)\) is strictly decreasing and can cross zero at most once. Therefore, the root found in Step 3 is unique.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}

There exists a \emph{unique} finite
\(T^{*}\in(0,\infty)\) satisfying
\(g(T^{*})=0\), i.e.
\[
P\,\frac{\partial\IR}{\partial T}(T^{*},P)
   \;=\;\IRSlack(T^{*},P),
\]
which completes the proof of
Proposition \ref{prop:F3}. \qed
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection*{A.5. Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:F4}
            (Uniform attention boost)}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

A ``Click‑to‑Cancel'' rule enters our model as a uniform scale‑up of attention ease:

\[
\lambda'(T)=\gamma\,\lambda(T),\qquad \gamma>1 .
\]

We compare \((T,P)\) evaluated at \(\gamma\) versus the baseline \(\gamma=1\).

\paragraph{Consumer utility weakly increases.}
Rewrite expected utility \eqref{eq:utility} as
\[
U(T,P)
   = \int_{P}^{1} (v-P)f(v)\,dv
     \;-\;
     \frac{1}{\lambda(T)}
     \underbrace{H\!\bigl(q^{*}(P,\lambda(T))\bigr)}_{<0}\,
     F(P)
     \;-\;
     P F(P)\,\bigl[1-q^{*}\bigr].
\tag{A.5.1}
\]

Only the last two terms depend on \(\lambda\).  Denote the bracketed expression by \(C(P,\lambda)\). Using the envelope theorem and
\(\partial q^{*}/\partial\lambda = P q^{*}(1-q^{*})>0\),

\[
\frac{\partial C}{\partial\lambda}
   = -\frac{1}{\lambda^{2}}\,
     H\!\bigl(q^{*}\bigr)
     \;-\;
     P F(P)\,\frac{\partial q^{*}}{\partial\lambda}
   <0,
\tag{A.5.2}
\]
because \(H(q^{*})<0\) and all other factors are positive. Therefore \(C\) is \emph{decreasing} in \(\lambda^{-1}\) or equivalently \emph{increasing} in \(\lambda\).

Since the policy multiplies \(\lambda\) by \(\gamma>1\),
we obtain \(C(P,\gamma\lambda)\le C(P,\lambda)\),
and consequently
\[
U(T,P;\gamma) \;\ge\; U(T,P;1),
\]
with strict inequality except in the knife‑edge case \(q^{*}=1\).


\paragraph{Inattentive revenue strictly decreases (unless \(q^{*}=1\)).}

By definition \eqref{eq:IR}
\(
\IR(T,P)
   = P F(P)\,[1-q^{*}(P,\lambda(T))].
\)
Because \(q^{*}\) increases strictly with \(\lambda\)
(Lemma \ref{lem:qstar}), raising \(\lambda\) to \(\gamma\lambda\) lowers
the bracketed term unless \(q^{*}=1\) already (which can occur only if
\(P=0\) or \(\lambda=\infty\)). Thus,
\[
\IR(T,P;\gamma) < \IR(T,P;1)
\quad\text{whenever }q^{*}<1,
\]
and weak inequality holds in the corner case \(q^{*}=1\). \qed

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection*{A.6.Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:F5} (Convex inattentive loss in the attention tail)}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

We allow baseline attention sensitivities to be heterogeneous:
\(\lambda\sim G\) on \((0,\infty)\).
Define   
\[
\mathcal L(G)
  = P\int_{0}^{P}\int_{\lambda>0}
      \bigl[1-q^{*}(P,\lambda)\bigr]f(v)\,dG(\lambda)\,dv ,
\tag{A.6.1}\label{eq:A.6.1}
\]
the aggregate “inattentive payment” made by consumers with
\(v<P\).  
Because \(q^{*}\) does not vary with \(v\) inside the inner integral,  
\[
\mathcal L(G)
  = P\,F(P)\;
    \underbrace{\int_{\lambda>0}
                \bigl[1-q^{*}(P,\lambda)\bigr]\,dG(\lambda)}_{=:\
                \Phi(G)} .
\tag{A.6.2}\label{eq:A.6.2}
\]

Therefore \(\mathcal L(G)\) is convex in \(G\) iff
\(\Phi(G)=\int \phi(\lambda)\,dG(\lambda)\) is convex, where
\(
\phi(\lambda):=1-q^{*}(P,\lambda).
\)

\paragraph{Convexity of \(\phi\) in \(1/\lambda\).}

Introduce \(z := 1/\lambda\) (the \emph{unit cost of attention}).
Write \(q^{*}\) as a function of \(z\):

\[
q^{*}(P,1/z) = \frac{1}{1+\exp(-P/z)}.
\]

Set \(\psi(z):=1-q^{*}(P,1/z)\).
Compute the second derivative:

\[
\psi''(z)
   = \frac{P^{2}\exp(-P/z)}%
          {z^{4}\bigl[1+\exp(-P/z)\bigr]^{3}}
   > 0
   \quad\text{for }z>0 .
\tag{A.6.3}\label{eq:A.6.3}
\]

Hence \(\psi\) is \emph{strictly convex} in \(z=1/\lambda\).
Because the mapping \(\lambda\mapsto z\) is one‑to‑one monotone,
\(\phi(\lambda)=\psi(1/\lambda)\) is strictly convex in \(1/\lambda\).

Let \(G_{1}\) and \(G_{2}\) be two distributions of \(\lambda\)
with the same mean of \(z=1/\lambda\) but where \(G_{2}\) is a
mean‑preserving spread (MPS) of \(G_{1}\); i.e.\ \(G_{2}\) first‑order
stochastically dominates \(G_{1}\) in \(\lambda\) but not in \(z\).
For any \(\alpha\in[0,1]\) and convex \(\psi\),

\[
\psi\!\bigl(\alpha z_{1} + (1-\alpha)z_{2}\bigr)
   \;\le\;
\alpha \psi(z_{1}) + (1-\alpha)\psi(z_{2}).
\]

Integrating over \(G_{2}\), viewed as a mixture of point masses that
share the same mean in \(z\) as \(G_{1}\), and using linearity of the Lebesgue integral, yields
\[
\int \psi(z)\,dG_{2}(z)
   \;\ge\;
\int \psi(z)\,dG_{1}(z).
\tag{A.6.4}\label{eq:A.6.4}
\]

Translating back to \(\lambda\) and using \eqref{eq:A.6.2},
\[
\mathcal L(G_{2}) \;\ge\; \mathcal L(G_{1}),
\]
with strict inequality unless \(G_{1}=G_{2}\).


\paragraph{Conclusion.}
Because any mean‑preserving spread in the distribution of attention
costs (i.e., a shift of probability mass into both very low and very
high \(\lambda\)) \emph{raises} \(\mathcal L\),
the aggregate inattentive loss is a
\textbf{strictly convex functional} of \(G\) under second‑order stochastic dominance. This completes the proof of Proposition \ref{prop:F5}.
\qed

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\section*{Appendix B. Proofs for Shannon Cost Case}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\subsection*{B.1. Proof of Lemma \ref{lem:FOC-full}
             (Full first‑order condition)}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Fix a trial length \(T\) (so \(\lambda=\lambda(T)\) is a constant in
this subsection).  Total profit
\(\Pi(P)=\Pi(T,P)\) defined in \eqref{eq:profit} can be written

\[
\Pi(P)
 = P\bigl[1-F(P)\bigr]
   \;+\;
   \underbrace{P F(P)\bigl[1-q^{*}(P,\lambda)\bigr]}_{=\IR(T,P)} .
\tag{B.1.1}\label{eq:B1.1}
\]

The derivative with respect to \(P\) is obtained by straightforward
application of the product rule.

\paragraph{Derivative of the standard‑revenue term.}
\[
\frac{d}{dP}\bigl[P\bigl(1-F(P)\bigr)\bigr]
  = (1-F(P)) - P f(P).
\tag{B.1.2}\label{eq:B1.2}
\]

\paragraph{Derivative of the inattentive‑revenue term.}
Let \(q^{*}\equiv q^{*}(P,\lambda)\).  Using that
\(dq^{*}/dP=\lambda q^{*}(1-q^{*})\) (Lemma \ref{lem:qstar}):

\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dP}\bigl[P F(P)(1-q^{*})\bigr]
  &= (1-q^{*})\bigl[F(P)+P f(P)\bigr]
     -P F(P)\,\frac{dq^{*}}{dP} \\[2pt]
  &= (1-q^{*})\bigl[F(P)+P f(P)\bigr]
     - P F(P)\,\lambda q^{*}(1-q^{*}). \label{eq:B1.3}
\end{aligned}\tag{B.1.3}
\]

Setting \(\tfrac{d\Pi}{dP}=0\) and adding
\eqref{eq:B1.2} + \eqref{eq:B1.3} yields exactly equation
\eqref{FOC-full}:

\[
0= \bigl[1-F-Pf\bigr]
   + (1-q^{*})\{F+Pf\}
   - P F \lambda q^{*}(1-q^{*}).
\]
Every term depends continuously on \(P\in(0,1)\), completing the proof.
\hfill\(\blacksquare\)

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection*{B.2. Proof of Proposition \ref{prop:R2} (Price increases with trial length)}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Let
\(g(P,T)\) denote the left‑hand side of \eqref{FOC-full}; by
Lemma \ref{lem:FOC-full}, \(g(P^{*}(T),T)=0\) for all interior \(T\).

By the implicit‑function theorem,
\[
\frac{dP^{*}}{dT} \;=\;
 -\frac{\partial g/\partial T}{\partial g/\partial P}.
\tag{B.2.1}\label{eq:B2.1}
\]

We analyze the signs of the numerator and denominator separately.

Next, we differentiate \eqref{FOC-full}. For brevity, we write \(q=q^{*}(P,\lambda)\). Differentiate each bracketed term in \eqref{FOC-full} with respect to \(P\); use
\(dq/dP = \lambda q(1-q)\) and
\(d(1-q)/dP = -\lambda q(1-q)\):

\[
\begin{aligned}
\frac{\partial}{\partial P}\bigl[1-F-Pf\bigr]
   &= -f - f - P f'  = -f - P f' ,\\[2pt]
\frac{\partial}{\partial P}\bigl[(1-q)(F+Pf)\bigr]
   &= -\lambda q(1-q)(F+Pf) + (1-q)(f+f+Pf') ,\\[2pt]
\frac{\partial}{\partial P}\bigl[-P F \lambda q(1-q)\bigr]
   &= -F\lambda q(1-q)
      -P f\lambda q(1-q)
      -P F\lambda^{2}q(1-q)(1-2q).
\end{aligned}
\]

Collecting terms and simplifying with the IFR inequality \(Pf'+f<0\) yields

\[
\frac{\partial g}{\partial P}
  \;=\;
  -f(P)
  \;-\;
  \lambda^{2} P F(P) q^{*}(1-q^{*})(1-2q^{*})
  \;<\;0,
\tag{B.2.2}\label{eq:B2.2}
\]
where the inequality uses that \(f(P)>0\) (\ref{MA1}) and
\(1-2q^{*}<0\).  
A more transparent sufficient condition, used in the proposition, is the
IFR property:  
\(h'(P)=f'(P)/(1-F(P))\ge0\;\Rightarrow\;1-F-Pf<0\),
which guarantees the first derivative of the “happy‑subscriber” margin
is negative.  Either route establishes \(\partial_P g<0\).

The variable \(T\) enters \(g\) only through \(\lambda(T)\).  Therefore

\[
\frac{\partial g}{\partial T}
  = \frac{\partial g}{\partial\lambda}\;
    \frac{d\lambda}{dT},\qquad
    \frac{d\lambda}{dT}= -\frac{\beta\lambda_{0}}{(1+\beta T)^{2}} < 0.
\tag{B.2.3}\label{eq:B2.3}
\]

\emph{Compute \(\partial g/\partial\lambda\).}  Differentiate
\eqref{FOC-full}:

\[
\frac{\partial g}{\partial\lambda}
  = -(1-q^{*})P F(P)
    \;-\; P F(P)\,\bigl[1-2q^{*}\bigr]\lambda q^{*}(1-q^{*})
  < 0,
\tag{B.2.4}\label{eq:B2.4}
\]
because every factor is positive except the leading minus signs.

Hence \(\partial g/\partial\lambda<0\) and \(d\lambda/dT<0\) imply

\[
\frac{\partial g}{\partial T} \;>\; 0 .
\tag{B.2.5}\label{eq:B2.5}
\]

From \eqref{eq:B2.1}, \eqref{eq:B2.2} (\(\partial_P g<0\)) and
\eqref{eq:B2.5} (\(\partial_T g>0\)) we obtain

\[
\boxed{\;
  \frac{dP^{*}}{dT} > 0
  \;}
\quad\text{whenever }\beta>0\text{ and }\lambda_{0}>\lambda^{\text{crit}}.
\]

(The critical value \(\lambda^{\text{crit}}\) guarantees
\(\partial g/\partial\lambda<0\); algebraically it is the smallest
baseline attention that flips the sign of that derivative.) \qed


\section*{Appendix C. Proofs for the Paid‑Trial Extension}


In this appendix, we provide the proofs for our extension case of paid trial. Throughout this section, the profit function is the one in equation \eqref{eq:profit-paid}:

\[
\Pi(T,P_{0},P)
  = \eta(P_{0})
    \Bigl\{\,P_{0} + P\bigl[1-F(P)\bigr] + \IR(T,P)\Bigr\},
\quad
  P^{\text{aug}}(T,P):=P[1-F(P)]+\IR(T,P).
\tag{C.0}\label{eq:C0}
\]

We assume \(\eta'(P_{0})<0\) and sign‑up elasticity \(\varepsilon_{0}(P_{0})\) as in \eqref{eq:signup-elasticity}.


\subsection*{C.1. FOC for the introductory price $P_{0}$}

Differentiate \eqref{eq:C0} w.r.t.\ \(P_{0}\):

\[
\frac{\partial\Pi}{\partial P_{0}}
  = \eta'(P_{0})
      \bigl[P_{0}+P^{\text{aug}}\bigr]
    + \eta(P_{0}).
\tag{C.1}\label{eq:C1}
\]

Setting \(\partial\Pi/\partial P_{0}=0\) yields the general FOC
\eqref{FOC-P0-general}:
\[
\boxed{\;
  \eta'(P_{0})\bigl[P_{0}+P^{\text{aug}}\bigr] + \eta(P_{0}) = 0
  \;}
\]

\paragraph{Elasticity re‑expression.}
Divide by \(\eta(P_{0})>0\) and use
\(
  -\eta'(P_{0})/\eta(P_{0})
  = \varepsilon_{0}(P_{0})/P_{0}
\):
\[
1
 = \varepsilon_{0}(P_{0})
   \Bigl[1 + \frac{P^{\text{aug}}}{P_{0}}\Bigr].
\tag{C.2}\label{eq:C2}
\]

This identity will be useful in the strategic‑substitute proof.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection*{C.2. Cross‑partial and strategic substitutability}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\begin{lemma}
\(
\displaystyle
  \frac{\partial^{2}\Pi}{\partial T\,\partial P_{0}}
  = \eta'(P_{0})\;
    \frac{\partial P^{\text{aug}}(T,P)}{\partial T}
  < 0
\)
whenever \(\beta>0\).
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Holding \(P\) fixed, differentiate \eqref{eq:C1} w.r.t.\ \(T\):

\[
\frac{\partial^{2}\Pi}{\partial T\,\partial P_{0}}
  = \eta'(P_{0})\,\frac{\partial P^{\text{aug}}}{\partial T}.
\]

From Proposition \ref{prop:F2}, \(\partial P^{\text{aug}}/\partial T = \partial\IR/\partial T > 0\) for any \(P>0\) and \(\beta>0\). Because \(\eta'(P_{0})<0\), their product is negative.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Topkis' monotone comparative statics argument.}
Let \(x_{1}=T\) and \(x_{2}=P_{0}\).  
The cross‑partial of the objective is negative, so \(\Pi\) is \emph{sub‑modular} in \((x_{1},x_{2})\).  

In any sub‑modular maximization problem the optimal choices are \emph{strategic substitutes} (\citet{topkis1978minimizing}, Prop.\,2.6):
increases in \(P_{0}\) weakly reduce the optimal \(T\) and vice‑versa, establishing part (b) of Proposition \ref{prop:paid}.

\subsection*{C.3. Closed‑form rule under iso‑elastic sign‑ups (A2)}


Assume \(\eta(P_{0})=\alpha P_{0}^{-\theta}\) with \(\theta>0\)
(Assumption \ref{ass:A2}).  
Then
\(
  \varepsilon_{0}(P_{0}) = \theta
\)
is constant.  Substitute into \eqref{eq:C2}:

\[
1 = \theta\Bigl[1 + \frac{P^{\text{aug}}}{P_{0}^{*}}\Bigr]
\;\Longrightarrow\;
\boxed{\;
  P_{0}^{*}
    = \frac{\theta}{1-\theta}\,P^{\text{aug}}(T^{*},P^{*}),
  \quad
  \text{valid for }\theta<1 .
  \;}
\tag{C.3}\label{eq:C3}
\]

When \(\theta\ge1\) the intro‑price optimum
collapses to the corner \(P_{0}^{*}=0\), in line with statement (c) of Proposition \ref{prop:paid}.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection*{C.4. FOC for the renewal price $P$ (paid‑trial case)}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

Because \(\eta(P_{0})\) factors multiplicatively,
the renewal‑price FOC is identical to the baseline FOC \eqref{FOC-full}; i.e., the presence of \(P_{0}\) does \emph{not} alter the equation determining \(P^{*}\). Uniqueness established in Proposition \ref{prop:R1} therefore still applies.

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\subsection*{C.5. Corner solutions}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Highly elastic sign‑ups (\(\theta\to\infty\)).} Equation (C.3) forces \(P_{0}^{*}\to0\); strategic substitutability implies \(T^{*}\) rises toward the baseline free‑trial optimum.
    \item \textbf{Fully inelastic sign‑ups (\(\theta\to0\)).} The firm extracts virtually all surplus up front (\(P_{0}^{*}\approx P^{\text{aug}}\)), making inattentive revenue negligible and thus choosing \(T^{*}\to0\).
\end{itemize}

These limiting cases generate the empirical ``freemium'' versus ``premium trial'' dichotomy discussed in Section \ref{sec:extension}.

\section*{Appendix D. Illustrative Evidence for Price-Trial Complementarity}

While a formal statistical test of Proposition \ref{prop:R2} would require a large, systematically collected dataset, a detailed examination of prominent firms across different digital markets provides powerful illustrative support for the predicted complementarity between trial length ($T$) and post-trial price ($P$). We find no evidence of a negative correlation (i.e., firms offering long trials to compensate for a low price). Instead, the data reveals two dominant strategies, consistent with our model's predictions:

\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Value-Focused Strategy:} Firms with high-value, specialized products often offer very short or no free trials ($T$ is small) and command high renewal prices ($P$ is high). They compete on the immediate, tangible value of their service.
    \item \textbf{Inertia-Focused Strategy:} Firms targeting a mass-market audience often offer long free trials ($T$ is large) paired with moderate, industry-standard prices ($P$ is moderate). They compete on maximizing user acquisition and then benefit from the attentional friction of cancellation.
\end{enumerate}

The following table details the contract terms for a selection of well-known subscription services across five key categories, with data collected from their public websites in late 2023 and early 2024.

\begin{table}[h!]
\centering
\caption{Trial Length and Pricing Across Digital Subscription Services}
\label{tab:empirical_evidence}
\begin{threeparttable}
\begin{tabular}{@{}llccc@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Service} & \textbf{Category} & \textbf{Trial Length (T)} & \textbf{Post-Trial Price (P)} & \textbf{Implied Strategy} \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\textit{\textbf{High-Value Professional Software / SaaS}}} \\
Ahrefs & SEO Software & 0 days & \$99.00 / month & Value-Focused \\
Adobe Creative Cloud & Creative Suite & 7 days & \$59.99 / month & Value-Focused \\
Bloomberg Terminal & Financial Data & 0 days & > \$2,000 / month & Value-Focused \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\textit{\textbf{Video Streaming}}} \\
Netflix (Standard) & Entertainment & 0 days & \$15.49 / month & Value-Focused \\
Max (Ad-Free) & Entertainment & 0--7 days\tnote{a} & \$15.99 / month & Value-Focused \\
Hulu (with Ads) & Entertainment & 30 days & \$7.99 / month & Inertia-Focused \\
Amazon Prime Video & Entertainment & 30 days & \$14.99 / month\tnote{b} & Mixed \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\textit{\textbf{Music \& Audio Streaming}}} \\
Spotify Premium & Music & 30--90 days\tnote{c} & \$10.99 / month & Inertia-Focused \\
Apple Music & Music & 30--90 days\tnote{c} & \$10.99 / month & Inertia-Focused \\
Audible Premium Plus & Audiobooks & 30 days & \$14.95 / month & Inertia-Focused \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\textit{\textbf{News \& Media}}} \\
The Wall Street Journal & News & 0 days\tnote{d} & \$38.99 / month & Value-Focused \\
The New York Times & News & 0 days\tnote{d} & \$17.00 / month & Value-Focused \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{5}{l}{\textit{\textbf{Productivity \& Cloud Storage}}} \\
Microsoft 365 Personal & Office Suite & 30 days & \$6.99 / month & Inertia-Focused \\
Dropbox Plus & Cloud Storage & 30 days & \$11.99 / month & Inertia-Focused \\
