\section{Conclusions}
From \autoref{fig:rmse_n}  the advantage of \textit{low discrepancy } methods over standard Monte Carlo is clear. In fact, the rate of convergence of both Sobol' and Faure is, as expected, approximately $O(n^{-1})$ with Sobol' sequence QMC outperforming the other techniques. 
With respect to the curse of dimensionality (\autoref{fig:rmse_d}) the results align with theoretical expectations, demonstrating that Monte Carlo methods are unaffected by increased dimension. In contrast, the \textsf{RMSE} associated with Faure Quasi-Monte Carlo soar for dimensions higher than seventy. This is why the Sobol' sequence is more advisable, not heavily suffering from the increase in the dimension of the initial problem. In fact, the graph shows that the \textsf{RMSE} associated with Sobol' follows a relatively constant trend.

When simulating the value of an Asian option, as it is a path-dependent option, there is a high likelihood that the sample intended to surrogate the normally distributed points -employing the aforementioned sequences- tends to lose its low-discrepancy properties.
To address this issue we implemented a \textit{Brownian Bridge}, which appears to preserve these features, as shown in \autoref{fig:rmse_A}. Moreover, by employing the randomization already implemented via the \texttt{scrambling} parameter in the corresponding function of the QMCPy package, Sobol' RQMC exhibits a convergence rate of $O(n^{-0.88})$.

It again outperforms the standard Monte Carlo; however, its precision is now slightly less pronounced compared to the Halton sequence, which was omitted from the geometric basket option example due to its uniformity degradation, further exacerbated by the lack of scrambling.

Further improvements could involve the implementation of alternative \textit{low-discrepancy} sequences, such as the Lattice Rules, as well as the incorporation of dimension reduction tools, such as the \textit{Principal Component Analysis}, as an alternative to the \textit{Brownian Bridge}.   
