\section{Introduction}
It is well-known that solitons, breathers, and rogue waves represent fundamental nonlinear wave phenomena, each exhibiting distinct dynamic behaviors across diverse physical domains. They have attracted extensive attention due to their emergence in numerous nonlinear models involving various fields such as plasmas physics, nonlinear optics, hydrodynamics and Bose-Einstein condensations\cite{0,1,2,3,4,5}. 

Breathers, which describe the growth of the perturbations in finite continuous backgrounds, contain two different types, namely Kuznetsov–Ma breathers and Akhmediev breathers \cite{km,ab}. Recently, the state transition mechanisms between the breathers (or rogue waves) and solitons have been explored in various nonlinear models.  Abundant converted waves including multi-peak solitons, periodic waves and anti-dark solitons have been presented starting from the breathers or rogue waves by an appropriate transitions condition. The first case involves the nonlinear models featuring high-order terms that contribute to the state transitions. The mixed nonlinear Schr\"{o}dinger equation (NLS)\cite{mix}, fifth-order NLS\cite{fif}, higher-order generalized NLS\cite{gnls} and six-order NLS equation\cite{six} have been successively discovered to possess various converted waves and nonlinear wave interactions. The Hirota equation\cite{hi1,hi2,hi3}, as an extended NLS-type equation with high-order dispersion terms, also exhibits the breather-to-soliton transition phenomenon. Meanwhile, Sasa–Satsuma equation\cite{ss} and the high-order Chen–Lee–Liu equation have also been considered\cite{cll}. Another case occurs in coupled equations describing multi-field interactions which facilitate the state transitions. The transitions of the breather solution have been progressively discussed in the coupled Hirota equation\cite{chi1,chi2}, multi-component AB system\cite{ab1,ab2,ab3}, coupled Fokas–Lenells system\cite{fl} and coupled extended modified Korteweg-de Vries system\cite{mkdv}.

In this paper, we focus on the the higher-order modified Gerdjikov–Ivanov equation (HMGI),
\begin{align}\label{eq}
i q_t &+ 2i q_{xxx}+ \left[(16\delta +4)|q|^2 - 4\delta\right] q_{xx}+ (4\delta + 1) q^2 q_{xx}^* + (12\delta + 3) q^{*2} q_x^2
\notag\\&+ \left[(20\delta + 2) q q_x^* - \left(48\delta^2 + 30\delta + \frac{15}{4}\right) i |q|^4\right.\left. + \left(24\delta^2 + 6\delta\right) i |q|^2 - 2i \delta^2\right] q_x \notag\\
&- \left[(24\delta^2 + 6\delta)|q|^2 q^2 - 8\delta^2 q^2\right] i q_x^* -\left(16\delta^3 + 24\delta^2 + \frac{15}{2}\delta + \frac{5}{8}\right)|q|^6 q \notag\\
&+ \left(16\delta^3 + 12\delta^2 + \frac{3}{2}\delta\right)|q|^4 q - \left(4\delta^3 + \delta^2\right)|q|^2 q + 2\delta^3 q = 0.
\end{align}
where $\delta$ is an arbitrary real number which imposes a significant influence on the self-steepening effect in transmission which is the self-frequency shift of Raman scattering. Guan, Yang and Meng et.al. obtained the high-order rogue wave solutions and their dynamic properties for the HMGI equation by generalized
Darboux transformation method\cite{guan}.  The soliton, breather and new rogue wave solutions of the HMGI euation are reached by Zhao, Wang and Yang through Darboux transformation method\cite{zhao}. The multi-positon solutions, interaction solutions and the modulation instability for the HMGI equation are exihited by Zhao, Wang and Zhang\cite{zhao2}.   

To our knowledge, the state transitions of breather-to-soliton for the HMGI equation have not been explored yet. This paper is organized as follows: in section \ref{1s}, we provide the state transition conditions by analyzing the explicit expression of the breather solution and obtain different nonlinear converted waves including W-shaped soliton, M-shape soliton, anti-dark soliton, multi-peak soliton and periodic waves. In section \ref{2s}, the interactions between the above various nonlinear converted waves are investigated. In section \ref{3s}, double-pole breather-to-soliton transition solutions are derived from the double-pole breather solutions of the HMGI equation. In addition, the asymptotic analysis is given for the double-pole anti-dark soliton solution to obtain the explicit expression of the asymptotic solitons. 


\section{Breather-to-soliton transitions}\label{1s}
In this section, we discuss the existence conditions for several types of nonlinear converted waves by analyzing the explicit expression of the first-order solution on the plane wave background. The Lax pair for Eq.\eqref{eq} have given in Ref.\cite{zhao2} as follows,
\begin{align}\label{lax}
    \Phi_x=P\Phi,~\Phi_t=Q\Phi,
\end{align}
where $\Phi=(\psi,\phi)^{\mathrm{T}}$ is the complex vector function and 
\[
P=\begin{pmatrix}
    \frac{1}{2}\mathrm{i}\lambda^2+\mathrm{i}\delta|q|^2& \frac{1}{2}\lambda q \\
    -\frac{1}{2}\lambda q^* & -\frac{1}{2}\mathrm{i}\lambda^2-\mathrm{i}\delta|q|^2
\end{pmatrix},~~
Q=\begin{pmatrix}
    Q_1 & Q_2 \\
    Q_3 & -Q_1
\end{pmatrix},
\]
with 
\begin{align*}
Q_1 &= \frac{1}{2} i q^* q_{xx} + \frac{1}{2} i q q_{xx}^* + \frac{1}{16}\left[ -8 i q_x^* + (488 + 12) |q|^2 q - \left( 8 \lambda^2 + 16 \delta \right) q^* \right] q_x \\
&- 3 \left[ q^* \left( \delta + \frac{1}{4} \right) q - \frac{\lambda^2}{6} - \frac{\delta}{3} \right] q q_x^* - 6 i |q|^6\left( \delta^2 + \frac{\delta}{2} + \frac{5}{96} \right) \\
&+ 4 i \left( \delta + \frac{3}{16} \right) \left( \frac{\lambda^2}{2} + \delta \right) |q|^4- \frac{i \left( \lambda^2 + \delta \right)^2 |q|^2}{2} + i \lambda^6 + 2 i \lambda^4 \delta + i \lambda^2 \delta^2 + i \delta^3,\\
Q_2 &= 2\lambda \bigg[ -\frac{q_{xx}}{2} + \bigg[ 3i \bigg( \delta + \frac{1}{4} \bigg) |q|^2 - \frac{1}{2}i\lambda^2 - i\delta \bigg] q_x+ \bigg[ i\delta q q_x^* +\frac{(\lambda^2 + \delta)^2}{2} \\
&\quad - 2 \bigg( \delta + \frac{1}{4} \bigg) \bigg( \frac{\lambda^2}{2} + \delta \bigg) |q|^2 +2 \bigg( \delta^2 + \frac{3}{4}\delta + \frac{3}{32} \bigg) |q|^4\bigg]q \bigg],\\
Q_3 &= 2\lambda \bigg[ \frac{q_{xx}^*}{2} + \bigg[ 3i \bigg( \delta + \frac{1}{4} \bigg) |q|^2 - \frac{1}{2}i\lambda^2 - i\delta \bigg] q_x^*+ \bigg[ i\delta^* q q_x- \frac{(\lambda^2 + \delta)^2}{2}  \\
&\quad + 2 \bigg( \delta + \frac{1}{4} \bigg) \bigg( \frac{\lambda^2}{2} + \delta \bigg) |q|^2 - 2 \bigg( \delta^2 + \frac{3}{4}\delta + \frac{3}{32} \bigg) |q|^4 \bigg] q^* \bigg],
\end{align*}
where $\lambda$ is the complex spectral parameter and the zero curvature equation $P_t-Q_x+PQ-QP=0$ generate original Eq.\eqref{eq}.

We introduce the zeed solution $q[0]=ce^{\mathrm{i}(ax+bt)}$ to Eq.\eqref{lax} with
\begin{align*}
b=&-16\delta^3 c^6 - 24\delta^2 c^6 - \frac{15}{2}c^6\delta - \frac{5}{8}c^6 + 16\delta^3 c^4 + 24\delta^2 c^4 a + 12\delta^2 c^4 + 24c^4\delta a \\&+ \frac{3}{2}c^4\delta + \frac{15}{4}c^4 a - 4\delta^3 c^2 - 16\delta^2 c^2 a - \delta^2 c^2 - 12c^2 a^2 \delta - 6c^2\delta a - 6c^2 a^2 \\
&+ 2a^3 + 4a^2\delta + 2\delta^2 a + 2\delta^3.
\end{align*}
According to the Darboux transformation given in \cite{zhao}, we assume $\Phi_j=(\psi_j,\phi_j)^\mathrm{T},~j=1,\cdots,n$ are the eigenfunctions under $\lambda=\lambda_j,~j=1,\cdots,n$, then we can obtain the $n$-order solution as follows,
\begin{align}\label{sol}
q[n]=q[0]-\dfrac{2\mathrm{i}|\Omega_1|}{|\Omega_2|},
\end{align}
with 
\[
\Omega_2=\begin{pmatrix}
   \lambda_1^{2n-2}\psi_1 & \lambda_1^{2n-4}\psi_1 & \cdots & \psi_1 &\lambda_1^{2n-1}\phi_1 & \lambda_1^{2n-3}\phi_1 & \cdots & \lambda_1\phi_1\\
    \lambda_2^{2n-2}\psi_2 & \lambda_2^{2n-4}\psi_2 & \cdots & \psi_2 &\lambda_2^{2n-1}\phi_2 & \lambda_2^{2n-3}\phi_2 & \cdots & \lambda_2\phi_2 \\
    \vdots & \vdots & \cdots &\vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \cdots & \vdots\\
    \lambda_n^{2n-2}\psi_n & \lambda_n^{2n-4}\psi_n & \cdots & \psi_n &\lambda_n^{2n-1}\phi_n & \lambda_n^{2n-3}\phi_n & \cdots & \lambda_n\phi_n \\
    \lambda_1^{*2n-2}\phi_1^* & \lambda_1^{*2n-4}\phi_1^*& \cdots & \phi_1^* &-\lambda_1^{*2n-1}\psi_1^* & -\lambda_1^{*2n-3}\psi_1^*&\cdots & -\lambda_1^*\psi_1^* \\
     \vdots & \vdots & \cdots &\vdots & \vdots & \vdots & \cdots & \vdots\\
    \lambda_n^{*2n-2}\phi_n^* & \lambda_n^{*2n-4}\phi_n^*& \cdots & \phi_n^* &-\lambda_n^{*2n-1}\psi_n^* & -\lambda_n^{*2n-3}\psi_n^*&\cdots & -\lambda_n^*\psi_n^*
\end{pmatrix},
\]
and $\Omega_1$ is given by replacing (n+1)-th column in $\Omega_2$ with 
$$(-\lambda_1^{2n}\psi_1,-\lambda_2^{2n}\psi_2,\cdots,-\lambda_n^{2n}\psi_n,-\lambda_1^{*2n}\phi_1^*,\cdots,-\lambda^{*2n}\phi_n^*)^{\mathrm{T}},$$
where $*$ denotes the complex conjugate and 
\begin{align*}
&\psi_j=[c_1(c\lambda_j\mathrm{e}^{B_j}+(H_j-D_j)\mathrm{e}^{-B_j})+c_2(-c\lambda_j\mathrm{e}^{-B_j}+(H_j+D_j)\mathrm{e}^{B_j})]\mathrm{e}^{A},\\
&\phi_j=[c_1((H_j-D_j)\mathrm{e}^{B_j}+c\lambda_j\mathrm{e}^{-B_j})+c_2((H_j+D_j)\mathrm{e}^{-B_j}-c\lambda_j\mathrm{e}^{B_j})]\mathrm{e}^{-A},\\
&A=\mathrm{i}(ax+bt),~~D_j=2\mathrm{i}\delta c^2+\lambda_j^2\mathrm{i} -a\mathrm{i},\\
&H_j=\sqrt{-4c^4\delta^2 - 4c^2\delta\lambda_j^2 + 4ac^2\delta - c^2\lambda_j^2 - \lambda_j^4 + 2a\lambda_j^2 - a^2},\\
&B=\frac{H_jx}{2}+H_jt\Bigg\{\left(4\delta^2 + 3\delta + \frac{3}{8}\right)c^4+ a^2 + \left(\lambda_j^2 + 2\delta\right)a + \left(\lambda_j^2 + \delta\right)^2\\
&\quad + \left[\left(-4\delta - \frac{3}{2}\right)a - 4\left(\frac{\lambda_j^2}{2} + \delta\right)\left(\delta + \frac{1}{4}\right)\right]c^2\Bigg\},
\end{align*}
where $c_1,c_2$ are the real coefficients. 

Subsequently, we firstly consider the first-order solution, namely $n=1$. Assume $H_1=H_{1,R}+\mathrm{i}H_{1,I}$ and $\lambda_1=\alpha_1+\mathrm{i}\beta_1$, we can find that the first-order solution comprises the hyperbolic function parts $\cosh(V_{1,H}t+H_{1,R}x),~\sinh(V_{1,H}t+H_{1,R}x)$ and the trigonometric function parts $\cos(V_{1,T}t+H_{1,I}x),~\sin(V_{1,T}t+H_{1,I}x)$, where
\begin{align*}
V_{1,H}&=W_1H_{1,R}-W_2H_{1,I},~V_T=W_1H_{1,I}+W_2H_{1,R},\\
W_1&=\frac{1}{4}\Bigg[32\left(c^2 - \frac{1}{2}\right)^2\delta^2 + \Bigl(24c^4  -16\alpha_1^2c^2 + 16\beta_1^2c^2 - 32ac^2 - 8c^2 - 16\beta_1^2 \\&+ 16\alpha_1^2+ 16a\Bigr)\delta+ 3c^4+ \bigl(-4\alpha_1^2 + 4\beta_1^2 - 12a\bigr)c^2+ 8\alpha_1^4 + \bigl(-48\beta_1^2 + 8a\bigr)\alpha_1^2 \\&+ 8\beta_1^4 - 8a\beta_1^2 + 8a^2\Bigg],\\
W_2&=4\alpha_1\beta_1\Big(-2c^2\delta + 2\delta - \frac{c^2}{2} - 2\beta_1^2 + 2\alpha_1^2 + a\Big).
\end{align*}
where the symbols $R$ and $I$ denote the real part and the imaginary part.

The above two function parts respectively describe the localization and periodicity of the wave's transverse distribution. In other words, the first-order solution is composed of the soliton parts and the periodic parts. It's obvious that the velocities of the two parts are $\frac{V_{1,H}}{H_{1,R}}$ and $\frac{V_{1,T}}{H_{1,I}}$, respectively. We can derive the first-order breather solution from Eq.\eqref{sol} when $\frac{V_{1,H}}{H_{1,R}}\neq\frac{V_{1,T}}{H_{1,I}}$. When $\frac{V_{1,H}}{H_{1,R}}=\frac{V_{1,T}}{H_{1,I}}$, the first-order solution will be converted into a continuous soliton. The breather-to-soliton state transition will be discussed.
\subsection{$\frac{V_{1,H}}{H_{1,R}}\neq\frac{V_{1,T}}{H_{1,I}}$}
In this case, the first-order solution become the localized wave solution with breathing behavior. Under the condition $\alpha_1^2-\beta_1^2+\frac{(4\delta + 1)c^2 - 2a}{2}=0$, when $4\beta_1^2\alpha_1^2 + \frac{c^4}{4} + c^2\left(2c^2\delta - a\right)>0$, the general breather solution is obtained as shown in Fig.\eqref{fg11}. The Akhmediev breather solution which is localized in time and periodic in space is presented in Fig.\eqref{fg12} when the condition $4\beta_1^2\alpha_1^2 + \frac{c^4}{4}+c^2\left(2c^2\delta - a\right)<0$ holds.
