\subsection{$\frac{V_{1,H}}{H_{1,R}}=\frac{V_{1,T}}{H_{1,I}}$}
In this case, the breather solution will be converted to the continuous soliton solution. We will discuss different types of the nonlinear converted waves.

The condition $\frac{V_{1,H}}{H_{1,R}}=\frac{V_{1,T}}{H_{1,I}}$ can be expanded in the following form,
\begin{align}\label{tj}
 -2c^2\delta + 2\delta - \frac{1}{2}c^2 - 2\beta_1^2 + 2\alpha_1^2 + a=0.
\end{align}

Given that the condition \eqref{tj} is satisfied, we can obtain the multi-peak soliton as is shown in Fig.\ref{fg2} by choosing appropriate parameters in Eq.\eqref{sol} when $\alpha_1^2-\beta_1^2+\frac{(4\delta + 1)c^2 - 2a}{2}\neq0$. We can find that Fig.\eqref{fg21}-Fig.\eqref{fg24} are the oscillation W-shaped soliton, while Fig.\eqref{fg25}-Fig.\eqref{fg28} are the oscillation M-shaped soliton. The transformation between the two kinds of solitons can be finished by changing the sign of the imaginary part $\beta_1$ of the eigenvalue. Futhermore, it's observed that when the real part $\alpha_1>0$ of the eigenvalue decreases, the number of wave peaks for the oscillation W-shaped and M-shaped soliton will increases. 

