\section{Nonlinear wave interactions}\label{2s}
In this section, the breather-to-soliton state transition of the second-order solution is discussed. In other words, we will explore the nonlinear converted wave interactions. We choose $n=2$, $H_2=H_{2,R}+\mathrm{i}H_{2,I}$ and $\lambda_2=\alpha_2+\mathrm{i}\beta_2$, then the second-order solution contains an additional hyperbolic function part $\cosh(V_{2,H}t+H_{2,R}x),~\sinh(V_{2,H}t+H_{2,R}x)$ and a trigonometric function part $\cos(V_{2,T}t+H_{2,I}x),~\sin(V_{2,T}t+H_{2,I}x)$ compared to the first-order solution. Hence, we need to consider two state transition conditions, namely $\frac{V_{j,H}}{H_{j,R}}=\frac{V_{j,T}}{H_{j,I}},j=1,2$ or $\frac{V_{j,H}}{H_{j,R}}\neq\frac{V_{j,T}}{H_{j,I}},j=1,2$. 

Firstly, under the conditions $\frac{V_{1,H}}{H_{1,R}}=\frac{V_{1,T}}{H_{1,I}}$ and $\frac{V_{2,H}}{H_{2,R}}\neq\frac{V_{2,T}}{H_{2,I}}$, the interactions between the breather and other nonlinear converted waves can be obtained. Fig.\ref{fg5} exhibits the interaction between the breather and the anti-dark soliton when additional conditions $\alpha_1^2-\beta_1^2+\frac{(4\delta + 1)c^2 - 2a}{2}=0$ and $4\beta_1^2\alpha_1^2 + \frac{c^4}{4}+c^2\left(2c^2\delta - a\right)>0$ are satisfied. When $4\beta_1^2\alpha_1^2 + \frac{c^4}{4}+c^2\left(2c^2\delta - a\right)<0$ holds, we can obtain the interaction between the breather and the periodic wave as is shown in Fig.\ref{fg6}. Moreover, when the condition $\alpha_1^2-\beta_1^2+\frac{(4\delta + 1)c^2 - 2a}{2}=0$ is satisfied, the second-order solution can be degenerated into the interaction between the breather and multi-peak soliton and we can see Fig.\ref{fg7}.
