\section{Simulations}
\label{sec:sim}

%\subsection{Comparisons}
In the previous sections, we have constructed $\gamma$- and $(\gamma,\gamma)$-MERGs, which provably achieve maximum $r$- and $(r,s)$-robustness with the fewest edges possible. In this section, we demonstrate the robustness of these graphs through two different sets of 
simulations (first in Sec.~\ref{subsub:first_sim} and second in Sec.~\ref{subsub:second_sim}). We run each simulation from time step $t=0$ to $t=30$. In each simulation, the normals agents in a network run the W-MSR algorithm to perform consensus in the presence of $F$-total misbehaving agents, which transmit adversarial values to disrupt consensus. The simulation results are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:consensus} and Figure~\ref{fig:consensus2}, where the states of normal agents are plotted in solid colored lines, while states of misbehaving agents are plotted in red dotted lines. 


\subsection{Consensus Performance\label{subsub:first_sim}}
In the first set of simulations, we demonstrate that in the presence of $F$ malicious agents, indexed by $i\in\{0,\dots, F-1\}$, normal agents $i\in \{F,\dots, n-1\}$ in $\gamma$- and $(\gamma,\gamma)$-MERGs are guaranteed to achieve resilient consensus. Each normal agent $i$'s initial state $x_i[0] \in \mathbb R$ is randomly generated on the interval $[-1000,1000]$. At time step $t\geq 0$, a malicious agent $i$ transmits an adversarial value: $1080\cos(t/5)$ if $i$ is even, and $1080\sin(t/5)$ if $i$ is odd. 

We consider networks with $n=49$ and $n=50$ agents in all simulations. Maximum $r$-robustness any network with $49$ and $50$ agents can achieve is $r=\lceil \frac n 2 \rceil = 25$-robustness. Thus, using Theorem~\ref{thm:max_r}, we construct $25$-MERGs with $49$ and $50$ nodes. Note these graphs achieve $25$-robustness with the fewest edges possible for a given number of nodes, and it can tolerate at most $F=12$ malicious agents. Thus, we run consensus simulations on these network graphs with $12$-total malicious agents, and the results are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:consensus} (a) and (b). Similarly, the maximum $(r,s)$-robustness any network with $49$ and $50$ agents can achieve is $(25,25)$-robustness. Thus, we use Theorem~\ref{thm:max_rs} to construct $(25,25)$-MERGs with $49$ and $50$ agents. These graphs can tolerate up to $F= 24$ malicious agents. We run consensus simulations on these network graphs with $24$-total malicious agents, and the results are shown in Figure~\ref{fig:consensus} (c) and (d). As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:consensus}, both $\gamma$- and $(\gamma,\gamma)$-MERGs allow all normal agents to successfully achieve consensus within the convex hull of their initial values through the W-MSR, despite the presence of $F$ malicious agents. 



\subsection{Consensus with an Edge Removal\label{subsub:second_sim}}

These $\gamma$- and $(\gamma,\gamma)$ graphs achieve their respective maximum robustness levels using the fewest edges possible for a given number of nodes. In other words, removing any edge reduces their robustness levels at least by one. To validate this minimalistic property, we compare the consensus performance of the original $\gamma$- and $(\gamma,\gamma)$-MERGs with that of modified versions in which a single edge has been removed. For each graph, comparison is conducted using identical initial states for the normal agents and in the presence of $F$ Byzantine agents.

\subsubsection{Simulations with $\gamma$-MERGs} We construct $5$-MERGs with $n=9$ and $n=10$ nodes as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:example1}. Note these graphs achieve $5$-robustness with the fewest edges possible for a given number of nodes, and they can tolerate at most $F=2$ byzantine agents. We set agents $i \in \{0,1\}$ as Byzantine, and agents $i \in\{2,\dots, n-1\}$ as normal. Each normal agent $i$ is initialized with a state $x_i[0] \in \mathbb{R}$ sampled uniformly at random from the interval $[15, 100]$ for $i \in \{2, \dots, 5\}$, from $[0, 7]$ for $i \in\{6 ,\dots,n-2\}$, and from $[8,14]$ for $i=n-1$. For all $t\geq 0$, all Byzantine agents adversarially share a value of $100$ with agents $i=0,\dots, \lceil \frac n 2\rceil$ and of $0$ with other remaining agents.

Figure~\ref{fig:consensus2}~(a)-(b) and (e)-(f) illustrate the consensus results with and without edge removal. We have removed the edge $(3,8)$ for $n=9$ and $(4,9)$ for $n=10$ from the $5$-MERGs shown in Figure~\ref{fig:example1}. In the $5$-MERGs, normal agents successfully reach resilient consensus (shown in Figure~\ref{fig:consensus2}~(e) and~(f)). However, removing the edge $(3,8)$ from each $5$-MERG reduces its robustness to $r = 4$, which is insufficient to guarantee resilient consensus in the presence of $F = 2$ Byzantine agents. As a result, normal agents in the modified graph fail to reach resilient consensus (as shown by the red arrows in Figure~\ref{fig:consensus2}~(a) and~(b)).


\subsubsection{Simulations with $(\gamma,\gamma)$-MERGs}
We use Theorem~\ref{thm:max_rs} to construct $(5,5)$-MERGs with $n=9$ and $n=10$ agents, as shown in Figure~\ref{fig:example2}. These graphs can tolerate up to $F=4$ Byzantine agents. We set agents $i\in \{0,\dots, 3\}$ as Byzantine, and agents $i\in\{4,\dots, n-1\}$ as normal. Each normal agent $i$ is initialized with a state $x_i[0] \in \mathbb{R}$ sampled uniformly at random from the interval $[50, 100]$ for $i \in \{4, \dots, n-2\}$ and from $[1, 50]$ for $i = n-1$. Byzantine agents share adversarial values: agent $3$ transmits $100$ to its neighbors at all times, while the remaining Byzantine agents transmit $0$ to their neighbors $\forall t \geq 0$.


Figure~\ref{fig:consensus2}~(c)-(d) and~(g)-(h) illustrate the consensus results with and without edge removal. We have removed the edge $(7,8)$ for $n=9$ and $(0,2)$ for $n=10$ from the $(5,5)$-MERGs visualized in Figure~\ref{fig:example2}. In these $(5,5)$-MERGs, normal agents successfully reach resilient consensus (shown in Figure~\ref{fig:consensus2}~(g) and~(h)). However, removing the edge from each $(5,5)$-MERG reduces its robustness to at most $(5,4)$-robustness, which is insufficient to guarantee resilient consensus in the presence of $F = 4$ Byzantine agents. As a result, normal agents fail to reach resilient consensus in the modified graph (as shown by the red arrows in Figure~\ref{fig:consensus2}~(c) and~(d)).


\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conc}

This paper investigates and presents the necessary conditions and topological structures of $r$- and $(r,s)$-robust graphs. These results are derived purely from the definitions of robustness, providing a general understanding of what these robustness levels require. We establish the tight lower bounds on the edge counts for graphs with any number of nodes to achieve maximum robustness. Then, we develop further from these results by constructing two classes of graphs of any number of nodes that achieve maximum $r$- and $(r,s)$-robustness with minimal sets of edges. For future work, we aim to extend our results to arbitrary robustness levels.

