\section{Future Directions and Challenges}

\subsection{Limitations of Current Studies}

\subsubsection{Technological Limitations}
% Despite enabling significant advancements in FBS research, current technologies pose several limitations. Tools such as ultrasound, CTG, and FMCG suffer from restricted imaging fields, low signal amplitudes, susceptibility to motion artifacts, and a reliance on mid-to-late gestation due to practical constraints. These limitations hinder early developmental studies and reduce the precision of fetal autonomic and respiratory system assessments. Although fetal MRI offers more direct insights into CNS activity, its high cost, limited accessibility, and need for specialized expertise restrict widespread use. Overall, current technological methods lack the resolution and scope necessary to fully characterize fetal neurodevelopment and sleep-related transitions, particularly during the early stages of gestation, which remain poorly understood due to these technological constraints.
While current tools have advanced FBS research, technologies like ultrasound, CTG, and FMCG face limitations such as low signal quality, motion artifacts, and limited applicability in early gestation. Fetal MRI provides better CNS insights but is costly and inaccessible. Overall, existing methods lack the resolution and scope to fully capture early fetal neurodevelopment and sleep transitions.

\subsubsection{Analytical Challenges}
% FBS detection methods often rely on manual visual inspection or algorithms trained on prototypical segments, leading to a potential oversight of transitional or ambiguous states. These approaches typically discard data with unclear patterns or overlapping state features, which may hold critical insights into the development of cognitive and physiological processes. Furthermore, inconsistency in the terminology and definitions used to describe fetal sleep states across studies hampers reproducibility and cross-study comparisons. Analytical methods also tend to underrepresent complex dynamics, such as diurnal and ultradian rhythms, or interactions with maternal physiology.
FBS detection often depends on visual inspection or algorithms trained on prototypical segments, overlooking transitional or ambiguous states that may offer important developmental insights. Inconsistent terminology across studies further hinders reproducibility and comparison. Most methods also fail to capture complex dynamics like diurnal rhythms or maternal-fetal interactions.

\subsubsection{Sample Size and Interindividual Variability}
% Small sample sizes remain a key limitation across many fetal sleep studies, reducing statistical power and generalizability. This is especially problematic when studying associations between FHR dynamics and biochemical markers. Inter- and intra-fetal variability, influenced by factors such as gestational age, maternal health, and environmental rhythms, complicates the development of standardized FBS classification schemes. Grouping data from broad gestational windows (e.g., mid and late gestation) may obscure important developmental shifts and delay the establishment of reliable norms.
