\begin{abstract}
%% Text of abstract
Conditional Value-at-Risk (CVaR) is a risk measure widely used to quantify the impact of extreme losses. Owing to the lack of representative samples CVaR is sensitive to the tails of the underlying distribution. In order to combat this sensitivity, Distributionally Robust Optimization (DRO), which evaluates the worst-case CVaR measure over a set of plausible data distributions is often deployed.
Unfortunately, an improper choice of the DRO formulation can lead to a severe underestimation of tail risk. This 
paper aims at leveraging extreme value theory to arrive at a DRO formulation 
which leads to representative worst-case CVaR evaluations in that the above pitfall is avoided while simultaneously, the worst case evaluation is not a gross over-estimate of the true CVaR. 
We demonstrate theoretically that even when there is paucity of samples in the tail of the distribution, our formulation is readily implementable from data, only requiring calibration of a single scalar parameter. 
We showcase that our formulation can be easily extended to provide robustness to tail risk in  multivariate applications as well as in the evaluation of other commonly used risk measures. Numerical illustrations on synthetic and real-world data showcase the practical utility of our approach.

 \noindent \textbf{Keywords} Conditional Value at Risk; Distributionally Robust Optimization; Extreme Value Theory; Distortion Risk Measures
\end{abstract}
\maketitle
\section{Introduction}\label{sec:intro}
Distributional uncertainty is inevitable in risk management problems which involve 
evaluation of a stochastic objective function. 
Distributionally Robust Optimization (DRO)  (see \cite{rahimian2022frameworks}) is a popular tool which
attempts
to hedge against such uncertainty by 
computing the worst case evaluation of the desired stochastic objective over a set of plausible models.
This set of models, referred to as the uncertainty set in literature is typically constructed by considering
perturbations of
a nominal distribution (for instance, proposed by a regulator). 
A centerpiece of DRO literature is to arrive at a suitable choice of the uncertainty set from available data samples. 
Different
measures of "similarity" between distributions are used to this end. These include
moment constraints (see \cite{zymler2013distributionally,delage2010distributionally,hanasusanto2015distributionally}), likelihood based metrics (see \cite{ben2013robust,lam2019recovering,duchi2021statistics}) and optimal transport distances (see \cite{blanchet2019quantifying,mohajerin2018data,gao2023distributionally}). 

In several applications, it is natural to consider risk measures which are evaluated on distribution tails: for instance, in an insurance setting, one may desire to quantify the impact of excessively large claims. 
Owing to a paucity of tail-samples, estimation of such tail risk measures directly from data becomes difficult. One way to combat this is to propose that the data has been realized from a family of distributions (such as Gaussian), and evaluate the objective under the member of this family whose parameters match those of the data.
This can lead to significant model risk, 
as the underlying data distribution may differ substantially from the proposed family and one may end up underestimating the tail objective.
Such errors are considered to hazardous, since one may potentially end up being over-optimistic about the extent of outliers present in data.

Use of DRO becomes a natural step towards combating such model risk.
In order to ensure that the formulation is rich enough to the above underestimation,
recent works such as \cite{kruse2021toolkit,jin2024constructing,kruse2019joint} advocate for use of
DRO with
uncertainty sets which contain a large number of plausible distributions.
However, the flip side 
is that the resulting formulation may be too conservative, and one may end up with
evaluations which are excessively large.
Consequently, one strives to balance robustness (not severely under-representing outliers) against conservativeness (excessively large risk evaluations). 
In this paper, 
considering a class of stochastic objectives evaluated only on the largest $\beta$ fraction of data outcomes, we (i) precisely characterize
how the choice of uncertainty set affects the worst case risk as the tail level $\beta$ is made smaller (that is, the  objective is evaluated further and further into the tail) and (ii)
use these characterizations to arrive at a data-driven DRO formulation which while not being overly conservative still ensures robustness against distributional ambiguity.

\subsection{CVaR as a measure of tail risk}
A popularly used measure of tail risk is conditional value-at-risk (CVaR) (see \cite{mcneil} and references therein).   
Given a distribution $P$, and a risk level $\beta$, define the value at risk associated with $P$ as
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:VaR}
    v_{1-\beta}(P) =  \inf\{u: P(Z> u) \leq \beta\}.
\end{equation}
Define the CVaR associated with a measure $P$ evaluated at a risk level $\beta$ as $C_{1-\beta}(P) = E_P[Z \mid Z\geq v_{1-\beta}(P)]$. Note that in the previous expression, the random variable $Z$ is assumed to have a distribution $P$.
Axiomatically, CVaR is a coherent risk measure: it is monotonic, convex and homogeneous (see \cite{artzner1999coherent,follmer2002convex,acerbi2002spectral} for precise definitions) and has the following optimization formulation due to \cite{rockafellar2002conditional}, all of which make it amenable to data-driven computation:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:cvar_opt}
    C_{1-\beta}(P)  = \inf_{u}\left\{u+\frac{1}{\beta}E_{P}\left[Z-u\right]^+\right\}, \text{ where } [Z-u]^+ = \max\{(Z-u),0\}.
\end{equation}
These desirable properties have lead to CVaR becoming a preferred vehicle for introducing risk aversion in problems in finance, operations research, planning machine learning etc.  (see \cite{Krokhmal,acerbi2002coherence,Ban,Tamar,bienstock2014chance,summers2015stochastic}). In addition, unlike VaR, which only quantifies the extent of loss at a particular risk level $\beta$, CVaR, captures losses in the \textit{entire} tail region beyond risk level $\beta$. Consequently, CVaR has become a favored tool for quantifying extreme risks.  For instance, in calculating minimum capital requirements, the Basel III accords advocate the use of CVaR over VaR since it provides a more 
accurate representation of downside risks faced by banks.

Despite possessing properties that make it attractive from a modeling standpoint, 
evaluation of CVaR is highly sensitive to the tails of the data owing to a lack of representative samples. This difficulty is only exacerbated when the data has heavy tails (see \cite{Lim,Caccioli2018}). Using DRO to tackle distributional uncertainty 
therefore becomes a natural solution to hedging against model risk.
Distributionally Robust CVaR evaluation
has received a fair bit of attention in recent literature: 
for instance, \cite{zhu2009worst,gotoh2013robust}  attempt to evaluate the worst-case CVaR of a portfolio loss, while
\cite{natarajan2014probabilistic} derive a model for minimizing the worst case CVaR of regret in a combinatorial optimization problem. In the context of distributionally robust CVaR constrained programs, \cite{zymler2013distributionally,hota2019data,chen2023approximations} provide tractable reformulations.
Finally, \cite{pesenti2024optimizing,bernard2023robust,chu2024wasserstein,wu2022generalization,cai2023distributionally} study evaluation of a more general class of robust risk measures with a focus on developing tractable reformulations.  
\subsection{Main Contributions} While previous works provide reformulations to ensure computational tractability, the question of how the choice of uncertainty set affects the worst case CVaR evaluation still remains unanswered.  To illustrate in a real context, consider the following question: "If a financial regulator uses DRO to combat model uncertainty, how would the excess  
minimum capital requirements, incurred due to robustification
depend on the choice of uncertainty set?".
In mathematical terms: given a set of probability distributions $\mathcal P$ which consist of perturbations of a nominal model $P_0$, how does $C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal P) : =\sup_{P\in\mathcal P}C_{1-\beta}(P)$ compare with $C_{1-\beta}(P_0)$? 
To answer this question, diverging away from the computational aspects of robust CVaR evaluation, in this paper we aim to quantify how much faster $C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal P)$ grows as compared to $C_{1-\beta}(P_0)$ as $\beta \to 0$ as a function of the uncertainty set $\mathcal P$. 

 A follow-up question is following: is it possible to carefully tailor the uncertainty set so that resulting DRO formulation is robust, yet not overly conservative?
In the context of the previous example:
"if the true distribution of data were $Q$,
can a regulator carefully identify
a set of plausible models
$\mathcal P$ for which $C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal P)$ is readily estimable from available data samples \underline{and} $C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal P)\approx C_{1-\beta}(Q)$ (in a sense to be made precise later), so that the despite model uncertainty the minimum capital requirement proposed by the regulator is representative of the (unknown) ground truth?" 
% Mathematically, if the uncertainty causing extreme downside risk had a (unknown)  distribution $Q$, if $\mathcal P$ is inappropriately chosen, then it may happen that $C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal P) \gg (\ll) C_{1-\beta}(Q)$, that is risk may be severely over(under)-estimated. 
% An important related question is therefore following: is it possible to design $\mathcal P$ such that resulting DRO formulation is accurately captures down-side risk while being robust to the model ambiguity inherent to estimation of distribution tails.
% In the context of the previous example: "is it possible to carefully design the set of potential models $\mathcal P$ for which $C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal P)\approx C_{1-\beta}(Q)$ (in a sense to be made precise later) at practicable risk levels $\beta$, so that the despite model uncertainty, the minimum capital requirement proposed by the regulator is representative of the (unknown) ground truth?" 
In light of recent financial, supply chain and energy market disruptions (among several others), it has become increasingly pertinent to tackle such questions. This paper undertakes the task of attempting to provide an answer to this important, yet relatively less studied problem. 
Our main contributions are below:

\noindent \textbf{i) Characterization of worst case CVaR: }We show the
the existence of a scaling function $g_\beta$ which depends on (i) the quantile function of of $P_0$ and (ii) measure of discrepancy between distributions used to define $\mathcal P$ such that 
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:Robust_CVaR_scaling}
    C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal P) \sim g_\beta.
\end{equation}
Using \eqref{eqn:Robust_CVaR_scaling}, 
we  characterize the extent of conservativeness over the baseline evaluation $C_{1-\beta}(P_0)$ for two popular classes of uncertainty sets:

\noindent \textbf{a) } If $\mathcal P$ were a $p$-Wasserstein ball around $P_0$ (defined in \eqref{eqn:wass_ambiguity}) 
we demonstrate the existence of a  
constant $c\in(1,\infty]$ such that 
\[
\frac{\log g_\beta}{\log C_{1-\beta}(P_0)} \to c.
\]
If $P_0$ has a power-law with tail index $\gamma$, then $c =\gamma/p$ is finite.
If instead if $P_0$ were Weibullian, then $c=\infty$. 
This observation suggests that that while computationally tractable, Wasserstein uncertainty sets can lead to significant conservativeness, which worsens if the nominal distribution itself had lighter tails.
% In order to isolate the cause of this conservativeness, we identify the distribution which leads to the worst case CVaR evaluation. Surprisingly, this distribution merely translates mass of $P_0$ beyond $v_{1-\beta}(P_0)$ by an amount proportional to $\beta^{-1/p}$, while leaving the bulk of the distribution unchanged.

\noindent \textbf{b) } If $\mathcal P$ was defined using a polynomial-divergence ball around $P_0$ then 
\[
\frac{\log g_\beta}{\log C_{1-
\beta}(P_0)} \to c\in(1,\infty) \text{ as }\beta\to 0
\]
when $P_0$  has 
a power-law with a suitable tail index $\gamma$. Here $c$ depends \textit{only} the type of divergence measure. Thus, in the presence of heavy tails, polynomial divergences are highly conservative. If $P_0$ had Weibullian tails, we show that  
\[
{g_\beta} \sim K {C_{1-\beta}(P_0)} \quad \text{where }K \in(1,\infty).
\]
where we identify the precise dependence of $K$ on both the type of divergence used, as well as the tail of $P_0$.
This shows that divergence based formulations also lead to conservativeness for Weibullian nominal distributions, albeit by only a constant factor.

% Key to our results is the characterization of the types of distributions which lie within the ambiguity set $\mathcal P$. We demonstrate that  irrespective of the precise nature of $P_0$, Wasserstein based ambiguity sets always contain a power-law distribution whose tail strength is more than that of $P_0$. In the polynomial divergence case however, it turns out that unless $P_0$ were heavy-tailed, $\mathcal P$ can only contain distributions with a tail which decays at a rate similar to that of $P_0$. 

\noindent \textbf{ii) Design of representative DRO formulations: }
The conservativeness of Wasserstein (or) divergence based DRO arises because the uncertainty set contains distributions with tails significantly heavier than that of $P_0$.
To resolve this issue there are two aspects that must be taken care of 
: (i) design of  
an uncertainty set which \textit{only} contains distributions whose tails are identical in strength to that of $P_0$ and (ii) ensure that the tails of $P_0$ match those of $Q$. The first of these ensures that the extent of conservativeness is controlled, while the second results in a nominal model which captures the scale of outliers in data accurately. We propose a
class of uncertainty sets of the form
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:f-div-sets}
   \mathcal Q_\beta = \left\{P: \int \phi\left(\frac{dP}{dQ_{\beta}}\right)dQ_{\beta} \leq \delta \right\},
\end{equation}
where we allow $Q_\beta$ to depend on the risk level at which CVaR is to be evaluated.
In most DRO literature, the uncertainty set is constructed around (i) the empirical distribution of data (call it $\hat P_n)$ or (ii) a specific family of distributions whose parameters are readily estimable from data.  
Owing to lack of representative samples, the empirical distribution does not capture tail properties of the data, while if one uses a parametric family of distributions in (ii) above whose tail properties don't resemble those of the data, it may lead to severe under-estimation of risk.


In contrast, the proposed nominal distribution $Q_\beta$ is constructed by
extrapolating the distributional information available in samples from a baseline risk level $\beta_0 \gg \beta$. In turn $\beta_0$ is carefully selected so that the largest $\beta_0$ fraction of data samples are representative of the distribution tail, and it is possible to accurately estimate quantiles at level $\beta_0$ from
these samples.
Our choice is rooted in literature on extreme value theory (see \cite{deHaan} and references therein), where similar extrapolations
are used to estimate quantiles at risk levels at which there are often very few or no data samples. The result is that the tail properties of $Q_\beta$ become increasingly representative of those of $Q$ as $\beta\to 0$. Carefully selecting $\phi(\cdot)$ in \eqref{eqn:f-div-sets} further
results in a DRO formulation which does not severely underestimate tail-risk \textit{and} is not overly conservative. In particular, we show  that for any $c\in(0,1)$, if $ \beta_0 = \beta^c$ then
\begin{align*}
    \log C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal Q_\beta) &\sim \log C_{1-\beta}(Q) \text{ if } Q \text{ were heavy tailed and}\\
    C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal Q_\beta) &\sim C_{1-\beta}(Q) \text{ if } Q \text{ were light tailed}.
\end{align*}

Our result demonstrates that the proposed DRO is far less conservative than both Wasserstein and polynomial-divergence based DRO formulations irrespective of the nature of the tail of the data.
From a practical standpoint, the implication of the above result is the following:
given $n$ samples of data, setting $\beta_0(n) = n^{-\kappa}$ for a hyper-parameter $\kappa\in (0,1)$, the proposed formulation
is able to robustly infer tail risk in the statistically challenging regime where  $\beta(n) = c_0n^{-q}$ for $q\geq 1$. This is in contrast to methods using $\hat P_n$ as the nominal distribution, which can only produce reliable risk estimates at levels $\beta(n) \gg 1/n$.
% Our choice of $\mathcal P_\beta$ above is rooted in the vast literature on extreme value theory (see \cite{deHaan,de2016approximation,deHaanRV,resnick1998tail,resnick2008multivariate} and references therein), where similar extrapolations from a baseline level are used to estimate quantiles and probabilities at a much rarer target level, at which there are often very few or no data samples. Indeed, we demonstrate that given $n$ samples of data, our DRO formulation can be readily implemented to evaluate CVaR at risk levels $\beta(n) = n^{-q}$ for $q>1$. Current methods typically use the empirical distribution as the nominal distribution $P_0$ and can only be used to accurately asses CVaR at levels of order $n^{-1}$ or greater. Our method therefore allows evaluation of tail risk in a robust fashion well beyond what is possible using sample average based methods.

\noindent \textbf{iii) Extensions to distortion risk measures: }
In many instances, agents making decisions under uncertainty end up attributing non-linear weights to low probability events. Expected utility frameworks which include measures such as CVaR fail to capture such a behavior. 
To remedy this, the notion of distortion risk measures was introduced in \cite{yaari1987dual,wang1997axiomatic}, 
where the authors propose weighing tail probabilities by a "dual-utility" function to capture non-linearity in an agent's perception of distribution tails. Further, 
\cite{zhu2012asymptotic} derives the following representation of such risk measures: 
Let    
\[
g_{\beta}(x) = \begin{cases}
   g\left(\frac{x}{\beta}\right) \quad \quad\quad 0\leq x \leq  \beta\\
1   \quad\quad\quad\quad\quad \ \ \ \text{ $x>\beta$}
\end{cases}
\]
where $g: [0,1]\to[0,1]$ is an increasing function. 
Define a risk measure
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:distortion_risk_measure}
   \rho_{g,1-\beta}(P_0) = \int_{0}^{\infty} g_{\beta}(P_0(X\geq x)) dx .
\end{equation}
 The formulation in \eqref{eqn:distortion_risk_measure} allows a modeler to "weigh" different regions of the distribution tail by the function $g(\cdot)$ in assessment of risk: setting $g(x) = x$ (that is assigns linear weights to the entire tail) leads to CVaR.
Recent works have studied distributionally robust formulations for these risk measures (see \cite{pesenti2024optimizing,bernard2023robust}). We  demonstrate that both the asymptotics for
worst case risk evaluation extended to this generality
and that the tailored uncertainty set in \eqref{eqn:f-div-sets} continues to produce representative risk estimates.


\noindent \textbf{iv) Application to evaluation of systemic risk:} We present an application of the previous ideas to distributionally robust evaluation of contagion in a financial system. 
Since the 2008 crisis, this problem has received widespread attention (see \cite{acemoglu2015systemic,Haldane:sys,Allen:sys} and references therein). 
A popular framework to capture contagion is to model the system as a (bipartite) graph where one side of the graph represents the various financial entities and the other represents common asset holdings (see \cite{kley2016risk,elliott2014financial}).  
When one or more of these assets are distressed, it may lead to a cascading effect and cause wide-spread losses to the financial entities. 
Considering a somewhat stylized model of a banking network, we demonstrate that the proposed formulation may be used to 
robustly
evaluate the magnitude of losses caused by such a cascade.


\noindent \textbf{v) Experimental Validation:} To establish practical credibility for the developed methods, we conduct an extensive simulation study. We observe that consistent with theory, the proposed DRO framework leads to worst-case evaluations which are far more representative of tail risk than existing techniques.
As a further application we show that in a financial setting where a manager wishes to re-balance her portfolio 
at discrete intervals of time,  
that of the proposed formulation may be used to arrive at a re-balancing frequency whose hedging error is small.
Short case studies on the Danish Fire Insurance data set and the FAMA French data set further serve to illustrate the utility of our methods in real-world applications.



\subsection{DRO formulations involving tail risks} We conclude by giving a brief review of literature which considers DRO problems involving distribution tails: In the setting of evaluating rare event probabilities \cite{lam2017tail,bai2023distributionally} study a robust optimization approach which imposes shape constraints on the tail of the underlying probability densities. 
However, their formulations become intractable beyond the case where the 
objective is the expected value of a random variable.
Kullback-Liebler divergences have been used to measure model risk in various financial contexts by \cite{glasserman2014robust,schneider2015robust}. The papers
\cite{blanchet2020distributionally,birghila2021distributionally,yuen2020distributionally} leverage extreme value theory and derive asymptotics for worst case tail probabilities and show the dependence of the worst case evaluation on the tails of the nominal distribution and the uncertainty set. 
Specifically, the first two works show how the tail strength of the nominal distribution affects the worst case probability evaluation while
\cite{blanchet2020distributionally} goes further and arrives at a procedure to compute worst case quantiles 
using a simple line search. 
In the multivariate context, \cite{yuen2020distributionally} demonstrates bound on the worst case Value-at-Risk in situations where there in ambiguity about the joint distribution of the data.
The paper \cite{engelke2017robust} provides asymptotic bounds on the worst case probability evaluation in a multivariate setting, considering a divergence based uncertainty
set with additional moment constraints.
In related work, the recent paper \cite{deo2025scaling} provides asymptotics to understand the scaling behavior for the optimal cost of a distributionally robust chance constrained problem for several popular ambiguity sets.
Similarly, \cite{kruse2019joint,kruse2021toolkit} study bounds on the strength of tails of distributions which are permissible within different $\phi-$divergence neighborhoods of a nominal distribution. In particular, they derive conditions on $P_0$ and the growth rate of $\phi$ in \eqref{eqn:f-div-sets} under which these neighborhoods contain distributions with infinite mean.
Building on this, the recent work \cite{jin2024constructing} derives conditions on $\phi-$divergence in \eqref{eqn:f-div-sets} that ensure a finite worst case CVaR. Our paper is more comprehensive, demonstrating that several of the choices proposed by \cite{jin2024constructing}, while leading to finite worst-case CVaR are excessively conservative.


\subsection{Structure of the paper and notation} The rest of this paper is structured as follows: In Section~\ref{sec:model}, we outline our problem formulation and state and explain the modeling assumptions under which we establish our main results. In Sections~\ref{sec:wass_dro} and \ref{sec:divergence_dro}, we derive asymptotics for worst case CVaR resulting from choosing $\mathcal P$ using Wasserstein distances and polynomial divergences respectively. In Section~\ref{sec:calib_uncert} we discuss calibration of the uncertainty set $\mathcal P$, so as to reduce the conservativeness of the resulting DRO formulation. In Section~\ref{sec:distortion}, we provide extensions of our results to robust evaluation of distortion risk measures.
We present an application to evaluating tail risk associated with inter-connected banking networks in Section~\ref{sec:application}. We conclude with numerical experiments on simulated and real data in Sections~\ref{sec:simulation} and \ref{sec:real_data} respectively. 

\noindent \textbf{Notation:}
We use $\xrightarrow{a.s.}$, $\xrightarrow{p}$ and $\xrightarrow{d}$ to denote convergence almost surely, in  probability and distribution, respectively. 
For a function $f$, we let $f^{\leftarrow}$ denote its inverse.
For two sequences $x_t$ and $y_t$, we say that $x_t \sim y_t$ if $\lim_{t\to \infty} (x_t/y_t) =1$. Given a set $\mathcal X$, we let $\mathcal M(\mathcal X)$ denote the set of all probability measures on $\mathcal X$. 
For $P\in \mathcal M(\R)$, we let $F_P$ denote its cdf, that is $F_P(x) = P(X\leq x)$. Further, we let $\bar F_P(x) =1-F_P(x)$ be its tail cdf and $\Lambda_P(x) = -\log \bar F_P(x)$ denote the hazard function.
We follow the notation that $E_P[X] = \int X dP $ for any $P\in \mathcal M(\mathcal X)$.
 For distributions $(P,Q)\in\mathcal M(\mathcal X)$, we let $\Pi(P,Q)$ denote the set of all joint distributions with marginals $P$ and $Q$.
 For a map $T:\mathcal X\to \mathcal Y$ and $P_0\in\mathcal M(\mathcal X)$, we denote $P_0\circ T^{-1}$ to be the push-forward distribution of $P_0$ under $T$, that is $(P_0\circ T^{-1})(B) = P_0(T(Z) \in B) $. Given $\mathcal P \subset \mathcal M(\mathcal X)$  and a risk measure $\rho: \mathcal M(\mathcal X) \to \mathbb R^+$, we use $\rho(\mathcal P) = \sup_{P\in \mathcal P}\rho(P)$ to denote the worst case evaluation of $\rho$ in the uncertainty set $\mathcal P$. Throughout, vectors will be denoted by boldface; that is we will use $\xx =(x_1,\ldots x_d)$ to denote an element of $\R^d$.

\section{Problem Setup and Modeling Assumptions}\label{sec:model}
\subsection{Background on Extreme Value Theory} Analysis of distribution tails is ubiquitous in applications across risk management. 
Extreme Value Theory (EVT) (see \cite{deHaan,Embrechts1}) provides a structured framework using which one can model and statistically estimate tail quantiles. To achieve this, one places mild regularity assumptions on the scaled behavior of maxima: given i.i.d. samples $Z_1,\ldots Z_n$ of a random variable $Z$ with distribution $P$, one assumes that there exist scaling constants $\{(a_n,b_n): n\geq 1\}$  such that
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:basics_of_evt}
    \max_{i=1}^n \frac{Z_n-b_n}{a_n} \xrightarrow{d} H_\alpha,
\end{equation}
where  $H_\alpha$ has the distribution function
\[
H_\alpha(x) = \begin{cases}
\exp\left\{-(1+\alpha x)^{-1/\alpha}\right\}, \quad 1+\alpha x\geq 0 \text{ if }\alpha>0\\ 
\exp(-\exp(-x)) \quad\quad \quad \ \ \quad\quad\ \  \quad \quad \  \text{ if } \alpha = 0.
\end{cases}
\]
This condition is called the maximum domain of attraction (MDA) condition in literature and will be henceforth denoted by $P \in \MDA(\alpha)$.
The parameter $\alpha $ governs the rate of decay of tails of $P$. The $\alpha >0$ case corresponds to $P$ having power-law tails. This includes Pareto, Cauchy, student-t and Logisitc distributions among others. The $\alpha=0$ case corresponds to distributions whose tails decay slower than a power-law. This includes Gaussian, Exponential, Gamma and Weibull distributions, among others. 
An equivalent characterization of \eqref{eqn:basics_of_evt} can be obtained in terms of the tail cdf of $P$. The notion of regular variation is central to this endeavor:
\begin{definition}\label{def:RV}
    A function $f$ is regularly varying with index $\rho$ if for any $x>0$,
    \[
    \lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{f(nx)}{f(n)} = x^{\rho}.
    \]
   We denote this by $f\in \RV(\rho)$ or  $f\in \RV$  if there is no explicit need to specify the index of regular variation. If $f\in \RV(0)$, we  say that $f$ is slowly varying.
\end{definition}
Regular variation offers a structured approach to studying functions with polynomial-like growth/decay. 
Examples of regularly varying functions include $x^2$, $x^k\log^p(x)$ and $x^k \exp(\log^q(x))$ for $q\in(0,1)$. We refer the reader to \cite{deHaan} for more examples.
The following characterization of the condition $P\in \MDA$ in terms of the tail cdf $\bar F_P$ serves to significantly ease our analysis (see \cite{Embrechts1}, pg. 132): whenever $\alpha>0$,
\[
P\in \MDA(\alpha) \iff \bar F_P\in \RV(-\gamma), \, \ \ \text{ where } \gamma=1/\alpha.
\]


\subsection{Problem Formulation and Modeling Assumptions}\label{sec:formulation}
Consider  evaluating the worst-case (largest) CVaR over a set of pluasible data distributions:
\[
{\tt CVaR-Rob}(\beta):  \sup_{P\in \mathcal P}C_{1-\beta}(P).
\]
The set $\mathcal P$ is chosen as a perturbation of some nominal model $P_0$. More concretely, let $\mathcal D$ denote a discrepancy measure between two probability distributions, where $\mathcal D(P,P_0) = 0$ if and only if $P_0=P$ -  commonly used examples  include $\phi-$divergences and Wasserstein distance.
Note that nowhere do we require $\mathcal D$ to be a proper distance between probability distributions.
We consider a class of plausible models around $P_0$ by considering uncertainty sets of the form
\[
\mathcal P = \{P: \mathcal D(P,P_0)\leq \delta\}.
\]
Here $\delta$ is referred to the budget parameter, and specifies the extent of perturbation permissible. Throughout this paper, we will assume that $\delta$ is fixed. Whenever $\mathcal P$ is defined through either Wasserstein distance (see \eqref{eqn:wass_ambiguity}) or by setting
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:phi-div}
   \mathcal P =  \Dphi(P_0) = \left\{P: \int \phi\left(\frac{dP}{dP_0}\right) dP_0\leq \delta\right\},
\end{equation}
for some convex $\phi:\R \to\R$, $\Dphi(P_0)$ is a convex set of probability distributions. To see this, if $\mathcal D(\cdot)$ is defined via Wasserstein distance then $\mathcal P$ is a metric ball in the space of probability measures (see \cite{villani2008optimal}, Chapter 6), and is therefore convex.
So long as $\phi(\cdot)$ is convex, 
\[
P\to \int \phi\left(\frac{dP}{dP_0}\right)dP_0 
\]
is a convex  function (see \cite{csiszar1972class} pg. 194). Since $\Dphi(P_0)$ is the lower level set of a convex function it is a convex set. With 
all our probability measures being defined on $\R$ (a closed and convex set), 
an application of Lemma 1 of \cite{natarajan2014probabilistic} 
suggests that in either of these cases
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:wc_cvar}
    C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal P) = \inf_{u}\left\{u+ \frac{1}{\beta}\sup_{P\in\mathcal P}E_P\left[Z-u\right]^+\right\}.
\end{equation}
The representation \eqref{eqn:wc_cvar} is especially useful, since it permits us to use well known duality results for worst case expectation in order to examine $C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal P)$ and facilitates numerical evaluation.

\noindent \textbf{Assumptions on Tail Behavior:}
We impose some minimal regularity assumptions on $P_0$ discussed which are standard in literature on modeling distribution tails. 
\begin{assumption}[Heavy tailed nominal distribution]\label{assume:heavy_tailed_data}
There exists a $\gamma>0$  such that $\bar F_{P_0} \in \RV(-\gamma)$ .
\end{assumption}
% Assumption~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data} captures a wide range of distributions with power-law tails. This includes the various Pareto distributions, Student-t, Cauchy and Logistic distributions.  
% For more details on extreme value theory, we refer the reader to \cite{deHaan,Embrechts1,Resnickbook}.  Assumption~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data} is however fails to capture distributions with lighter tails (the case where $\gamma=0$). 
\begin{assumption}[Nominal Distributions with Weibullian Tails]\label{assume:weibullian_tails}
There exists a $\gamma>0$  such that $\Lambda_{P_0} \in \RV(\gamma)$.
\end{assumption}
Assumption~\ref{assume:weibullian_tails} supplements Assumption~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data} and captures several distributions whose tails decay at a rate faster than power law. To see this, $\Lambda_{P_0}\in \RV$ implies that $\bar F_{P_0}(x) = \exp(-x^{\gamma+o(1)})$, while under Assumption~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data}, $\bar F_{P_0}(x) = x^{-\gamma+o(1)}$. Examples of such light tailed distributions include
Exponential, Gamma, Weibull and Gaussian among others.
When Assumption~\ref{assume:weibullian_tails} holds, it can be further shown that
$P_0\in \MDA(0)$ (see \cite{de2016approximation}, Theorem 2). 
While it is possible that $P_0 \in {\tt MDA}(0)$ but $\Lambda_{P_0}\not\in \RV$, 
in this paper we will only consider the light-tailed distributions which satisfy Assumption~\ref{assume:weibullian_tails}. 
We point out that crucially that  Assumptions~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data}-\ref{assume:weibullian_tails} 
are semi-parametric in nature, and 
do not require (i) $P_0$ to be a member of a parametric family (such as Gaussian or student-$t$), and (ii)  $P_0$ to have a density.

Interestingly, the above assumptions  translate to regularity on CVaR. We collect these observations in Lemmas~\ref{lem:CVaR_regularity}-\ref{lem:CVaR_regularity_Weibull} below. We point out that Lemma~\ref{lem:CVaR_regularity} is known in literature (see \cite{zhu2012asymptotic}), and refer the reader to the appendix for the proof of Lemma~\ref{lem:CVaR_regularity_Weibull}. For a distribution $P$, define the function $U_P(t) = v_{1-1/t}(P)$. 
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:CVaR_regularity} 
     Suppose that a distribution $P$ satisfies Assumption~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data} with $\gamma>1$. Then, $C_{1-\beta}(P) \sim \gamma/(\gamma-1) U_P(1/\beta)$. As a result, $C_{1-\beta}(P) = \beta^{-1/\gamma+o(1)}$ as $\beta\to 0$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:CVaR_regularity_Weibull}
    Suppose that a distribution $P$ satisfies Assumption~\ref{assume:weibullian_tails}. Then, $C_{1-\beta}(P) \sim U_{P}(1/\beta).$ As a result, $C_{1-\beta}(P) = \log^{1/\gamma+o(1)}(1/\beta)$ as $\beta\to 0$.
\end{lemma}
The above two results imply the following:
for distributions with a power law type tail, CVaR grows polynomially, while for those with Weibullian tails, it grows poly-logarithmically in $(1/\beta)$. These observations serve as a starting point for quantifying the extent of conservativeness incurred upon the
use of DRO.
\section{Conservativeness of Wasserstein DRO} \label{sec:wass_dro}
Recall the following definition of Wasserstein distance between probability measures.
\begin{definition}\label{def:wass_distance}
Given a distance metric $d$, and $p\geq 1$, the $p-$th order Wasserstein between $P$ and $Q$ is given by 
\begin{equation*}
 W_{p}(P,Q) = \inf\left\{E_{\pi}\left[d^p(X,Y)\right]^{1/p}: \pi \in \Pi(P,Q) \right\}.   
\end{equation*}
\end{definition}
The $p$th order Wasserstein distance defines a metric on the space of probability distributions with finite $p$th moments (see \cite{villani2008optimal}, Theorem 6.9), and therefore is a discrepancy measure as in Section~\ref{sec:model}. Consider an uncertainty set is defined by the metric ball
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:wass_ambiguity}
    \mathcal W_{p,\delta} = \{P: W_{p}(P,P_0)\leq \delta\}.
\end{equation}
To gain intuition on asymptotic rates of $C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal W_{p,\delta})$ we first analyze the contents of 
$ \mathcal W_{p,\delta}$.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:wass_maximal}
There does not exist a finite $\delta>0$ for which there exists a  $P\in \mathcal W_{p,\delta}$ such that
    \[
    \liminf_{x\to\infty} x^{p} \bar F_P(x) > 0.
    \]
    Further  suppose that $P_0$ satisfies either Assumption~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data} with $p<\gamma$ or Assumption \ref{assume:weibullian_tails}. Then, for any $(\kappa,\delta)>0$ such that $(p+\kappa)<\gamma$ there exists  $\tilde P\in \mathcal W_{p,\delta}$ such that $\bar F_{\tilde P}\in \RV(-p-\kappa)$.
\end{proposition}
Proposition~\ref{prop:wass_maximal}  suggests that distributions  in a $p$-Wasserstein ball around $P_0$ can at most have a tail decaying as $x^{-p}$.
This suggests that 
 $C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal W_{p,\delta})$ can at most grow at a rate $(1/\beta)^{1/p}$. Alternately, the presence of a distribution with tail index $(p+\kappa)$ for any $\kappa>0$ in $\mathcal W_{p,\delta}$ suggests that the worst case CVaR is
 of order $(1/\beta)^{1/p+o(1)} $. Since $\gamma>p$, $\mathcal W_{p,\delta}$ \textit{always} contains distributions whose tail is significantly heavier than that of the nominal distribution. 
  In what follows, we demonstrate that the above upper and lower bounds are in fact tight, in that $C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal W_{p,\delta}) \sim (\beta/\delta)^{-1/p}$, and therefore Wasserstein DRO is excessively conservative when used to evaluate CVaR.

\subsection{Exact Characterization of worst case risk and tail asymptotics}
The following well-known duality result (see \cite{blanchet2019quantifying,gao2023distributionally}) provides a starting point for the subsequent analysis: for a measurable function $f$,
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:wass_duality}
     \sup_{P\in \mathcal W_{p,\delta} } E_P[f(Z)]= \inf_{\lambda\geq 0}\left\{ \lambda \delta^p  + E_{P_0}\left[ \sup_{y} \left\{ f(y) - \lambda|y-Z|^p\right\} \right]\right\}.
\end{equation}
Note that in our application, $f(y) = (y-u)^+$. It turns out that the inner maximization in \eqref{eqn:wass_duality} can be computed in closed form with this choice of $f(\cdot)$.
  Define 
 \[
\vartheta(u,\lambda) = u - \frac{1-1/p}{(p\lambda)^{1/(p-1)}}.
\] 
Note that as $p\downarrow 1$, $\vartheta(u,\lambda) \to -\infty$ if $\lambda< 1$, $\vartheta(u,\lambda) \to u$ if $\lambda\geq 1$.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:Dual_Mult}
For any $(u,\lambda)$, the inner expectation in \eqref{eqn:wass_duality} evaluates to
\[
E_{P_0}\left[\sup_{y} \left\{ (y  - u )^+- \lambda |y -Z|^p\right\}\right] = E_{P_0}\left[(Z-\vartheta(u,\lambda))^+\right],
\]
where if $p=1$, $\nu(u,\lambda)$ is to be understood as its limit as $p\downarrow 1$.
Consequently, the worst case CVaR can be written as the value of following joint optimization problem in $(u,\lambda)$:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:wc_variational_problem}
C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal W_{p,\delta}) = \inf_{u,\lambda\geq0}\left\{u+ \beta^{-1}\lambda \delta^p  + \beta^{-1}E_{P_0}[Z-\vartheta(u,\lambda)]^+\right\} = \inf_{u,\lambda\geq0}\varphi_\beta(u,\lambda)   . 
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}
The optimization problem in \eqref{eqn:wc_variational_problem} can be solved by setting the derivatives of $\varphi_\beta(u,\lambda)$ to $0$. This leads to an \textit{exact} characterization of worst case CVaR under the Wasserstein metric.    
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:wass_exact_characterisation}
    The worst case CVaR under Wasserstein ambiguity of order $p\geq 1$ has the form 
    \begin{equation}\label{eqn:c_wass_wc}
         C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal W_{p,\delta}) = C_{1-\beta}(P_0) +\delta/\beta^{1/p}.
    \end{equation}
Suppose further that $P_0$ satisfies Assumption~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data} with $\gamma > p$. Then, $C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal W_{p,\delta}) \sim \delta/\beta^{1/p}$. Consequently,
\[
\lim_{\beta\to 0}\left[\frac{\log C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal W_{p,\delta})}{\log C_{1-\beta}(P_0)} \right]= \frac{\gamma}{p}.
\]
Suppose $P_0$ satisfies Assumption~\ref{assume:weibullian_tails}, then one has the stronger condition that 
\[
\lim_{\beta\to 0} \left[\frac{\log C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal W_{p,\delta})}{\log C_{1-\beta}(P_0)}\right] =\infty.
\]
\end{theorem}


Upon inspection of \eqref{eqn:c_wass_wc}, the worst case CVaR evaluation can be seen as a "penalized" version of CVaR under the nominal distribution. 
The extent of this penalty term grows as (i) $\beta\to 0$ and (ii) the $\delta$ is made larger (therefore permitting more distributions to lie in $\mathcal W_{p,\delta}$).

\begin{remark}\label{rem:wu_et_al}
    \em The recent paper \cite{wu2022generalization} also demonstrates that the distributionally robust CVaR optimization problem with Wasserstein uncertainty can be evaluated as the CVaR under the nominal distribution plus a penalty term. However, an important aspect of our work is to understand structurally why the Wasserstein DRO leads to excessive conservativeness. Indeed unlike \cite{wu2022generalization}, the use of a duality argument above allows 
    for a constructive proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:wass_exact_characterisation}, and allows
    us to clearly identify
    the distribution leading to the worst case CVaR evaluation. 
\end{remark}
\begin{remark}\label{rem:lambda_p}\em
    Recall that existence of $p$-th moments is a requirement for defining Wasserstein distances. If $\bar F_{P_0}\in \RV(-\gamma)$, then $P_0$ has $p$th moment for all $p<\gamma$. The added condition $p<\gamma$ therefore ensures that $P_0$ lies in the space of all distributions with finite $p-$th moments.
\end{remark}
\subsection{Identifying the worst case distribution}  \label{sec:cons_Wasserstein} 
The growth rate for $C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal W_{p,\delta})$ proposed by
Theorem~\ref{thm:wass_exact_characterisation} corresponds to the CVaR of a random variable whose tail cdf decays at a rate $x^{-p}$. 
Due to Proposition~\ref{prop:wass_maximal},  $\mathcal W_{p,\delta}$ does not contain any such distribution.
In order to understand the cause of this apparent discrepancy, 
we explicitly identify the optimal transport plan which leads to the worst case CVaR evaluation in the result below:
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:wc_transport_map}
    The distribution which results in the worst case CVaR evaluation when
    $\mathcal P = \mathcal W_{p,\delta}$ satisfies
    \[
    P_{\beta, \tt wc} = P_0 \circ T_{\beta,\delta}^{-1}, \text{ where } T_{\beta,\delta}(x) = x\mv{1}(x\leq v_{1-\beta}(P_0)) + (x+ \delta/\beta^{1/p})\mv{1}(x>v_{1-\beta}(P_0))
    \]    
    if $p>1$. If $p=1$, then the worst case distribution is supported on the
    region 
    \[
    \bigcup_{c\geq 0}\left\{(x,y): y = x \mv{1}(x\leq v_{1-\beta}(P_0)) + (x+c)\mv{1}(x>v_{1-\beta}(P_0))\right\}.
    \]
\end{proposition}
Proposition~\ref{prop:wc_transport_map} demonstrates that the worst case distribution in fact translates "tail" samples of $Z$ further out, while the samples from the distribution meat remain unchanged.  
Interestingly, both $P_0$ and the worst case distribution $P_{\beta, \tt wc}$ 
have the \textit{same} tail behavior: 
if $P_0$ satisfies Assumption~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data} (or \ref{assume:weibullian_tails}) with tail parameter $\gamma>0$, so does $P_{\beta, \tt wc}$.
To illustrate this, let us consider the case where $p>1$ and fix
 $\beta$. Since the map $T_{\beta,\delta}(\cdot)$ is invertible, $
\bar F_{P_{\beta, \tt wc}}(t) = \bar F_{P_0}(T^{\leftarrow}_{\beta,\delta}(t))$ (resp. $\Lambda_{P_{\beta,\tt wc}}(t) = \Lambda_{P_0}(T^{\leftarrow}_{\beta,\delta}(t))$. 
Taking $t>v_{1-\beta}(P_0)$, the right hand side of the previous display becomes equal to $\bar F_{P_0}(t - \delta/\beta^{1/p}) $. 
Since regular variation is invariant to translations, $\bar F_{P_0}\in \RV$ (resp. $\Lambda_{P_0}\in \RV$) implies that $\bar F_{P_{\beta, \tt wc}}\in \RV$ (resp. $\Lambda_{P_{\beta, \tt wc}}\in \RV)$. 
The intuition behind the rather surprising observation above can be derived from
Proposition~\ref{prop:wass_maximal} which shows that it is too costly to transport samples of $P_0$ to a distribution whose tail decays exactly at the rate $x^{-p}$.
However, it is feasible to translate the samples in the tail region \textit{just} enough so that the resulting CVaR \textit{appears} as though it is being evaluated from a distribution whose tail decays as $x^{-p}$. We illustrate this intuition through Numerical Example~\ref{illustrate:wass_dro} below:
