\section{Design of Representative DRO Formulations}\label{sec:calib_uncert}
It is desirable that the uncertainty set be designed such that the worst-case evaluation resembles $C_{1-\beta}(Q)$ as closely as possible. We introduce the notion of a rate-preserving uncertainty set to capture this 
scenario:
\begin{definition}[Rate-Preserving uncertainty set]\label{def:Scale_preserving}
   Given a discrepancy measure $\mathcal D$, nominal distributions $Q_\beta$ and budget parameter $\delta$, let $\mathcal Q_\beta = \{P: \mathcal D(Q_\beta,P)\leq \delta\}$ denote a sequence of uncertainty sets, and let $Q\in \mathcal M(\mathbb R)$ be a fixed distribution. We say that $\mathcal Q_\beta$ is $Q$-\textit{weakly} rate-preserving if as $\beta\to 0$,
    \begin{equation*}
     \log C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal Q_\beta) \sim \log C_{1-\beta}(Q).
    \end{equation*}
     We say that $\mathcal Q_\beta$ is $Q$-\textit{strongly} rate-preserving if 
      \begin{equation*}
      C_{1-\beta}(\mathcal Q_\beta) \sim C_{1-\beta}(Q).
    \end{equation*}
\end{definition}
% Let $Q$ will always be used to denote the true data distribution so that $C_{1-\beta}(Q)$ represents true risk due to uncertainty. 
% Ambiguity sets which are rate-preserving with respect to $Q$ are desirable since they lead to robust evaluations which are (asymptotically) representative of the true downside risk.
% Momentarily suppose that $P_0=Q$, that is the nominal measure is actually the true distribution. 
% Then, it is immediately apparent from Theorems~\ref{thm:wass_exact_characterisation}-\ref{thm:poly_divergence} that if $P_0$ satisfies either Assumption~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data} or \ref{assume:weibullian_tails}, the resulting ambiguity set $\mathcal W_{p,\delta}$ is not even weakly scale preserving with respect to $Q$ since the worst case CVaR grows at an orders of magnitude faster rate than $C_{1-\beta}(P_0)$. Similarly, $\Dphi(P_0)$ is not even weakly scale preserving if $Q$ satisfies Assumption~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data} and is not strongly scale preserving if it satisfies Assumption~\ref{assume:weibullian_tails}. The reason is that $\Dphi(P_0)$ admits distributions with tails that are significantly heavier than that of $P_0$.
% Note that in practice $Q$ is unknown so it is impractical to assume that $P_0=Q$, and one needs to carefully
Definition~\ref{def:Scale_preserving} attempts to 
capture DRO formulations
which 
lead to worst-case risk evaluations that are robust, but at the same time, not too conservative. Indeed, when Wasserstein distances (or) polynomial divergences are used to 
construct uncertainty sets, the resulting worst case evaluation is significantly larger than the true risk. 
Instead, uncertainty sets $\mathcal Q_\beta$ which are $Q-$strongly (resp. weakly) rate preserving lead to less conservative risk evaluations than each of these two cases for heavy (resp. light) tailed $Q$. 
On the other hand, the extent of underestimation for such a formulation is also relatively small. 
We point out that Definition~\ref{def:Scale_preserving} is tight in that 
if one wishes to derive convergence rates for the worst-case risk evaluation to the true CVaR, 
additional conditions characterizing the rate of convergence in Assumptions~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data}-\ref{assume:weibullian_tails}  are required. While such conditions have been explored in extremes literature (see \cite{deHaan}, Chapter 4) this extension is beyond the scope of the current paper and will be pursued as a follow-up work.

A sufficient condition for uncertainty sets $\mathcal Q_\beta$ to be $Q-$weakly (strongly) rate preserving can be derived by comparing the tail of the worst case distribution with that of $Q$. This is the content of Proposition~\ref{prop:rate_preserving_suffcient} below.  
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:rate_preserving_suffcient}
    Let $\mathcal Q_\beta$ be constructed as in Definition~\ref{def:Scale_preserving}. Then if $\bar F_{Q}\in \RV(-\gamma)$ with $\gamma>1$, $\mathcal Q_\beta$ is $Q-$weakly rate preserving so long as 
    \begin{equation}\label{eqn:suff_cond}
        \liminf_{\beta\to 0} \inf_{t\in(0,\beta]} \frac{\log v_{1-t}(Q_\beta)}{\log v_{1-t}(Q)} \geq 1\quad \text{ and }\quad \limsup_{\beta\to 0} \sup_{t\in(0,\beta]} \frac{\log v_{1-t}(\mathcal Q_\beta)}{\log v_{1-t}(Q)} \leq 1.
    \end{equation}
 If instead $\Lambda_Q\in \RV$, then $\mathcal Q_\beta$ is $Q-$strongly rate preserving if 
 for any $\kappa>1$, 
 \begin{equation}\label{eqn:suff_cond_lt}
        \liminf_{\beta\to 0} \inf_{t\in(\beta^{\kappa},\beta]}  \frac{v_{1-t}(Q_\beta)}{v_{1-t}(Q)} \geq 1 \quad \text{ and }\quad \limsup_{\beta\to 0} \sup_{t\in(\beta^{\kappa},\beta]} \frac{ v_{1-t}(\mathcal Q_\beta)}{v_{1-t}(Q)} \leq 1, 
    \end{equation}
    and the upper bound in \eqref{eqn:suff_cond} holds uniformly $t\in (0,\beta^\kappa]$.
\end{proposition}
Since the condition in
Proposition~\ref{prop:rate_preserving_suffcient} is in terms of worst case quantiles, it is relatively easy to verify (as we do next) using several well studied characterizations.
As a consequence, Proposition~\ref{prop:rate_preserving_suffcient} provides a pathway to designing DRO formulations which lead to representative tail risk evaluations that are readily implementable using available data samples.

\subsection{EVT Based Design of Rate Preserving Uncertainty Set} 
There
are two steps to designing DRO formulations whose evaluations are representative of the true tail risk: first, one must
arrive at a nominal $Q_\beta$ whose tail properties are similar to those of $Q$ in that $Q_\beta$ does not severely under-represent outliers.
Having chosen $Q_\beta$ the discrepancy function $\mathcal D(\cdot)$ must then 
be chosen so distributions whose tail behavior differs significantly from $Q_\beta$ 
are not included in $\mathcal Q_\beta$ (so that the upper bound in \eqref{eqn:suff_cond} holds). We demonstrate how each of these steps may be accomplished
using ideas from extreme value theory. 

\noindent \textbf{Identification of Nominal Distribution:} 
If 
$\bar F_Q\in \RV(-\gamma)$,  $\bar F_Q(tx) \sim x^{-\gamma}\bar F_Q(t)$. If $t_0$ were chosen to be large, but such that there is enough data to accurately infer $\bar F_Q(t_0)$. Then conceptually, one may approximate $\bar F_Q(t)$ by $(t/t_0)^{-\gamma}\bar F_Q(t_0)$. 
If  instead $\Lambda_Q \in \RV(\gamma)$, the above observations can be repeated with $\bar F_Q$ replaced by $\Lambda_Q$. Suppose
\begin{align}\label{eqn:nominal_dist}
     dQ_{\beta}(t) &=  dQ(t) \mv{1}(t\leq v_{1-\beta_0}(Q)) + dG_{\beta}(t) \mv{1}(t>v_{1-\beta_0}(Q)), \text{ where }\nonumber  \\
G_{\beta}(t) &= \begin{cases}
    1-\beta_0 \left(\frac{t}{v_{1-\beta_0}(Q)}\right)^{-\gamma} \quad \quad\quad\quad\  \quad\text{ if $\bar F_Q\in \RV(\gamma)$}\\
1-\exp\left(\log(\beta_0)\left(\frac{t}{v_{1-\beta_0}(Q)}\right)^\gamma\right)      \quad\text{ if } \Lambda_Q\in \RV(\gamma),
    \end{cases} 
\end{align}
where $\beta$ is the target level at which one wishes to evaluate risk and $\log \beta_0 = c\log \beta$ 
for $c<1$. 
Note that $Q_\beta$ 
 is identical to $Q$ for $t\leq v_{1-\beta_0}(Q)$.
This represents the meat of the data where there are enough samples to accurately infer the empirical distribution. We therefore set the nominal distribution to be the data distribution in this region. In turn, for $t> v_{1-\beta_0}(Q)$ where there is a scarcity of samples, 
$Q_\beta$ is constructed by extrapolating the data distribution from $t_0 = v_{1-\beta_0}(Q)$ using the regularity in $\bar F_Q$ (or) $\Lambda_Q$. Observe that
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:nominal_tail}
   \bar F_{Q_\beta}(x) = \int_{x}^{v_{1-\beta_0}(Q)} dQ(t) + \int_{x\lor v_{1-\beta_0}(Q)}^\infty  dG_{\beta}(t) = Q((x,v_{1-\beta_0}(Q)]) + \beta_0\left(\frac{x\lor v_{1-\beta_0}(Q)}{v_{1-\beta_0}(Q)}\right)^{-\gamma}.   
\end{equation}
We use \eqref{eqn:nominal_tail} to derive Lemma~\ref{lem:lb_suff} which shows that $Q_\beta$ satisfies the lower bound in \eqref{eqn:suff_cond}.
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:lb_suff}
    Let the tail cdf of $Q_\beta $ be given by \eqref{eqn:nominal_dist}
    with $\log \beta_0 = c\log \beta$ for some $c<1$. Then, for any $\kappa>1$
    \[
    \liminf_{\beta\to 0} \inf_{t\in(0,\beta]} \frac{\log v_{1-t}(Q_\beta)}{\log v_{1-t}(Q)} \geq 1 \text{ if $\bar F_Q\in \RV$} \quad \text{ and }\quad \liminf_{\beta\to 0} \inf_{t\in(\beta^\kappa,\beta]} \frac{ v_{1-t}(Q_\beta)}{ v_{1-t}(Q)} \geq 1 \text{ if $\Lambda_Q\in \RV$}.
    \]
\end{lemma}
