\noindent\textbf{Choice of Divergence Function:} 
Momentarily suppose that $Q_\beta=Q$, that is the nominal distribution is actually equal  to the data distribution,
and that $\phi\in \RV(p)$ for some $p>0$. Then $\Dphi(Q)$ is not even $Q$-weakly scale preserving if $\bar F_Q\in \RV$, and is not $Q$-strongly scale preserving $\Lambda_Q\in \RV$. 
Recall that from Proposition~\ref{prop:f_div_contents}, the issue was that the polynomial growth of $\phi(\cdot)$ is
insufficient to exclude distributions with tails heavier than $Q$  from the resulting uncertainty set.
Motivated by this discussion, let us assume the following:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:phi_growth}
    \log \circ \ \phi  \in \RV(p) \text{ for some } p>0.
\end{equation}
Observe that \eqref{eqn:phi_growth} implies $\phi(\cdot)$ grows at a faster than polynomial rate.
It turns out that if $\Dphi(Q_\beta)$ is defined using  $\phi(\cdot)$ satisfying
\eqref{eqn:phi_growth} then distributions with tails significantly heavier than $Q$ are excluded, and
the upper bound in Proposition~\ref{prop:rate_preserving_suffcient} holds. This leads to Theorem~\ref{thm:scale_preserving_dro} below.
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:scale_preserving_dro}
  Suppose $\phi(\cdot)$ satisfies Assumption~\ref{assume:f-assume} and the growth condition in \eqref{eqn:phi_growth}. Let  the tail cdf of $Q_\beta$ be given by \eqref{eqn:nominal_dist}  where $\log \beta_0 =  c \log \beta$ for some $c<1$. Then
   if $\bar F_Q\in \RV(-\gamma)$ (resp. $\Lambda_Q\in \RV(\gamma)$), then $\Dphi(Q_\beta)$ is $Q$-weakly (resp. $Q-$strongly).
   In particular, $C_{1-\beta}(\Dphi(Q_\beta)) = [C_{1-\beta}(Q)]^{1+o(1)}$ (resp. $C_{1-\beta}(\Dphi(Q_\beta)) = (1+o(1)) C_{1-\beta}(Q)$) as $\beta\to 0$. 
\end{theorem}
While selecting a divergence function $\phi(\cdot)$ which satisfies \eqref{eqn:phi_growth}, it is important that the convex conjugate $\phi^*$ be analytically tractable for the sake of computation. This may be achieved for instance
 by setting $\phi^*(s) = (1+s)\ln(1+s)$. An application of \cite{jin2024constructing}, Proposition 2 shows that $\phi^*(s)$ is the convex conjugate of $\phi(x) = e^{x-1} - x$, which satisfies \eqref{eqn:phi_growth}. In general, the conjugate may be chosen as any convex function $\phi^*(s)\sim s\log^{r} s$ for $r\in(0,\infty)$, and the resulting $\phi$ can be shown to satisfy the conditions of \eqref{eqn:phi_growth}. 

