\subsection{Design of Representative DRO}
Propositions~\ref{prop:Robust_distortion_measures}-\ref{prop:Robust_distortion_measures_poly_divergence} suggest that the conservativeness of worst case risk evaluation extends to the more general case; indeed the same tail bounds derived in Theorems~\ref{thm:wass_exact_characterisation}-\ref{thm:poly_divergence} are applicable here as well.
A natural question is to ask whether one may calibrate the uncertainty set more carefully in order to obtain a DRO formulation which is representative of the true risk. In particular, we desire that the robust evaluation is $Q$-rate preserving with respect to $\rho_{g,\cdot}$ (that is, when $C_{1-\beta}$ is replaced by $\rho_{g,1-\beta}$ in Definition~\ref{def:Scale_preserving}).
Interestingly, even in this more complex case where the risk measure is obtained by using a weighted average of quantiles, uncertainty sets derived from EVT continue to produce representative worst-case evaluations. 
\begin{theorem}\label{thm:dist_measure_calib}
Suppose $g(x) = x^{k}$ for some $k\in( 0,\infty) $ and $\phi(\cdot)$ satisfies Assumption~\ref{assume:f-assume} and the growth condition \eqref{eqn:phi_growth}. Then, if $Q_{\beta}$ has a tail cdf function as defined in \eqref{eqn:nominal_dist},
\begin{align*}
    \log \rho_{g,1-\beta}(\Dphi(Q_{\beta})) &\sim \log \rho_{g,1-\beta}(Q) \quad\quad\quad\text{ if $\bar F_Q\in \RV$ and}\\
    \rho_{g,1-\beta}(\Dphi(Q_{\beta})) &\sim \rho_{g,1-\beta}(Q) \quad\quad\quad\quad\ \ \text{ if $\Lambda_Q\in \RV$}.
\end{align*}
\end{theorem}
As was the case with CVaR the key here lies in comparing the quantiles of $Q_\beta$ and the distribution with 
heaviest tail in $\Dphi(Q_\beta)$ with $Q$ 
in the sense given by Proposition~\ref{prop:rate_preserving_suffcient}.
Importantly however,  unlike CVaR, robust evaluation of more general distortion risk measures is not easy outside of a few cases (see \cite{pesenti2024optimizing}). 
Fortunately, the following bound holds: 
\begin{align*}
    \rho_{g,1-\beta}(\Dphi(Q_{\beta})) &\leq \beta^{-1}\int_{0}^\beta g^\prime(t/\beta) v_{1-t}(\Dphi(Q_{\beta}))dt = E[g^\prime(Y/\beta) v_{1-Y}(\Dphi(Q_{\beta}))],
\end{align*}
where $Y$ is uniformly distributed in $[0,\beta]$.  Let $\varrho_\beta(\Dphi(Q_{\beta}))$ be the right hand side above. 
Since it is possible to easily compute worst case VaR via a simple root-finding procedure from \cite{blanchet2020distributionally}, $\varrho_\beta(\Dphi(Q_{\beta}))$ is readily computable using Monte-Carlo simulation. The next result 
which directly follows from the proof of Theorem~\ref{thm:dist_measure_calib}
demonstrates that if in the interest of computational tractability, one were to use $\varrho_\beta(\Dphi(Q_{\beta}))$ as a proxy for worst-case risk, not much is lost in terms of conservativeness.
\begin{lemma}\label{lem:ub_for_risk_measures}
The conclusions of Theorem~\ref{thm:dist_measure_calib} continue to hold if $\rho_{g, 1-\beta}(\Dphi(Q_{\beta})) $ is replaced by $\varrho_{g,1-\beta}(\Dphi(Q_{\beta})) $. 
\end{lemma}

