\section{Multivariate Extension: Application to Robust Evaluation of Tail Risk in a financial network}\label{sec:application}
Consider a financial network with $K$ agents (financial firms) and $d$ objects (risky assets). When object $j$ gets damaged, a claim  of $\xi_j$ arises, which is distributed among all agents which have insured that object. The amount of loss on object $j$ which affects agent $i$ is therefore  
\[
f_{i}(V_j) = W_{ij}\xi_j \mv{1}(j\leftrightarrow i)
\]
where $W_{ij}$ is the proportion of loss of object $j$ which affects firm $i$. The indicator $\mv{1}(j\leftrightarrow i)$ equals $1$ if firm $i$ has invested in object $j$ and equals $0$ otherwise.
Let $A$ be a matrix whose  $(i,j)$the entry $A_{ij} = W_{ij}\mv 1(i\leftrightarrow j) $.
Further, we allow cross holdings between firms, in that for firms $(k,l)$, $C_{kl}$ denotes the fraction of firm $k$ owned by firm $l$. Let $\hat C$ denote a matrix with
 $\hat C_{ii} = 1-\sum_{j}C_{ji}$ and $\hat C_{ij}=0$ if $i\neq j$. This is the fraction of assets owned by  shareholders \textit{external} to the banking network.
 Then, the market value of the firms is given by $\mv{F}(\XX) = \hat C (\mv{I} - C)^{\leftarrow} A \XX$ (see \cite{elliott2014financial}). 
Suppose these firms are insured by an external agent, who wishes to robustly evaluate tail risk due to shocks to asset prices. As in the uni-variate case, this amounts to evaluation of worst-case CVaR 
\[
\inf_{u}\left\{u+ \frac{1}{\beta}\sup_{P\in\mathcal P}E_P\left[L(\XX)-u\right]^+\right\}, 
\]
where $L(\cdot)$ maps the distress in asset prices to size of the claim incurred by the agent and
$\mathcal P$ is an uncertainty set constructed around the distribution of  $\XX$.  
Different measures of risk may be obtained from constructing functions of the exposure vector $\mv{F}$. 
For instance the $p$th norms of $\mv F$, $L_p(\zz) = \|\mv{F}(\zz)\|_p$ allow a regulator to capture 
a variety of features related to systemic risk: setting $p=1$ allows for evaluation of the overall contagion, while $p=\infty$ gives the 
largest loss suffered among all firm in the network. Indeed, such losses have been studied in the context of characterizing probabilities of large losses (see \cite{kley2016risk}).

From Proposition 5 of \cite{kruse2021toolkit}, for any loss function $L$, divergence $\phi$, and random variable $\XX$, the following result holds:
\begin{equation}\label{eqn:mv_to_uv}
    \sup_{\tilde P \in \Dphi(P_0)}E_{\tilde P}\left[L(\XX) - u\right]^+ = \sup_{P\in \Dphi(P_Z)}E_{P}\left[Z - u\right]^+
\end{equation}
where $P_Z$ is the distribution of $Z = L(\XX)$. A key consequence of \eqref{eqn:mv_to_uv} is that whether the uncertainty set is designed at the  asset level (that is, around $\XX$) or at the loss level (that is, around $Z$) does not affect the worst case evaluation. For our case, this means that is order to evaluate systemic risk in a manner robust to the distribution of $\XX$, it is sufficient to do so in a manner that is distributionally robust to $Z$ instead. In order to showcase how the methods in Section~\ref{sec:calib_uncert} may be adapted to this setting, we make some basic regularity assumptions on $L(\cdot)$ and the distribution of $\XX$.
\begin{assumption}\label{assume:loss_functional}
    Suppose that the loss function satisfies the homogeneity condition that for any $\xx\in \R^d$, $L(t\xx) = t^{\rho} L(\xx)$ for some $\rho>0$.
\end{assumption}
Note that the above assumption is quite mild, and is readily satisfied for losses defined through the $p$th norm as above, as well as several more general frameworks. We refer the reader to \cite{deo2023achieving} for more details. It has been empirically observed that asset losses are heavy tailed. To capture this, we make the following assumption, which is a multivariate generalization of Assumption~\ref{assume:heavy_tailed_data}:

\begin{assumption}\label{assume:mrv}
 Let $\mv{M}_n = \max_{i=1}^n \XX_i$, where the maximum is taken component-wise. Then there exists a limiting random variable $\YY$ and a scaling sequence $a_n$ such that $a_n^{-1}\mv{M}_n \xrightarrow{d}\YY$. 
\end{assumption}
% Note that  \eqref{eqn:mv_to_uv} implies that it is sufficient to place an appropriate uncertainty set around the distribution of $Z$ instead, in order to use the theory developed in Section~\ref{sec:calib_uncert} here, it is necessary to characterize the tail behavior this random variable. It turns out that  
% Assumptions~\ref{assume:loss_functional}-\ref{assume:mrv} are
% sufficient in to this end.
\begin{proposition}\label{prop:mv_cvar}
    Suppose that  Assumptions~\ref{assume:loss_functional}-\ref{assume:mrv} hold. Then with $Q = Q_0\circ L^{-1}$,  $ \bar F_{Q}\in \RV$.
\end{proposition}
Proposition~\ref{prop:mv_cvar} suggests that 
under reasonably mild assumptions, the loss distribution
satisfies the regularity conditions required to design representative DRO formulations for the uni-variate case.  
Therefore, here too, one expects that the uncertainty set from Section~\ref{sec:calib_uncert} 
would work well to combat model risk.
\begin{corollary}\label{cor:mv_extension}
    Let Assumption~\ref{assume:loss_functional}-\ref{assume:mrv} hold. 
    Let $Q$ be the distribution of
    $L(\XX)$. Then, defining $Q_{\beta}$ as in \eqref{eqn:nominal_dist} the conclusions of Theorem~\ref{thm:scale_preserving_dro} continue to remain true.
\end{corollary}
\begin{remark}\label{rem:mv_alternate}
    \em  Corollary~\ref{cor:mv_extension} remains true if we wish to hedge against model uncertainty while evaluating  $L(\XX)$ so long as $L(\cdot)$ and $\XX$ satisfy Assumption~\ref{assume:loss_functional}-\ref{assume:mrv}. This includes several other important examples such as linear programs with stochasticity, outputs of neural networks and capacitated supply network problems among others. We refer the reader to \cite{deo2023achieving} for a more comprehensive list of such examples.
\end{remark}
\section{Simulation Experiments}\label{sec:simulation}
We first demonstrate the applicability of the proposed DRO on synthetic data. The following procedure is used to compare the performance of our formulation with other uncertainty sets commonly used in literature: given a target level $\beta$, intermediate level $\beta_0$ (indicated in the respective figures) and number of samples $n$:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item[(i)] Generate samples $\{Z_1,\ldots,Z_{n}\}$ from the from the data distribution. Evaluate worst-case risk using Algorithm~\ref{alg:robust_cvar_estimation}.
    \item[(ii)] Repeat steps (i) ${\tt rep}$ times, each time with a fresh input data.
    \item[(iii)] Compute $C_{1-\beta}(Q)$
    from a Monte-Carlo simulation with $n=5e6$ samples. Compare this with median and lower quartiles of the above $\tt rep$ runs.
\end{enumerate}
In the multivariate setup, in step (i) above we construct $Z_i=L(\XX_i)$, where $\{\XX_1\ldots ,\XX_n\}$ are generated from the distribution of risk causing factors. 
In order to test whether $\bar F_Q\in \RV$, it suffices to ascertain that $\gamma<\infty$ with a sufficiently high confidence. As a proxy, we instead check for $\gamma< M$ (since if $\gamma<M$, certainly $\gamma<\infty)$. Recall that \cite{deHaan}, Theorem 3.2.5 shows that if $k = \lfloor n/\beta_0(n)\rfloor$ samples are used to estimate $\gamma,$ then $\sqrt{k}(\hat \gamma_n-\gamma) \to N(0,\gamma^2)$ as $n\to\infty $ (under mild additional conditions which we omit for brevity).
This suggests the following hypothesis test for heavy tailedness: let $H_0: \gamma \geq M$ and $H_a:\gamma<M $. Given $n$ input samples of data, a confidence level $(1-\alpha)$, and Hill estimator $\hat \gamma_n$ computed using $k$ tail samples, ${\tt REJECT}$ $H_0$ if $\hat \gamma_n < M (1-z_{\alpha}/\sqrt{k})$ and conclude that $\bar F_Q\in \RV$. 
\subsection{Uni-variate Example}\label{sec:univariate}
Consider first a simple uni-variate example where $Z$ represents the loss due to an insurance claim. We consider two distributions for $Z$: (i) we suppose that $\bar F_{Q}(x) \sim 0.2 x^{-3.4}\log(x)$ and (ii) $\Lambda_Q(x) \sim x^{0.9}\log^{0.5}(1+x)$. Note that the first example has $\bar F_Q\in \RV$ and the second has $\Lambda_Q\in \RV$. 
For each of the above distributions, we first evaluate using $n$ samples of data, the distributionally robust CVaR under the following $\phi-$divergence based uncertainty sets: (i) Gaussian nominal distribution whose mean and variance are evaluated from data with $\phi(t) = 0.5(t-1)^2$, (ii) nominal distribution $\hat Q_{\beta}^{(n)}$ with $\phi(t) = 0.5(t-1)^2$, and (iii)  nominal distribution $\hat Q_{\beta}^{(n)}$ with $\phi(t) = e^{t-1}-t$. For fair comparison, we use common random numbers to draw samples for each of the above evaluations.
We plot the median  (dashed line) and the range between the median and the lower quartile (shaded region) of risk evaluation
computed over $\tt reps$ replications for each setup.
For reference, we also plot the true CVaR (solid black line). Results of the experiment are given in Figure~\ref{fig:data_univaruiate} below.



