     \caption{Common parameters: $n=500$, $\beta_0 = \min\{0.1, \ \beta^{0.5}\}$, $\delta = 0.1$, ${\tt reps}=100$.  Markers on each graph denote actual risk evaluations.}\label{fig:data_univaruiate}
\end{figure}

We point out a few important observations: in Figure~\ref{fig:data_drive_ht} where data is heavy tailed, the $\chi^2-$divergence DRO ends up overestimating the underlying risk by a significant amount, even when tails of the nominal distribution are representative of those of $Q$. Conversely using a Gaussian distribution leads to severe underestimation of risk even when a fairly generous choice of uncertainty set is used.
In Figure~\ref{fig:data_driven_lt} where data is
light tailed, the $\chi^2$-divergence based DRO is not too conservative; however, using the proposed DRO still ends up cutting the conservativeness by about 50\%. Crucially, irrespective of the tails of $Q$, 
even the \textit{lower} quartiles of the worst-case evaluation are greater than the true risk: indeed over ${\tt reps} = 100$, the evaluation of worst case risk exceeds the true risk $95$ times (averaged over different risk levels). This suggests that the estimates are robust in that risk is not underestimated. 

To investigate the extent of underestimation (whenever it occurs), in Figures~\ref{fig:ht_scatters}-\ref{fig:lt_scatters} we display the scatter plots of the risk evaluations over the first $30$ of the ${\tt reps} =100$ independent replications used in this experiment. In instances where the CVaR is underestimated, its extent is small for the proposed method (marked by the red stars): indeed even the smallest evaluation of robust risk over $100$ replications only underestimates downside risk by only about $25\%$, which is significantly less than when a Gaussian nominal distribution is used.

\subsection{Insurance Risk}\label{sec:Sys_risk}
Consider the setup from Section~\ref{sec:application} where there are $d=48$ assets and $K$ firms invested in these assets. We suppose that $q = d/K$ is an integer. Assume that the connectivity matrix $A_\lambda = (1-\lambda) A_0+\lambda A_1$ for some $\lambda\in[0,1]$. Here $A_0$ is a matrix such that $A_{i,j} =1$ if $i\in\{q(j-1),\ldots qj-1\}$ and $A_{0,i,j}=0$ otherwise and $A_1$ is a matrix such that $A_{1,i,j} = 1/K$ for all $(i,j)$. 
The first of the above represents a 
situation where each firm invests in a distinct set of assets. The second situation represents a fully connected network, where losses on any given asset has an impact on \textit{all} firms. The matrices $\{A_\lambda:\lambda\in[0,1]\}$ capture all intermediate levels of shared assets. 
We also assume that there are no cross holdings, so that $C=0$ and $\hat C$ is the identity matrix. 
To 
test the robustness of our approach to the underlying structure of the financial system, we compare the performance of all methods on various network setups (that is by varying $\lambda$ and $K$).
The distribution generating losses on assets is assumed to have generalized Pareto marginals, with a dependence given by a multivariate $t$ copula with $4$ degrees of freedom and whose covariance matrix is identity (note that this \textit{does not} imply independence of asset losses). We compute the robust CVaR of $L(\zz) = \|A_\lambda \zz\|_p/d$ (notice that we normalise the loss by number of assets). We repeat the two experiments conducted in the previous section. As in the univariate example, the proposed formulation is simultaneously less conservative than $\chi^2-$divergence, while not underestimating risk like when Gaussian nominal distribution is used; see Figure~\ref{fig:systemic_risk} for results.



