\subsection{Discrete Delta Hedging} Consider a single stock $\{S_t: t\in[0,1]\}$ whose price follows a geometric Brownian motion with mean $\mu$ and volatility $\sigma^2$, and  a call option whose terminal
payoff  is $C(S_1) = (S_1-K)^+$. 
Suppose a portfolio aims to replicate the option payoff. We divide the overall time window into $m$ parts and re-balance the portfolio at the start of each time period.
At time step $t_i = i/m$, the total value of the portfolio is the sum of cash investments, ${\tt cash}(t_i)$ plus the stock value, ${\tt stock}(t_i)$. The number of shares of $S$ held between $[t_i,t_{i+1})$ is denoted by $\delta_{t_i}$, and is computed using the Black-Scholes formula. The stock and cash values are given  by the recursion 
\begin{align*}
    {\tt stock}(t_i) &= S_{t_i} \delta_{t_i}\\
    {\tt cash}(t_i) &= {\tt cash}(t_{i-1})\exp(r/m) -S_{t_i}(\delta_{t_i} - \delta_{t_{i-1}}) -k_0 - k_1|\delta_{t_i}-\delta_{t_{i-1}}|S_{t_i},
\end{align*}
where $r$ is the risk-free interest rate, $(k_0,k_1)$ are respectively the fixed cost of transaction and the cost to transact per share.
The initial stock and cash, call them ${\tt stock}(0)$ and ${\tt cash}(0)$ are set to that portfolio which determines the option price when transaction costs are set to $0$.
The random variable of interest for us is the hedging error, or the absolute difference between the option payoff and portfolio value at time $t=1$,
\[
X_{\tt err} = \left|(S_1-K)^+ - {\tt cash}(1) - {\tt stock}(1)\right|.
\]
We test the performance of the proposed DRO in hedging against model uncertainty in $X_{\tt err}$. To this end, for each $m$ we compute the CVaR of $X_{\tt err}$ at $\beta=0.01$ using a large Monte-Carlo simulation, and compare it with worst-case evaluation under the uncertainty sets from Section~\ref{sec:univariate}. The results are summarized in Figure~\ref{fig:delta_heedge}. We note two observations: first, the proposed DRO approximates the risk under the data distribution well irrespective of the frequency of re-balancing, giving an error of about $20\%$.  Further this observation is agnostic to the actual model specifications. 
Second, due to discretizations and transaction costs, the delta hedge will never exactly replicate the option price; instead, the CVaR of the error has a U-shaped curve (black line). The shape of this curve is closely replicated by the proposed DRO in the following sense:  
if an investor selects the re-balancing frequency with minimum worst-case risk evaluation under the proposed DRO, then the resulting CVaR of $X_{\tt err}$ under the data distribution is within $10\%$ of its minimum value. Our experiment therefore suggests that the proposed DRO can be used to effectively combat against model risk while hedging a financial option. 
