\section{Conclusion and Future Directions}We arrive at a DRO formulation for a wide class of tail risk measures that is robust, while not being too conservative. We demonstrate that even in regimes where there is a severe paucity of representative tail samples and sample averages fails to produce a good uncertainty set, the proposed method continues to produce accurate risk estimates. There are two directions which merit investigation: first, several works derive conditions on the budget parameter $\delta$ that ensure the output of the DRO formulation acts as a lower bound for the true risk with a required level of confidence. A follow up question to ask is whether under one can impose minimal additional regularity assumptions on $Q$ beyond those given by regular variation to arrive at such a $\delta$ while estimating tail risk measures. Second, one may ask whether extreme value theory based DRO formulations may be naturally incorporated into stochastic optimization formulations, so as to be able to robustly solve decision making problems involving distribution tails. We intend to pursue both these directions as follow-up works. 
