\subsection{Analysis of Dataset 2}\label{sec:dataset2_results}

We further explore the two-dimensional Klowss Model's behavior by sampling all 10 ``Varying'' parameters from Table \ref{table: parameters perturbed} to generate Dataset 2. We summarized all 54,450 simulations in this dataset by computing the Cell count and Final cell density DVs and found that 3-dimensional DRDVs are suitable representatives for the DVs (Supplementary Figure \ref{fig:scree_plots}), and that $k=4$ clusters reliably cluster the data (Supplementary Figure \ref{fig:elbow_plots}). We trained the $k$-means clustering algorithm to the training datasets for both types of DRDVs, and the parameter labels that resulted from both types of DRDVs led to high OOS accuracy values (Table \ref{tab:OOS_scores}). The representative simulations from the four clusters demonstrate that Cluster 1 (4) simulations have the largest (smallest) necrotic cores. Representative DVs for these simulations  quantify these differences (Supplementary Figure \ref{fig:DS2_representative_DVS}).

Ridgeline plots visualize the distribution of parameter samples within each cluster (Figure \ref{fig:DS2_counts}). The ridgeline plots when using Cell counts or Final cell density DRDVs are similar, so we present the Cell counts DRDV results here (Final cell density DRDV results shown in Supplementary Figure S\ref{fig:DS2_density}). Visual inspection of the distributions reveals that:
\begin{itemize}
\item Cluster 1 emerges from low $c_a$ values; 
\item Cluster 2 emerges from intermediate $c_a$ values, large $c_d$ values, low $\eta_1$ values, and low $\eta_3$ values; 
\item Cluster 3 emerges from intermediate $c_a$ values, low to moderate $c_d$ values, and moderate to large $\eta_1$ values; and 
\item Cluster 4 emerges from large $c_a$ values, and large $\eta_1$ values.
\end{itemize}
 These observations suggest that the most sensitive models parameters are $c_a$, $c_d$, $\eta_1$, and $\eta_3$.

