\section{Introduction}

Product homogeneity might be one of the most difficult hypothesis of perfect competition to defend in front of new Economics students. Current biggest market capitalizations are firms that have built their success on objective and subjective differentiation: Microsoft and Apple. Apple for instance does not try to sell computers, it want to sell ``Macs'', emphasizing their differentiation strategy. Examples of such behaviors are everywhere: from credit cards to car makers, almost all companies try not to sell a standard product, but their exclusive version of the product.

Despite being ubiquitous, this fact clearly contradicts the seminal paper from \cite{hotelling1929} who, after introducing the first model of spatial competition, concluded on the tendency of rational firms to select minimum differentiation. It is only 50 years later, with the seminal work of \cite{aspremont1979} that full differentiation has been seen as a possibility if transportation costs were quadratic. 

Horizontal differentiation has been and is still a topic that deserves attention. In this paper, we decide to mention ``horizontal'' rather that ``spatial'' differentiation as we believe that the main relevance of these models is not in their spatial use. Geographic implantation of firms is a very complex matter on which it seems hard to bring useful results with general models. On the contrary, if one sees the spatial space in models said to be \textit{a la Hotelling} as a more general space that can represent political opinions, tastes or product characteristics, these models become much more insightful (as did \cite{wendell1981new} in their seminal paper with voting theory). Indeed, if studying the position of ice-cream sellers on a segment that should model a beach can be seen as far from real world applications, the $[0,1]$ segment becomes much more insightful if it is seen as a bipartisan spectrum or a product feature spectrum. 

This work is therefore motivated by three major limitations and needs in the current literature on horizontal differentiation. We refer to the literature review of \cite{BiscaiaRicardo2013Mosc} for a good state-of-the-art and historical description of previous models.

First, as we want to give to horizontal differentiation models a more general relevance than spatial localisation on segments, we need to enlarge the spaces that they can handle. Several works have tried to expand the Hotelling model in more Euclidean dimensions (\cite{michler2021differentiation, barop2021generalising, bosch2023hotelling}) but they all suffer from the same limitation: the existence of hard boundaries. These boundaries truly limit the model as several attributes of product differentiation are in fact a periodic continuum. The seminal work of \cite{salop1979circle} has paved the way to unbounded spaces, but, to the best of our knowledge no existing work has pushed forward in the direction of more complicated spaces.

Second, this lack of work on more complicated spaces may be explained by the unscalability of the mathematical formalism induced by Hotelling and that have been carried in almost every paper until today. For the sake of simplicity, Hotelling computes the demand by looking at the ``\textit{indifferent consumer}'', i.e. the point that delimits the influence regions of the two firms on the segment. While being a very efficient trick to fasten computations, it is highly unscalable: where is the indifferent consumer with more than two firms? in more dimensional spaces? Several works have attempted to push forward the model in more sophisticated settings, but were intrinsically limited by this formalism that needs to be entirely rewritten. This limitation has made several authors from \cite{jonas1968spatial, eaton1975principle} to \cite{ottino2017spatial} claim that the problem was ``\textit{analytically intractable}'', which will be proven excessive by the analytical developments of this paper that are allowed by our novel framework. 

Finally, due to this poor mathematical framework, works that tried to expand the model have been uncoordinated, and have sometimes overlapped. Several papers have for instance tried to increase the number of firms on the market (\cite{economides1993nfirms, anderson1994spatial, brenner2005nfirms}) but their efforts have not been used by the community that tried to expand the possible spaces. This led to the emergence of a wide collection of slightly altered frameworks of the standard Hotelling model (or of the Salop circle), which cannot be plugged together. 

Therefore, our objective in this paper is plural. We develop a new general framework of horizontal differentiation (models said to be \textit{a la Hotelling}) that can encapsulates most of the later developments to unify them. Using this general framework, we push forward the model with new spaces (non-Euclidean), a general number of firms, and the possibility to conduct agent-based simulations. We also remove the perfect rationality assumption from the Hotelling model. If we want to be able to conduct micro-based simulations, we aim to demonstrate that it is false to claim that analytical results cannot be obtained in more general spaces due to their intrinsic complexity. Therefore, this paper will only rely on simulations to \textit{illustrate} theoretical conclusions.

Despite being a continuation of existing models of horizontal differentiation, this work is not incremental. Our objective is to demonstrate the need for new mathematical tools in Economics models: we aim to illustrate that Riemannian geometry and stochastic geometry should now be standard tools in the Economics toolkit for modeling problems that involve spaces. Physics has long left the Euclidean paradigm, and to the best of our knowledge, Economics is lagging behind. We have found attempts to use differential geometry only in Econometrics (\cite{marriott2000applications, andrews2012geometric}) as a tool of information theory, or to better understand and model utility and demand functions (\cite{russell1993geometric, chiappori1999aggregation, beggs2021demand, haroutunian2024differential}). Overall, it is safe to say that differential geometry has still to be proven relevant for Economics models, and this paper aims to demonstrate this.

Our model can be considered as a Hotelling-like model. We will assume that consumers are distributed uniformly on a Riemannian manifold. Each firm has an influence area where consumers demand its product. Each consumer is assigned to the nearby firm, with proximity being determined by the inner distance of the manifold and the price (a higher price makes the firms more costly for the consumers). We say that we are in continuation of Hotelling as consumers are fully passive, and cannot move. We will show that the topology of the manifold will determine the differentiation outcome, with the irrationality degree of the consumers.

As we aim to conceive a model that encapsulates the findings of a collection made of dozens of papers spread across almost 100 years, it will be impossible for us to delve into every details and implications of our framework. Therefore, we restrict this study to two main points: we will give a rigorous presentation of our framework and of our model, and we will study the concentrated equilibrium (i.e. the situation without differentiation). This choice has been made as the \textit{principle of minimum differentiation} is the motivating conclusion of Hotelling, and every other market configuration have been discovered or analyzed by contrast with this original setting. Therefore, we will leave to future research works the study of other equilibrium classes. 

This paper is organized as follow. In \textbf{Section 2}, we describe our model, our assumptions, and the mathematical tools that we will use. In \textbf{Section 3}, we prove that our model is able to recover the results of previous models that it encapsulates. In \textbf{Section 4}, we study specifically the concentrated equilibrium and compute conditions for it to be possible. In \textbf{Section 5}, we test our conditions for existence and reachability on several manifolds, and use the versatility of our framework to confirm existing results, and to push forward in higher dimensional or non-euclidean spaces.  

    \section{Problem statement}
    \subsection{Model description}

    We first consider $N$ firms, randomly distributed on a smooth manifold $\mathcal{M} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^D$ (a space, possibly curved and periodic, that locally resembles Euclidean space and allows for calculus to be done smoothly across its surface). Each firm is assumed to be perfectly rational, and aims to maximize its profit by deciding its price $p_i$ and its location $y_i$ on the manifold. We singularize with the Hotelling model and with many later developments by considering a one-stage game: firms decide their positions and prices at the same time for the sake of realism. We provide in Figure \ref{fig:ourFramework} a summary of how our framework complements the existing literature. 

    \begin{figure}[h]
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.7\linewidth]{img/FrameworkInLitt.png}
        \caption{Our framework in the Economics literature. }
        \subcaption*{This diagram is not exhaustive, and does not show that our game is a one stage-game contrary to most of the mentioned models.\nocite{matsushima2001cournot, gupta2004locate, tsai2005spatial, anderson1988equilibrium}}
        \label{fig:ourFramework}
    \end{figure}

    The manifold (the space where firms and consumers live) is assumed to be compact and path connected. This does not mean that it has a boundary (the unit circle is a manifold), but it means intuitively that given two points there exists a path between them, and that path is of finite length.

    On this manifold live consumers that are uniformly distributed following a probability density function $\pi$ on $\mathcal{M}$. This hypothesis has no consequence on the analysis as every flat manifold with density can be converted to a curved manifold with uniform density as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:densityvscurvature}.

