              \draw[->] (4.8, 0) -- ++(3, 0) node[midway, below, yshift=-0.5em]
                  {Parameter space $\mathbb{R}^d$};

              \draw[thin, fill=ensblue!30, draw=none, shift={(5.8, 0.5)}, rotate=20]
                (0, 0) -- (1.2, 0) -- (1.2, 1.2) -- (0, 1.2) -- cycle node[midway, xshift=16pt, yshift=6pt] {$M$};

              \draw[thick,->,black] (1.8, 1.5) ++(4.2, 0.2) to [out=150, in=55]
                node[midway, below, xshift=2pt, yshift=-2pt] {$\mathfrak{M}$} ++(-3.6, -0.5);

            \end{tikzpicture}
        \caption{Schematic of the relationships between $\mathcal{M}$, M, $\mathbb{R}^d$, $\mathbb{R}^n$ and $\mathfrak{M}$.}
        \subcaption*{Consumers and firms live in the Ambient space, but to further simplify our computations, we will work on the Parameter space.}
        \label{fig:manifold_schematic}
    \end{figure}

    $\mathcal{M}$ is a $d$-dimensional manifold in $\mathbb{R}^n$. We equip $\mathcal{M}$ with a smooth immersion (i.e. $\mathcal{C}^\infty$-immersion) $\mathfrak{M} : M\subseteq \mathbb{R}^d \longrightarrow \mathbb{R}^n$ such that $\mathcal{M} = \mathfrak{M}(M)$. A smooth immersion is a differentiable map between manifolds with an injective derivative at every point, allowing the source to be smoothly placed into the target space, possibly with self-intersections. According to the Whitney immersion theorem, any $d$-dimensional manifold can be immersed in $\mathbb{R}^{2d-1}$, ensuring the existence of a smooth immersion $\mathfrak{M}$. We say that $M$ is the parametric space of $\mathcal{M}$.

    Economically, the manifold $\mathcal{M}$ represents our economic space This can be as usual a map but it can also be a representation of political mindsets or some features space for a product. While this space exists within a higher-dimensional Euclidean space $\mathbb{R}^n$ (meaning each position can be described by $n$ real numbers), it only has $d$ true degrees of freedom. Think of $d$ as the essential number of independent variables or parameters needed to fully describe any state within $\mathcal{M}$. The \textit{smooth immersion} $\mathfrak{M}$ acts as a blueprint or a mapping. It takes points from a simpler, $d$-dimensional parametric space $M$ (which is just a subset of $\mathbb{R}^d$) and smoothly transforms them into the more complex, $n$-dimensional economic space $\mathcal{M}$. This $\mathfrak{M}$ ensures that the local structure of $M$ is preserved in $\mathcal{M}$, meaning small changes in our $d$ essential parameters lead to smooth and predictable changes in the economic state. The \textit{Whitney immersion theorem} is a crucial guarantee here: it tells us that such a smooth mapping is always possible, at least if we allow our economic space to live in a sufficiently high-dimensional ambient space ($\mathbb{R}^{2d-1}$). 

    This embedding induces a Riemannian metric on $\mathcal{M}$, which matrix in local coordinates $(x_1, \cdots, x_d) \in M$ is:
    \[
    g_\mathcal{M} = \left[\left<\frac{\partial \mathfrak{M}}{\partial x_i}, \frac{\partial \mathfrak{M}}{\partial x_j}\right>_{\mathbb{R}^n}\right]_{i,j=1}^d = (D\mathfrak{M})^T(D\mathfrak{M})
    \]

    This embedding also defines $\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}$ the volume form on $\mathcal{M}$, that can be pulled back to $M$ using $\mathfrak{M}$ ($\wedge$ being the wedge product):
    \[
    \mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x_1, \cdots, x_d) = \sqrt{\det(g_\mathcal{M})} dx_1\wedge\cdots \wedge dx_d
    \]

    Intuitively, just as we calculate area or volume in $\mathbb{R}^n$, the volume form allows us to measure the size or measure of regions within $\mathcal{M}$. The volume form is derived directly from the Riemannian metric using the determinant of the metric matrix, $\det(g_\mathcal{M})$: intuitively, it tells us how infinitesimal volumes in the parametric space $M$ are scaled when mapped into the economic space $\mathcal{M}$. This is crucial for integration over $\mathcal{M}$, allowing us to compute the consumer mass over regions of our space, even when that space is curved.

    The tangent space to $\mathcal{M}$ at the point $Y = \mathfrak{M}(y)$ is the linear subspace of $\mathbb{R}^n$ defined by:
    \[
    T_Y \mathcal{M} := \mathrm{Im}\big( D\mathfrak{M}(y) \big) = \left\{ D\mathfrak{M}(y)[v] : v \in \mathbb{R}^d \right\},
    \]
    where $D\mathfrak{M}(y) : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}^n$ is the Jacobian of \(\mathfrak{M}\) evaluated at \( y \). We also define $P_{Y} : \mathbb{R}^n \longrightarrow T_{Y}\mathcal{M}$ the projection operator onto the tangent space at point $Y$:
    \[
    P_y(v) = (D\mathfrak{M}(y))((D\mathfrak{M}(y))^T(D\mathfrak{M}(y)))^{-1}(D\mathfrak{M}(y))^Tv
    \]

    We also recall the usual expression of the Riemannian distance $d_\mathcal{M}$ on the manifold:
    \[
    d_\mathcal{M}(X,Y) = \begin{cases}
        \inf_\gamma \int_0^1 \sqrt{g_\mathcal{M} (\dot\gamma(t), \dot\gamma(t))}dt\\
        \text{s.t.} \quad \gamma\in\mathcal{C}^1, \quad \gamma(0) = X, \quad \gamma(1) = Y
    \end{cases}
    \]
    with $\dot\gamma(t)$ the tangent vector of the curve $\gamma$ at point $t$. This computes us the length of the shortest path possible between two points while traveling on the manifold.

    Using this formalism, instead of optimizing directly on $\mathcal{M}$, we can rewrite our optimization problem on the parameter space using a standard change of variables: 

    \[
    \Lambda(V_i) = \int_\mathcal{M}f_i\ d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M} = \int_M  f_i(x)\sqrt{\det(g_\mathcal{M}(x))}dx
    \]

    For the sake of simplicity, we will consider that $f_i$ can be defined both using ambient and local coordinates. We also accept that $d_{M}(x,y) \triangleq d_{\mathcal{M}}(\mathfrak{M}(x),\mathfrak{M}(y))$ with $x,y\in M$, and will write $d_{\mathcal{M}}(x,y)$ for $d_{M}(x,y)$ . In the same spirit, we do not mention the differentiability conditions of the functions that we build on this distance. The interested reader will find in \textbf{Appendix A} the justification of their existence when needed.

    \subsection{An Iterative Process}

    One secondary objective of the introduction of this framework is to study the dynamics of the Hotelling game. As the problem involves two decision variables and a differentiation space, it seems to us that the static point of view cannot embrace the full complexity induced by the game. In reality, firms will likely need several attempts before reaching their optimal positions, and the process of reaching the optimum decision will likely be closer to a Walrasian auction rather than a ex-ante decision. Therefore, in addition to our static analysis, we introduce a dynamical one. We assume that firms start at a random price, and a random position on the manifold, and that they can move onto the two spaces.

    As firms are perfectly rational, we assume that they will move according to the gradient vector of their profit (their objective function). Consequently, we model this iterative process as a gradient ascent of the profit. We introduce learning rates that control the size of step at each time. 

    This iterative process is motivated by the analysis, but offers new possibilities for the study of complex manifolds that would otherwise be too difficult to consider. Throughout this paper, we will confirm our theoretical and static findings via simulations of this iterative process.

    We introduce here a gradient-ascent-like algorithm starting from a random position $(\tilde y_i, \tilde p_i)$ for each firm $i$. We compute an update rule using the gradient of $\Pi_i$:
    \begin{align*}
        \nabla_{p_i}\Pi_i &= \Lambda(V_i) + (p_i - c) \nabla_{p_i}\Lambda(V_i)\\
        &=  \frac{1}{\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}\int_{\mathcal{M}}  f_i(x)\big[1 - \beta (p_i-c)(1 - f_i(x))\big] d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\\
        \nabla_{y_i}\Pi_i &=  (p_i - c) \nabla_{y_i}\Lambda(V_i)\\
        &= \frac{-\beta(p_i - c)}{\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})} \int_{\mathcal{M}} f_i(x) (1 - f_i(x)) \cdot \nabla_{y_i} d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)
    \end{align*}

    To construct a gradient ascent, one must also make sure that the step will stay on the manifold. $R_x(\eta)$ is the retraction operator\footnote{We define the retraction operator as the mapping $R:\mathcal{M}\times T\mathcal{M} \longrightarrow \mathcal{M}$, $(x, \eta) \longmapsto R_x(\eta)$ such that $\forall x\in \mathcal{M}, R_x(0) = x$ and $\left.\frac{\partial}{\partial t} R_x(t\eta)\right\vert_{t=0} = \eta$.}. Intuitively, $R_x(\eta)$ is a way to move from point $x \in \mathcal{M}$ along a tangent vector $\eta \in T_x\mathcal{M}$, and land back on the manifold. It is useful in such optimization algorithms on manifolds, where we compute a search direction in the tangent space, then \textit{retract} back onto the manifold.

    Hence, according to the former gradient, the update rule of the tuple $(y_i, p_i)$ is:
    \begin{align}\label{eq_updateRule}
            (y_i^{t+1}, p_i^{t+1})^T &=  \begin{cases}
                \begin{aligned}
                R_{y_i^t} &\left( - \frac{\lambda_y \beta(p_i^t - c)}{\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})} \right. \\
                &\left. \cdot P_{y_i^t} \left( \int_{\mathcal{M}} f_i(x)(1 - f_i(x)) \cdot \nabla_{y_i} d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i^t) \, d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) \right) \right)
                \end{aligned}
                \\
                p_i^{t} + \frac{\lambda_p}{\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}\int_{\mathcal{M}}  f_i(x)(1 -\beta (p_i-c)(1 - f_i(x))) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\\
            \end{cases}
    \end{align}
    with $\lambda = (\lambda_y, \lambda_p)$ the learning rate.



\section{A sanity check on the well-known Hotelling model}
The first natural question one can ask about this formalism is how the well-known model of Hotelling on $\mathcal{M}=[0,1]$ with $N=2$ translates in our unified framework. We first study the usual case with linear cost, and extend our approach to higher order cost structures.

We can rewrite the objective ($\mathcal{P}$) of our firm as:
\begin{equation*}
        (\mathcal P) \quad \begin{cases}
            \underset{p_i, y_i}{\text{argmax}}\ \Pi_i = (p_i - c)\Lambda(V_i) = (p_i - c)\int_{0}^{1} f_i(x)dx\\
            \text{s.t.} \quad y_i \in [0,1]
        \end{cases}
\end{equation*}

We can derive the associated Lagrangian:
\[
\mathcal{L}(\mathbf{p}, \mathbf{y}, \lambda) = (p_i - c)\Lambda(V_i) - \lambda_1y_i -\lambda_2(1-y_i)
\]

We can write the First Order Conditions (FOC): 
\begin{align}
   {\label{linear_city_1foc}}&\int_0^1f_i(x)(1-\beta(p_i-c)(1-f_i(x))dx = 0\\
   {}&-\beta(p_i-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)(1-f_i(x))\nabla_{y_i}d_\mathcal{M}(x,y_i) dx -\lambda_1 + \lambda_2= 0\\
   {}& \lambda_1 y_i = 0 \quad \lambda_1 \geq 0 \quad  y_i\geq0\\
   {}& \lambda_2 (1-y_i) = 0 \quad \lambda_2 \geq 0 \quad  1- y_i\geq0
\end{align}

As usual in Economics, as firms are identical, we know that at equilibrium we must have $p_i = p_j$. Using this result, and $f_i = 1-f_j$, we can rewrite (eq. \ref{linear_city_1foc}):
\begin{align}
    {\label{linear_city_pi-c}}&\int_0^1f_i(x)(1-\beta(p_i-c)(1-f_i(x))dx = 0 \Leftrightarrow p_i-c = \frac{\int_0^1f_i(x)dx }{ \beta \int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)dx }\\
    {\label{linear_city_intfi}}&p_i = p_j \Rightarrow \left[\int_0^1f_i(x)dx = \int_0^1f_j(x)dx \Leftrightarrow y_i + y_j = 1\right] \Rightarrow \int_0^1 f_i(x)dx = \frac{1}{2}
\end{align}

As we want to study the equilibrium, we will unite the FOC of firms $i$ and $j$ in a single system:
\begin{align}
   {}&p_i = p_j \quad y_j = 1 - y_i\\
   {\label{linear_city_2afoc}}&\beta(p_i-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)\nabla_{y_i}d_\mathcal{M}(x,y_i) dx +\lambda_1 - \lambda_2= 0\\
   {\label{linear_city_2bfoc}}&\beta(p_j-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)\nabla_{y_j}d_\mathcal{M}(x,y_j) dx + \lambda_2 - \lambda_1= 0\\
   {}& \lambda_1 y_i = 0 \quad \lambda_1 \geq 0 \quad  y_i\geq0\\
   {}& \lambda_2 (1-y_i) = 0 \quad \lambda_2 \geq 0 \quad  1- y_i\geq0
\end{align}

\subsection{Linear cost}
With linear costs, we have $d_\mathcal{M}(x,y) = \vert y-x\vert$ and consider $\nabla_yd_\mathcal{M}(x,y) = \sign(y-x)$ and $\nabla_y^2d_\mathcal{M}(x,y) = 2\delta(y-x)-1$ (taking the distributional derivative approach, $\delta$ being the Dirac distribution). 

Using (eq. \ref{linear_city_2afoc}) and (eq. \ref{linear_city_2bfoc}):
\begin{align*}
\beta(p-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)(\sign(y_i-x)- \sign(y_i- (1 -x))) dx =0\Rightarrow y_i \in \left\{0, \frac{1}{2}, 1\right\}
\end{align*}

By construction of $[0,1]$, $y_i$ can be binded only by one constraint at the time. Without loss of generality, we assume $y_i = 0$ (which is symmetric with the case $y_i = 1$), and will study the case $y_i = \frac{1}{2}$ after.

Therefore, we can use the strict positivity constraint on $\lambda_1$ (as we have assumed that the associated constraint does bind):
\[
\begin{cases}
    \beta(p_i-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)\sign(-x) dx +\lambda_1 = 0\\
\beta(p_j-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)\sign(1-x) dx - \lambda_1= 0\\
\lambda_1 > 0 \quad  y_i=0\\
\end{cases}
\Leftrightarrow \beta(p_i-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x) dx >0
\]

Using (eq. \ref{linear_city_pi-c}) and (eq. \ref{linear_city_intfi}), we have:
\[
\underbrace{\beta(p_i-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x) dx}_{\nabla_{y_i}\Pi_i\vert_{y_i=0}}= \frac{1}{2} > 0
\]

The only remaining candidate is $y_i=y_j=\frac{1}{2}$. As it is an interior point, we have $\lambda_1 = \lambda_2 = 0$. The system collapses into this single condition:
\[
\beta(p-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)^2\sign(\frac{1}{2}-x) dx=0
\]

Which is always true. We therefore have two possible solutions.

For the sake of simplicity, we leave to the interested reader to check that both are local maxima of the profit. We highlight that $y_i=0$ cannot be a Nash equilibrium as $\nabla_{y_i}\Pi_i\vert_{y_i=0}>0$ which means that there exists a profitable deviation for the firm $i$ to move toward the center of the line. Our generalized model finds back the usual Hotelling results. 

\subsection{Higher order cost with imperfect rationality}

We use $d_\mathcal{M}(x,y) = \vert y-x\vert^\alpha$, with $\alpha > 1$:
\[
\nabla_yd_\mathcal{M}(x,y) = \alpha \sign(y-x)\vert y-x\vert^{\alpha-1} \quad
\nabla_y^2d_\mathcal{M}(x,y) = (\alpha - 1)\vert y-x\vert^{\alpha-2}
\]

The first order conditions rewrite in this setting as:
\begin{align}
   {}&p_i = p_j \quad y_j = 1 - y_i\\
   {\label{linear_city_2cfoc}}&\beta(p_i-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)\alpha \ \sign(y_i-x)\vert y_i-x\vert^{\alpha-1} dx +\lambda_1 - \lambda_2= 0\\
   {\label{linear_city_2dfoc}}&
   \begin{aligned}
   \beta(p_j-c)&\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)\alpha\sign(y_i-(1-x))\vert y_i-(1-x)\vert^{\alpha-1} dx\\ &+ \lambda_1 -\lambda_2 = 0
   \end{aligned}\\
   {}& \lambda_1 y_i = 0 \quad \lambda_1 \geq 0 \quad  y_i\geq0\\
   {}& \lambda_2 (1-y_i) = 0 \quad \lambda_2 \geq 0 \quad  1- y_i\geq0
\end{align}

If we assume that no constraint is binding $\lambda_1 = \lambda_2 = 0$, equations \ref{linear_city_2cfoc} and \ref{linear_city_2dfoc} give:
\[
\begin{cases}
    \beta(p_i-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)\alpha \ \sign(y_i-x)\vert y_i-x\vert^{\alpha-1} dx = 0\\
   \beta(p_j-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)\alpha \ \sign(y_i-(1-x))\vert y_i-(1-x)\vert^{\alpha-1} dx= 0
\end{cases}
\Leftrightarrow y_i = \frac{1}{2}
\]

This result is important as (eq. \ref{linear_city_2cfoc}) is $\nabla_{y_i} \Pi_i$, therefore, we know that if there is a boundary candidate, the lagrange multiplier will have to be non-null by the KKT conditions as $\nabla_{y_i} \Pi_i$ is non-null on the boundary. This result relies on the assumption that $p_i \neq c$, which is always the case for finite $\beta$.

We can rewrite equations \ref{linear_city_2cfoc} and \ref{linear_city_2dfoc} and obtain that the concentrated equilibrium is the only one possible in this case:
\begin{align*}
    \alpha\beta(p-c)\int_0^1 &f_i(x)f_j(x) \left( \sign(y_i-x)\vert y_i-x\vert^{\alpha-1} \right.\\
    &\left.- \sign(y_i-(1-x))\vert y_i-(1-x)\vert^{\alpha-1} \right)dx= 0 \underset{\beta \in \mathbb{R}}{\Leftrightarrow} y_i = \frac{1}{2}
\end{align*}

This result is of economic interest: the imperfect rationality of consumers makes boundary solutions no longer worthy for firms as they have an incentive to stay at the center to capture indecisive consumers.

\subsection{Higher order cost with perfect rationality}
If we assume $\beta \to +\infty$, we can compute the optimal price. First, we study the boundary of the two Voronoi cells (often called indifferent consumer in the Economics literature). $f_if_j$ concentrates mass at point where $\vert y_i-x\vert^\alpha = \vert 1-y_i-x \vert^\alpha$, i.e. at $x=\frac{1}{2}$. Outside of a small neighborhood centered at this point, $f_i(x)f_j(x)$ is null almost everywhere when $\beta \to + \infty$ as $f_i$ converges to a dirac mass function.

We zoom on this region and perform a change of variables. We first rewrite $f_i$ as:
\[
f_i(x) = \frac{\exp(-\beta \vert y_i-x\vert^\alpha)}{\exp(-\beta \vert y_i-x\vert^\alpha) + \exp(-\beta \vert y_y-x\vert^\alpha)} = \frac{1}{1 + \exp(\beta (\vert 1- y_i-x\vert^\alpha - \vert y_i-x\vert^\alpha))}
\]

We define:
\[
\Delta(x) = \vert 1- y_i-x\vert^\alpha - \vert y_i-x\vert^\alpha, 
\qquad \Delta'\left(\frac{1}{2}\right) = 2\alpha \  \sign\left(y_i - \frac{1}{2}\right)\left\vert y_i - \frac{1}{2}\right\vert^{\alpha-1}
\]

We can therefore linearize $\Delta(x)$ around $\frac{1}{2}$: $\Delta(x) \approx \Delta'(x) (x-\frac{1}{2}) = u$ and name it $u$ for our change of variables:
\begin{align*}
&\int_0^1 f_i(x)f_j(x)dx = \int_0^1 \frac{e^{\beta\Delta(x)}}{(1+e^{\beta\Delta(x)})^2}dx \\
&\approx \int_{-\infty}^{+\infty}\frac{e^u}{(1 + e^u)^2} \cdot \frac{1}{\beta \Delta'(\frac{1}{2})}du = \frac{1}{2\alpha\beta\vert y_i -\frac{1}{2}\vert^{\alpha-1} }
\end{align*}

Using (eq. \ref{linear_city_pi-c}), we can conclude:
\[
p_i - c \underset{\beta\to \infty}{\longrightarrow} \frac{\frac{1}{2}}{\frac{\beta}{2\alpha\beta\vert y_i -\frac{1}{2}\vert^{\alpha-1}}} = \alpha\left\vert y_i -\frac{1}{2}\right \vert^{\alpha-1}
\]

This equation is perfectly consistent with existing knowledge on the Hotelling model: the more the costs are convex, the more firms have market power. The price increases with the distance to the center, indicating that the boundary positions can lead to a pseudo-monopoly for the firms. 

Getting back to the position equation (eq. \ref{linear_city_2cfoc}), we have:
\[
\beta(p_i-c)\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)\alpha \ \sign(y_i-x)\vert y_i-x\vert^{\alpha-1} dx \underset{\beta\to +\infty}{\longrightarrow} \frac{\alpha}{2}\sign\left(y_i-\frac{1}{2}\right)\left\vert y_i -\frac{1}{2}\right \vert^{\alpha-1}
\]

This condition is indeed verified only when $y_i=\frac{1}{2}$, confirming that the center of the line is the only interior candidate. Nonetheless, contrary to the case with $\beta$ real and to the case of linear costs, we have now:
\[
\nabla_{y_i}\Pi_i\vert_{y_i=0} = - \frac{\alpha}{2^\alpha}< 0 
\]

This means that the boundary equilibrium is stable as firms want to go even further away from each other (but are stopped by the boundary). We check the second-order condition: 
\[
\nabla^2_{p_i}\Pi_i = (p-c)\beta^2\underbrace{\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)(1-2f_i(x))dx}_{=0}-2\beta\int_0^1f_i(x)f_j(x)dx < 0
\]

Therefore the boundary equilibrium is indeed a local maximum, and we have that it is a global maximum as:
\[
\lim_{\beta\to +\infty} \Pi_i\vert_{y_i=0} = \frac{\alpha}{2^\alpha}> 0 = \lim_{\beta\to +\infty}\Pi_i\vert_{y_i=\frac{1}{2}}
\]

\section{Theoretical Study of the concentrated equilibrium}
\subsection{The median of a manifold}
Before delving into the details of our model, we will define the concepts of median of a manifold, and of concentrated equilibrium.
\begin{defi}[Median of a manifold]\label{def:median}
    The set of medians $\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}}$, which elements are denoted $\bar y$, of the manifold $\mathcal{M}$ is the set of points that verify:
    \[
    \bar y \in \underset{y\in \mathcal{M}}{\operatorname{argmin}} \int_\mathcal{M} d_\mathcal{M}(x,y)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)
    \]
\end{defi}

Assuming that $d_\mathcal{M}$ is differentiable, we have by the Fermat theorem that
\[
y \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}} \Rightarrow \nabla_y \int_\mathcal{M} d_\mathcal{M}(x,y)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) = 0 \Rightarrow \int_\mathcal{M} \nabla_y d_\mathcal{M}(x,y)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) = 0 
\]

\begin{defi}[Concentrated equilibrium]\label{def:concentrated_equilibrium}
    The equilibrium of the market is said to be \textit{concentrated} if all firms choose the same position $y$, \textit{i.e.} $y_i=y$ for all $i$. This situation is also known as \textit{minimum differentiation}.
\end{defi}

We will show that concentrated equilibrium can only occur at points that are in $\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}}$. As $\mathcal{M}$ is compact, and as $\int_\mathcal{M} d_\mathcal{M}(x,y)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)$ is continuous, $\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}}$ is not empty. Without making further assumptions on $\mathcal{M}$, we cannot prove for now that $\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}}$ is a singleton.

\subsection{Nash equilibrium}

In this section, we will give conditions for the concentrated equilibrium to be a Nash equilibrium, \textit{i.e.} a situation where no firm has a profitable deviation to its selected strategy. These conditions are in general not sufficient\footnote{Our simulations demonstrated that these conditions could actually be sufficient for Euclidean manifolds and finite $\beta$.} and may require a specific analysis which would differ for each manifold. Nonetheless, we believe our results to be insightful as they provide conditions for the concentrated equilibrium to be a local maxima of the profit functions of the firms. Moreover, this is especially relevant for our iterative analysis where firms cannot change dramatically their strategy at each time step.

We consider $N$ firms. As we are looking for an equilibrium where all firms are at the same point with the same price, we set $\forall i \leq N, p_i = \bar p, y_i = \bar y \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}}$. At this point, we have:
\begin{align*}
    \forall x \in \mathcal{M}, f_i(x) &= \frac{\exp(-\beta (d_\mathcal{M}(x, \bar y) +\bar p))}{\sum_{k=1}^N \exp(-\beta (d_\mathcal{M}(x, \bar y) +\bar p))}= \frac{1}{N}
\end{align*}

\[ 
\forall i \leq N, \Lambda(V_i) = \frac{1}{N \cdot \mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}\int_{\mathcal{M}} d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) = \frac{1}{N}
\]
We can now describe the equilibrium:
\begin{align*}
    \nabla \Pi_i(\mathbf{\bar y, \bar p}) = 0 &\Leftrightarrow \begin{cases}
                \frac{\beta(p_i - c)}{\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})} \int_{\mathcal{M}} f_i(x) (1 - f_i(x)) \cdot \nabla_{y} d_\mathcal{M}(x, \bar y) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) = 0\\
                \frac{1}{\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}\int_{\mathcal{M}}  f_i(x)(1 -\beta (\bar p-c)(1 - f_i(x))) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) = 0
            \end{cases}\\
    &\Leftrightarrow \begin{cases}
    \frac{\beta(\bar p - c)(N-1)}{N^2}\cdot\frac{1}{\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})} \int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y} d_\mathcal{M}(x, \bar y) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) = 0\\
                \frac{1}{N}\left(1 -\beta (\bar p-c)\left(\frac{N-1}{N}\right)\right) \cdot \frac{1}{\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}\int_{\mathcal{M}}   d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) = 0\\
    \end{cases}\\
    &\Leftrightarrow \begin{cases}
     \int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y} d_\mathcal{M}(x, \bar y) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) = 0\\
                \bar p-c = \frac{N}{\beta (N-1)} \\
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}

The first condition is satisfied if $\bar y \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}}$. This gives us our first theorem on every compact manifold:

\begin{thm}\label{thm:concentrated_needs_center}
    A concentrated equilibrium can only occur at a median of the manifold.
\end{thm}

The second condition complements the original model of Hotelling: if we are in a framework where all consumers are perfectly rational ($\beta \to +\infty$), we find back that $\bar p \to c$, but when consumers are irrational ($\beta \to 0$), firms can increase their prices and make positive profits. These two results are general and do not rely on any specific assumption on the topology of the manifold. 

Nonetheless, before going any further, we need to check that this critical point is indeed a (local) maximum of the profit function. We first compute the second derivatives and evaluate them at the point $(\mathbf{\bar y, \bar p})$:
\begin{align*}
    \nabla^2_{p_i} \Pi_i =&\frac{-\beta}{N}\quad \quad
    \nabla^2_{p_i, y_i} \Pi_i=0 \\
    \nabla^2_{ y_i} \Pi_i =& \frac{1}{N\cdot\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}\left(\frac{\beta(N-2)}{N}\int_{\mathcal{M}} \left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right)\otimes\left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\right.\\
    &\left.-\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\right)
\end{align*}


As $\nabla^2_{p_i, y_i} \Pi_i = 0$\footnote{We refer to \textbf{Appendix D} for detailed computations.}, the eigenvalues of the Hessian matrix are therefore $\nabla^2_{p_i} \Pi_i$ and $\nabla^2_{ y_i} \Pi_i$. We have $\nabla^2_{p_i} \Pi_i <0$ as $\beta >0 $ and $N\in \mathbb{N}$. The equilibrium can therefore be a Nash equilibrium only if:
\begin{align}\label{eq:eigenConcentratedCond}\begin{split}
\nabla^2_{ y_i} \Pi_i < 0\Leftrightarrow  \frac{\beta(N-2)}{N}&\int_{\mathcal{M}} \left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right)\otimes\left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\\
&<\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)
\end{split}\end{align}

This condition\footnote{For the sake of simplicity, we treat this expression as a scalar. For manifolds of inner dimension 1, it is true. For isotropic manifolds, both integrals will result in matrices proportionate to the identity: the inequality is therefore between the proportionality coefficients of these integrals. For non-isotropic manifolds, the inequality is to understand as the positive definiteness (or semi-definiteness) of the resulting matrix.} can be interpreted as \textit{at the center of the manifold, the average curvature of the Riemannian distance function dominates a scaled average squared sensitivity}. This condition is very informative. First, when $\beta \to +\infty$ and $N>2$, the left-hand side diverges to $+\infty$ requiring the right-hand side to do so which is clearly impossible. Therefore, this condition is only satisfied in this setting if the left integral is zero, \textit{i.e.} if $\bar y$ is the only point of $\mathcal{M}$ which is impossible.


\begin{thm}\label{thm:no_concentrated_rational_oligopolies}
    For any manifold of our requirements and $N>2$, there exists a rationality threshold ($\bar \beta$) from which the concentrated point cannot be an equilibrium ($\beta > \bar \beta$). Therefore, concentration is never possible with $N>2$ when consumers are perfectly rational ($\beta \to +\infty$).
\end{thm}

This theorem is very interesting for market analysis: no matter the space of differentiation, if firms are not in a duopoly and if consumers are fully rational, they will choose to differentiate their product, no matter what differentiation might actually be.

As $N\geq2$, the left-handside of (eq. \ref{eq:eigenConcentratedCond}) is positive, therefore, a necessary condition for (eq. \ref{eq:eigenConcentratedCond}) to be verified is:
\[
\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) >0
\]

We prove that this can be true only for manifolds with boundaries. A proof of this theorem can be found in the Appendix \ref{proof:concentrated_noboundary}, and we illustrate this result on a the surface of a Doughnut shape in Figure \ref{fig:voronoiDoughnut}.

\begin{thm}\label{thm:concentrated_noboundary}
    The concentrated equilibrium is only feasible on manifolds that do have a non-empty boundary.
\end{thm}

\begin{figure}[h]
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{img/doughnut.png}
        \caption{Voronoi Diagram of the market on an unbounded compact manifold (a doughnut-like surface). }
        \subcaption*{$\beta =20, c=0.2, N=4$. As expected, firms cannot select the concentrated equilibrium.}
        \label{fig:voronoiDoughnut}
    \end{figure}


Another consequence of the later equation is on the uniqueness of the median of the manifold. As it is assumed to be an interior point, the equation can be seen as the second order condition for the global convexity of the optimization problem that defined the median of the manifold.

\begin{thm}\label{thm:concentrated_uniquemedian}
    The concentrated equilibrium cannot exist if the median of the manifold is not unique. 
\end{thm}

In addition to these general results, we introduce a more specific proposition. We define the general cylinder as the Riemannian product manifold $\mathcal{M} = M_1 \times T^k$, where $M_1$ is a homogeneous bounded manifold and $T^k = S^1(R_1) \times \dots \times S^1(R_k)$ is a homogeneous $k$-torus. Our objective here is to investigate the equilibrium on spaces that have bounded directions, and unbounded ones (\textit{i.e.} periodic ones). We obtain the following proposition, for which we provide a mathematical proof in \textbf{Appendix \ref{proof:separability_cylinder}}, and a use case in \textbf{Section \ref{sec:results}}:

\begin{prop}\label{prop:separability_cylinder}
    On a general cylinder, the firms’ optimization problem is separable. Therefore, the equilibrium in the bounded coordinates is the same as if no other coordinates existed, and likewise for the periodic coordinates.
\end{prop}


\subsection{Is the Nash equilibrium reachable?}
Even if we assume that the concentrated equilibrium is a Nash equilibrium (and the better Nash equilibrium available), this market configuration has no guarantee to occur in an iterative model. We will now derive a condition on $\beta$ for the model to converge toward such an equilibrium.

Getting back to the dynamical system (\ref{eq_updateRule}), we first need to deal with the issue of the geometric stability. Indeed, we are projecting the update step on the manifold, and this can induce instability. For small steps, as the retractation operator first-order stable, this shall not be of any concern of us\footnote{If we denote $C$ the injectivity radius of the exponential map of the manifold, a safe ``small step'' for the manifold would be: 
\[
\lambda_{y} \leq C \left(\max_{\mathbf{y,p}}\left\Vert- \frac{\lambda_y \beta(p_i - c)}{\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})} \cdot P_{y_i} \left( \int_{\mathcal{M}} f_i(x)(1 - f_i(x)) \cdot \nabla_{y_i} d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i) \, d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) \right)\right\Vert_\mathcal{M}\right)^{-1}
\]
}.

Therefore, we will analyze the system as if it were Euclidean. We know that the system will locally converge to an equilibrium if the Jacobian of the system ($I_{2N} + \lambda\nabla^2\Pi$) in the neighborhood of the equilibrium has eigenvalues which modulus are less than 1. The first condition for the equilibrium is to be a fixed point of the system, which it is as the gradient at this point is null:

\begin{align*}
X^{(t+1)} = X^{(t)} + \lambda\nabla\Pi &\Leftrightarrow \mathbf{D}X = I_{2N} + \lambda\nabla^2\Pi \numberthis \label{eq_linearJacobian}\\
&\Leftrightarrow
\mathbf{D}X = I_{2N} + \lambda\begin{bmatrix}
    \nabla^2_{ y_i} \Pi_i & \nabla^2_{p_i, y_i} \Pi_i  & \nabla^2_{ y_j,y_i} \Pi_i & \nabla^2_{ p_j,y_i} \Pi_i & \cdots\\
    \nabla^2_{ y_i,p_i} \Pi_i & \nabla^2_{p_i} \Pi_i  & \nabla^2_{y_j,p_i} \Pi_i & \nabla^2_{p_j,p_i} \Pi_i & \cdots\\
    \nabla^2_{y_i, y_j} \Pi_j & \nabla^2_{p_j, y_j} \Pi_j & \nabla^2_{ y_j} \Pi_j & \nabla^2_{ p_j,y_j} \Pi_j  & \cdots\\
    \nabla^2_{y_i,p_j} \Pi_j & \nabla^2_{p_i,p_j} \Pi_j & \nabla^2_{y_j,p_j} \Pi_j & \nabla^2_{p_j} \Pi_j  & \cdots\\
    \vdots & \vdots & \vdots &\vdots & \ddots
    \end{bmatrix} 
\end{align*}

Let's now consider the second condition on the eigenvalues. We first write $\nabla^2\Pi$ at the point $(\mathbf{\bar y, \bar p})$:
\begin{align*}
    &\left. \nabla^2\Pi \right\vert_{(\mathbf{\bar y, \bar p})} = \begin{bmatrix}
    \nabla^2_{ y_i} \Pi_i & 0  & \nabla^2_{ y_j,y_i} \Pi_i & 0 & \cdots\\
    0 & \nabla^2_{p_i} \Pi_i  & 0 & \nabla^2_{p_j,p_i} \Pi_i & \cdots\\
    \nabla^2_{y_i, y_j} \Pi_j & 0 & \nabla^2_{y_j} \Pi_j & 0  & \cdots\\
    0 & \nabla^2_{p_i,p_j} \Pi_j & 0 & \nabla^2_{p_j} \Pi_j  & \cdots\\
    \vdots & \vdots & \vdots &\vdots & \ddots
    \end{bmatrix}
    \\&\Rightarrow \text{det}\left(\left. \nabla^2\Pi \right\vert_{(\mathbf{\bar y, \bar p})}\right) = \begin{vmatrix}
    \nabla^2_{y_1} \Pi_1 &\cdots  & \nabla^2_{y_N, y_1} \Pi_1 & 0 & \cdots & 0 \\
    \vdots & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
    \nabla^2_{y_1, y_N} \Pi_N & \cdots & \nabla^2_{y_N} \Pi_N & 0& \cdots& 0  \\
    0 & \cdots &0 & \nabla^2_{p_1} \Pi_1  & \cdots &\nabla^2_{p_N, p_1} \Pi_1 \\
    \vdots & \ddots & \vdots & \vdots & \ddots & \vdots \\
    0 & \cdots & 0 & \nabla^2_{p_1, p_N} \Pi_N  & \cdots & \nabla^2_{p_N} \Pi_N  \\
    \end{vmatrix}\\
    &\Rightarrow \text{det}\left(\left. \nabla^2\Pi \right\vert_{(\mathbf{\bar y, \bar p})}\right) = \begin{vmatrix}
    \nabla^2\Pi^{(y)} & 0\\
    0 & \nabla^2\Pi^{(p)}
    \end{vmatrix}
\end{align*}

This block-matrix expression allows us to know that $\text{Sp}(\nabla^2\Pi) = \text{Sp}(\nabla^2\Pi^{(y)}) \cup \text{Sp}(\nabla^2\Pi^{(p)})$. Each of these matrices can be written in the form $M = aI_N + b(\mathbf{1}_N-I_n)$ (with $a,b\in \mathbb{R}$):
\begin{align*}
    \left(\nabla^2\Pi^{(y)}\right)_{(i,j)} &= \frac{1}{N\cdot\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}\begin{cases}
        \begin{aligned}
        &\frac{\beta(N-2)}{N}\int_{\mathcal{M}} \left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right)\otimes\left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\\ &\qquad-\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x),
        \end{aligned} &i=j\\
        \frac{-\beta (N-2)}{N (N-1)} \int_{\mathcal{M}} (\nabla_{y_i} d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i))^2 d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)  &i\neq j
    \end{cases} \\
    %&= \left(\frac{\beta(N-2)}{N}-2)\right)I_N -\frac{\beta(N-2)}{N(N-1)}(\mathbf{1}_N-I_n)\\
     \left(\nabla^2\Pi^{(y)}\right)_{(i,j)} &= \frac{\beta}{N}\begin{cases}
        -1, &i=j\\
        \frac{1}{N(N-1)}, &i\neq j
    \end{cases} 
        = \frac{\beta}{N}\left(- I_N -\frac{1}{N(N-1)}(\mathbf{1}_N-I_n)\right)
\end{align*}

Denoting $\mu^{(y)}_1, \mu^{(y)}_2$ and $\mu^{(p)}_1, \mu^{(p)}_2$ the eigenvalues of $\nabla^2\Pi^{(y)}$ and $\nabla^2\Pi^{(p)}$ respectively, we have:
\begin{align*}
    \mu^{(y)}_1 =& \frac{1}{N\cdot\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}\left(\frac{\beta(N-2)}{N}\int_{\mathcal{M}} \left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right)\otimes\left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) \right. \\
    &\left.-\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) + \frac{\beta (N-2)}{N (N-1)} \int_{\mathcal{M}} (\nabla_{y_i} d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i))^2 d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) \right)\\
    =& \frac{1}{N\cdot\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}\left(\frac{\beta(N-2)}{N-1}\int_{\mathcal{M}} \left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right)\otimes\left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\right.\\
    &\left. - \int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\right) \\
    \mu^{(y)}_2 =& \frac{1}{N\cdot\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}\left(\frac{\beta(N-2)}{N}\int_{\mathcal{M}} \left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right)\otimes\left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) \right. \\
    &\left.-\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) - \frac{\beta (N-2)}{N} \int_{\mathcal{M}} (\nabla_{y_i} d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i))^2 d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\right)\\
    =& -\frac{1}{N\cdot\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\\
    \mu^{(p)}_1 =& \frac{\beta}{N}\left(-1-\frac{1}{N(N-1)}\right) = \frac{\beta(-N^2+N-1)}{N^2(N-1)} \\
    \mu^{(p)}_2 =& \frac{\beta}{N}\left(-1+\frac{N-1}{N(N-1)}\right) = \frac{-\beta(N-1)}{N^2}
\end{align*}

We need to check that the fourth eigenvalues of our Jacobian matrix are in the unit circle, i.e. that we have $\vert 1 + \lambda \mu_k\vert <1$. To verify this condition, we have to check whether $\mu_k < 0$, and if so, we get an upper bound $\lambda < \frac{2}{\vert \mu_k\vert}$ (for $\mu_k \neq 0$).

We start by the price hessian, and observe that the strict negativity condition is always met. We derive a convergence condition on the price learning rate $\lambda^{(p)}$.
\begin{align*}
    \begin{cases}
        N > 1\\
        \beta >0 
    \end{cases} &\Rightarrow
    \begin{cases}
        \frac{\beta(-N^2+N-1)}{N^2(N-1)} < 0 \\
        \frac{-\beta(N-1)}{N^2} <0
    \end{cases}
     \Rightarrow \begin{cases}
         \mu^{(p)}_1 <0\\
         \mu^{(p)}_2 < 0
    \end{cases}\\
    \begin{cases}
        N > 1\\
        \beta >0 
    \end{cases} &\Rightarrow
    \left\vert \frac{\beta(-N^2+N-1)}{N^2(N-1)} \right\vert > \left\vert\frac{-\beta(N-1)}{N^2}\right\vert\\
     &\Rightarrow \left[\begin{cases}\lambda^{(p)} < \frac{2}{\vert \mu^{(p)}_1 \vert}\\ \lambda^{(p)} < \frac{2}{\vert \mu^{(p)}_2 \vert}
     \end{cases} \Leftrightarrow \lambda^{(p)} < \frac{2N^2}{\beta(N-1)} \right]
\end{align*}

It is interesting to notice that the greater is beta, the smaller the learning rate should be. This indicates that higher rationality leads to numerical instability. This is something we observed in our simulations. 

\begin{prop}\label{prop:rationality_lr}
    The greater is the rationality of the consumer, the less should a firm change its differentiation strategy at a time, to reach its optimum situation in terms of profit.
\end{prop}

Similarly, we have for the position hessian that the Nash condition (\ref{eq:eigenConcentratedCond}) implies $\mu^{(y)}_1 < 0$. $\mu^{(y)}_2<0$ gives a condition on the topology of the manifold:
\begin{equation}
\mu^{(y)}_2<0 \Leftrightarrow \int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) >0
\end{equation}
which is redundant with (eq. \ref{eq:eigenConcentratedCond}) and therefore is always true. 

Enforcing $\lambda < \frac{2}{\vert \mu_k\vert}$, we get a condition on the learning rate:
\begin{equation*}
\vert \mu^{(y)}_1 \vert < \vert \mu^{(y)}_2 \vert \Rightarrow \frac{2}{\vert \mu^{(y)}_1 \vert}>\frac{2}{\vert \mu^{(y)}_2 \vert} > \lambda^{(y)} \Rightarrow \lambda^{(y)} < \frac{2N\cdot\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}{\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)}
\end{equation*}

To summarize, necessary conditions for the concentrated equilibrium to be a Nash equilibrium are:
\begin{align}
    & \bar y \in \mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}} \label{cond:median} \quad \text{card}(\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}}) = 1\\
    & \bar p= \frac{N}{\beta (N-1)} +c \label{cond:price} \\
    & \begin{aligned}
    0 &\leq \frac{\beta(N-2)}{N}\int_{\mathcal{M}} \left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right)\otimes\left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\\
    &<\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)
    \end{aligned} \label{cond:curvature}
\end{align}

Likewise, necessary conditions for it to be reachable are:
\begin{align}
    %& \text{card}(\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}}) = 1 \label{cond:uniqueness} \\
    %& \int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) >0 \label{cond:secDeriv} \\
    & \lambda^{(p)} < \frac{2N^2}{\beta(N-1)} \label{cond:lambda}
    & \lambda^{(y)} < \frac{2N\cdot\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}{\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)} 
\end{align}

\subsection{Is the Nash equilibrium socially optimal?}

If the concentrated equilibrium can be selected under certain conditions as it is the best situation for firms under these conditions, one can wonder the effect of such a choice on the society and on the global welfare.

First, we wonder whether the concentrated equilibrium can be a local maximum of the social welfare function:
\begin{align*}
    &\nabla\mathcal{O} = 0 \Leftrightarrow \begin{cases}
    \sum^N_{j=1}\int_\mathcal{M} (d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_j) + c)\nabla_{p_i}f_j(x)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)=0\\
    \sum^N_{j=1}\int_\mathcal{M} (\nabla_{y_i}d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_j)f_j(x)) + (d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_j) + c)\nabla_{y_i}f_j(x) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)=0\\
    \end{cases}\\
    &\Leftrightarrow \begin{cases}
        \begin{aligned}
        \beta\sum^N_{k=1, k\neq i}&\int_\mathcal{M}(d_\mathcal{M}(x,y_k) + c)f_i(x)f_k(x)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\\
        &-\beta \int_\mathcal{M}(d_\mathcal{M}(x,y_i) + c)f_i(x)(1-f_i(x))d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)=0
        \end{aligned}\\
        \begin{aligned}
        \sum^N_{k=1, k\neq i}&\beta\int_\mathcal{M}(d_\mathcal{M}(x,y_k) + c)f_i(x)f_k(x)\nabla_{y_i}d_\mathcal{M}(x,y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\\
        &+ \int_\mathcal{M}(1-\beta(d_\mathcal{M}(x,y_i) + c)(1-f_i(x)))f_i(x)\nabla_{y_i}d_\mathcal{M}(x,y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)=0
        \end{aligned}\\
    \end{cases}
\end{align*}

At the concentrated point, we have $f_i(x) = \frac{1}{N}$ and $y_i = \bar y$ for all $i$ and $x\in \mathcal{M}$. We can therefore evaluate $\nabla \mathcal{O}$ at this point:
\[
\nabla \mathcal{O}(\mathbf{\bar p}, \mathbf{\bar y}) =
\begin{cases}
        \underbrace{\beta\frac{(N-1)}{N^2}\left(\int_\mathcal{M}(d_\mathcal{M}(x,\bar y) + c)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)-\int_\mathcal{M}(d_\mathcal{M}(x,\bar y) + c)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\right)}_{=0}\\
         \beta\frac{(N-1)}{N^2}\int_\mathcal{M}\nabla_{\bar y}d_\mathcal{M}(x,\bar y)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)
\end{cases}
\]
Therefore, the concentrated equilibrium is a critical point of the social welfare function if and only if $\bar y \in \mathcal{S}_\mathcal{M}$, which is true by Theorem \ref{thm:concentrated_needs_center}.

Using the gradients at this point, we have that the concentrated equilibrium is a local maximum of the social welfare function if and only if:
\[
\int_\mathcal{M}\nabla_{\bar y}^2d_\mathcal{M}(x,\bar y)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) >0 
\]

This condition is guaranteed by Theorem \ref{thm:concentrated_uniquemedian}. Therefore, when it exists, the concentrated equilibrium is always a local maximum of the social welfare function.

Secondly, it is actually very easy to see that the concentrated equilibrium is not a global maximizer of the social welfare function. We have established that when all prices are equal, the problem can be reduced to finding the Voronoi tesselation where the radius of the cells is minimized. Placing all firms at the center cannot be optimal: if we assume that all firms share the same position, moving one firm will reduce the transport for at least one subset of consumers, which, as prices are equal, will be better-off in this scenario. Therefore, the concentrated equilibrium cannot be the global maximum of the welfare function. 

\section{Results on well-known manifolds}
\label{sec:results}
\subsection{The original Hotelling model on $\mathcal{M} =[0,1]$}
Returning to the standard Hotelling model on $\mathcal{M}=[0,1]$, we have that $\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})=1$. We use:
\[
d_\mathcal{M}(x,y) = \vert y-x\vert \quad \nabla_yd_\mathcal{M}(x,y) = \sign(y-x) \quad \nabla_y^2d_\mathcal{M}(x,y) = 2\delta(y-x)-1
\]

Therefore,
\[
\int_0^1\nabla_y^2d_\mathcal{M}(x,y)dx = 2>0 \qquad \int_0^1(\nabla_yd_\mathcal{M}(x,y))^2dx = 1>0
\]
which gives us unicity of the manifold center, and $\bar y = \frac{1}{2}$. 

We can therefore rewrite the conditions given in \cref{cond:curvature,cond:lambda,cond:median,cond:price}:
\begin{align}\label{eq:finalConcentratedOn01}
    \begin{split}
        &\bar y = \frac{1}{2}\quad \bar p= \frac{N}{\beta (N-1)} +c \\
        &\frac{\beta(N-2)}{N} <2 \Rightarrow \beta < \frac{2N}{N-2}\\
        &\lambda^{(p)} < \frac{2N^2}{\beta(N-1)}\\
        &\lambda^{(y)} < \frac{2N\cdot\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(\mathcal{M})}{\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)} = N
    \end{split}
\end{align}

Overall, we get back to the standard result of Hotelling, as $\beta \to +\infty \Rightarrow \bar p = c$, and $\bar y = \frac{1}{2}$, i.e. firms concentrate at the center for $N=2$.

  \begin{figure}[h]
        \centering
        \includegraphics[width=0.5\linewidth]{img/PhaseDiagramN2Concentration.png}
        \caption{Phase Diagram of the market structure outcome on $\mathcal{M} = [0,1]$. We empirically find the phase transition given by (eq. \ref{eq:finalConcentratedOn01}).}
        \label{fig:phaseLinear01}
    \end{figure}


These conditions are especially interesting regarding the behavior of the model with respect to the $\beta$ parameter. For $N=2$, all $\beta$ values will lead to a concentrated equilibrium, providing the learning rates are appropriate. For $N>2$, the concentrated equilibrium on the $[0,1]$ segment is only a Nash equilibrium if consumers are not perfectly rational. If one wants to fall back to previous models with perfect rationality, this therefore implies that the concentrated equilibrium is only possible if the market is a duopoly.

Using our iterative process, we simulate some market settings to verify (eq. \ref{eq:finalConcentratedOn01}) and find back the phase transition (Fig. \ref{fig:phaseLinear01}). Fig. \ref{fig:linearCity} gives examples of the market outcome and of the convergence process for $\beta = 1$ and $\beta = 100$ (and $N=3$).

Interestingly, we find with our simulations the same patterns of \cite{eaton1975principle} when the number of firms increases: for $N=5$ firms, the segment is split in 3 equal parts, firms are at the middle of the parts and 2 firms are at same centers for the outside splits of the segment. For the sake of conciseness, we do not include here the simulation dumps, but this has been observed in every simulation with $\beta$ higher than the phase transition for the concentrated equilibrium, meaning that their result was robust to limited-rationality of the consumer. 

