\subsection{Linear city with convex cost}

We use, with $\alpha > 1$:
\[
d_\mathcal{M}(x,y) = \vert y-x\vert^\alpha, \quad 
\nabla_yd_\mathcal{M}(x,y) = \alpha \ \sign(y-x)\vert y-x\vert^{\alpha-1}
\]
\[
\nabla_y^2d_\mathcal{M}(x,y) = (\alpha - 1)\vert y-x\vert^{\alpha-2}
\Rightarrow 
\int_0^1\nabla_y^2d_\mathcal{M}(x,y)dx >0
\]
which gives us unicity of the manifold center, and $\bar y = \frac{1}{2}$.

Condition \ref{cond:curvature} can be rewritten,
\begin{align*}
    & \frac{\beta(N-2)}{N} \int_0^1 \alpha^2 \left\vert \frac{1}{2} -x \right\vert^{2\alpha-2} dx< \int_0^1  \alpha (\alpha - 1) \left\vert \frac{1}{2}-x \right\vert^{\alpha-2} dx
    %& \Longleftrightarrow \frac{\beta(N-2)}{N} \alpha \int_0^{1/2} x^{2\alpha-2} dx < (\alpha - 1) \int_0^{1/2}  x^{\alpha-2} dx \\
    %& \Longleftrightarrow \frac{\beta(N-2)}{N} \frac{\alpha}{2\alpha - 1} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{2 \alpha - 1} < (\alpha - 1) \frac{1}{\alpha - 1} \left( \frac{1}{2} \right)^{\alpha - 1} \\
    %& \Longleftrightarrow \frac{\beta(N-2)}{N} \frac{\alpha}{2\alpha - 1} \frac{1}{2^\alpha} < 1\\
    \Leftrightarrow \beta < \frac{2^\alpha (2\alpha -1)N}{\alpha(N-2)}
\end{align*}

Considering the usual $\alpha = 2$ case, we therefore have:
\begin{align*}
&\frac{\beta(N-2)}{N}\int_{\mathcal{M}} \left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right)\otimes\left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x) <\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)\\
&\Leftrightarrow \beta < \frac{6N}{N-2}
\end{align*}

Again, we find back what we discovered in our first sanity check. With finite $\beta$, the concentrated equilibrium is possible with higher order transportation costs.

\subsection{The Salop circle on $\mathcal{M} = \mathbb{S}_1$}
On the unit-circle, each point is characterized by an angle $\theta \in [0,2\pi)$ (manifold inner coordinates) and the total volume is $\mathcal{V}_{\mathbb{S}_1}(\mathbb{S}_1) = 2 \pi$. The geodesic distance can be defined as $d_{\mathbb{S}_1} (x,y) = \min\{\vert \theta_x - \theta_y\vert, 2\pi -\vert \theta_x - \theta_y\vert \}$ and its partial derivative is:
\[
\nabla_{\theta_i} d_{\mathbb{S}_1} (x,y_i) =
\begin{cases} 
\operatorname{sign}(\theta_i - \theta_x), & |\theta_x - \theta_i| < \pi, \\
-\operatorname{sign}(\theta_i - \theta_x), & |\theta_x - \theta_i| > \pi
\end{cases} = \sign (\theta_i - \theta_x) \sign(\pi - |\theta_i - \theta_x|)
\]

We can rewrite (\ref{eq_updateRule}) in this setting:
    \begin{align*}
        \begin{split}
            &(\theta_i^{t+1}, p_i^{t+1})^T = (\theta_i^{t}, p_i^{t})^T + \lambda \nabla\Pi_i\vert_{(\theta_i^{t}, p_i^{t})}\\
            &= \begin{cases}
            \theta_i^{t+1} = \theta_i^t - \frac{\lambda_y \beta (p_i - c)}{2\pi} \int_{0}^{2\pi} f_i(\theta_x)(1 - f_i(\theta_x)) \, \sign (\theta_i - \theta_x) \sign(\pi - |\theta_i - \theta_x|)d\theta_x \\
            p_i^{t+1} = p_i^t + \frac{\lambda_p}{2\pi} \int_0^{2\pi} f_i(\theta_x)\left(1 - \beta (p_i - c)(1 - f_i(\theta_x))\right) \, d\theta_x
            \end{cases}
        \end{split}
    \end{align*}

First, $\text{card}(\mathcal{S}_{\mathcal{M}}) = +\infty$ by definition of the circle, which breaks the unicity condition of the median of the manifold. Moreover for the characterization of the Nash equilibrium, the condition \ref{cond:median} and \ref{cond:price} are fulfilled, but the \ref{cond:curvature} is not. Indeed, following the definition of $\nabla_{\theta_i}d_{\mathbb{S}_1} (x,\theta_i)$, we have:
\[
\int_{\mathbb{S}_1} \left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathbb{S}_1}(x, y_i)\right)\otimes\left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathbb{S}_1}(x, y_i)\right)dx = \int_{\mathbb{S}_1} (\sign (\theta_i - \theta_x) \sign(\pi - |\theta_i - \theta_x|))^2dx = 2\pi
\]

Taking the second distributional derivative, we have:
\[
\nabla^2_y  d_{\mathbb{S}_1} (x,y) = 2\delta(\theta_x - \theta_i) - 2\delta(\vert\theta_x - \theta_i\vert - \pi) \Rightarrow \int_{\mathbb{S}_1} \nabla^2_y  d_{\mathbb{S}_1} (x,y)dx = 0
\]

Which breaks a Nash equilibrium condition:
\begin{align*}
&\frac{\beta(N-2)}{N}\underbrace{\int_{\mathcal{M}} \left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right)\otimes\left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right) d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)}_{=2\pi} <\underbrace{\int_{\mathcal{M}} \nabla_{y_i}^2 d_\mathcal{M}(x, y_i)d\mathcal{V}_\mathcal{M}(x)}_{=0}\\
&\Leftrightarrow \frac{2\pi \beta(N-2)}{N} < 0
\end{align*}

This result is perfectly consistent with what we proved in the previous sections: on boundaryless manifolds, the concentrated equilibrium can never occur.

Using simulations and an agent-based model on the iterative model, we find that even with a very low value of $\beta=1$, the market converges toward a perfectly uniform distribution of firm positions (Fig. \ref{fig:salopCircle}). 

