\subsection{The hypercube $\mathcal{M} = [0,1]^A$}
Let $\mathcal{M} = [0,1]^A$ with $A\in\mathbb{N}, \ A \geq 2$, and define the Euclidean distance $d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y) = \Vert x - y\Vert$. Fix $y_i = \bar y = (\tfrac{1}{2}, \dots, \tfrac{1}{2})$, the center of the hypercube. Then for any $x \in \mathcal{M}$, define $r = x - y_i$, so that $d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i) = \|r\|$. We note $I_A$ the identity matrix of dimension $A \times A$.

We have:
\[
\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i) = -\frac{r}{\|r\|}, \quad \left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right)\otimes\left(\nabla_{y_i} d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i)\right) = \frac{r r^\top}{\|r\|^2}.
\]
\[
\nabla_{y_i}^2 d_{\mathcal{M}}(x, y_i) = \frac{1}{\|r\|} \left(I - \frac{r r^\top}{\|r\|^2}\right)
\]

These expressions can now be substituted directly into the curvature condition:
\[
0 \leq \frac{\beta(N-2)}{N} \underbrace{\int_{\mathcal{M}} \frac{r r^\top}{\|r\|^2} \, dx}_{\triangleq \iota_1(r, A) = i_1\times I_A}
< \underbrace{\int_{\mathcal{M}} \frac{1}{\|r\|} \left(I_A - \frac{r r^\top}{\|r\|^2} \right) dx}_{\triangleq \iota_2(r, A) = i_2\times I_A}.
\]

As both sides are symmetric $A \times A$ matrices proportional to the identity matrix, we can rewrite the condition in scalar terms:
\[
0 \leq \frac{\beta(N-2)}{N}  i_1 < i_2 \underset{i_1 \neq 0}{\Leftrightarrow} \beta < \frac{i_2N}{i_1(N-2)}
\]

We can easily compute $i_1$:
\begin{align*}
\int_{[0,1]^A}\frac{r\,r^\top}{\|r\|^2}\,dx
&= \mathrm{diag}\Bigl(\,\int_{[0,1]^A}\frac{r_1^2}{\|r\|^2}\,dx,\dots,\int_{[0,1]^A}\frac{r_A^2}{\|r\|^2}\,dx\Bigr),\\[6pt]
\int_{[0,1]^A}\frac{r_i^2}{\|r\|^2}\,dx
&\overset{\text{symmetry}}= \frac{1}{A}\sum_{k=1}^A\int_{[0,1]^A}\frac{r_k^2}{\|r\|^2}\,dx
= \frac{1}{A}\int_{[0,1]^A}\frac{\sum_{k=1}^A r_k^2}{\|r\|^2}\,dx
= \frac{1}{A}\int_{[0,1]^A}1\,dx
= \frac{1}{A},\\[6pt]
\therefore\quad
i_1
&= I_A^{-1}\int_{[0,1]^A}\frac{r\,r^\top}{\|r\|^2}\,dx
= I_A^{-1}\,\bigl(\tfrac{1}{A}I_A\bigr)
= \frac{1}{A}.
\end{align*}

And for $i_2$, but this time we will not have a closed form solution:
\[
i_2 = \frac{1}{I_A}\int_{[0,1]^A}\frac{1}{\|r\|}\Bigl(I_A - \frac{r r^\top}{\|r\|^2}\Bigr)\,dx
      = \frac{1}{A}\int_{[0,1]^A}\frac{A-1}{\|r\|}\,dx
      = \frac{A-1}{A}\int_{[0,1]^A}\frac{1}{\|r\|}\,dx,\\
\]

We can rewrite our bound on beta:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:hypercube_upper_bound}
    \beta < \frac{i_2\,N}{i_1\,(N-2)}
         = \frac{\frac{A-1}{A}\displaystyle\int_{[0,1]^A}\frac{1}{\|r\|}\,dx\;\;N}
                {\frac{1}{A}(N-2)}
         = \frac{N}{N-2}\,\underbrace{\int_{[0,1]^A}\frac{A-1}{\|r\|}\,dx}_{\triangleq \bar i(A)}.
\end{equation}

This result supports the analytical claims of \cite{hehenkamp2010survival} in $N=2$: firms will always choose minimum differentiation (concentrated equilibrium) no matter of $\beta$ in the duopoly case. 

