\subsection{Generalized cylinder}
The remaining question of interest to us is the study of manifolds that do not have boundaries in every dimension. We foresee many applications for the use of this type of manifolds in Economics: the political spectrum for instance is bounded in some dimensions, but in others is periodic. Consider, for example, the case of inheritance taxation: strong support may arise from both ends of the political spectrum—from individuals identified as far-left (e.g., communists) and those categorized as far-right (e.g., extreme liberals). This oversimplified example advocates for the need of such manifolds to represent differentiation possibilities. 

In this last section, we will test Proposition \ref{prop:separability_cylinder} on a 2d-cylinder. For the sake of simplicity we do not provide analytical closed form solutions for the Cylinder as it would be redundant with the results we already obtained for the hypercube and the circle. Figure \ref{fig:2dCylinderSimuls} illustrate the theoretical results: firms do select only one position in the bounded direction $(y^{(2)})$ and several in the periodic $(y^{(1)})$. 

