\section{Conclusion}
We have developed a new microeconomic model for horizontal differentiation. This model fits our initial requirements: it encapsulates previous results from models said to be \textit{a la Hotelling}, it provides a solid new formalism to study the location choices of firms, and it offers exciting new results on general manifolds. 

Overall, we have proved that the initial Hotelling claim of minimal differentiation was more an exception than a general rule in more complicated spaces with more firms. It actually holds only in duopolies with Euclidean spaces, or with more firms but with consumer limited rationality. Despite being an interesting mathematical result, it is also of great economic interest, as it reconciles models with observed reality.

In addition, we have joined the results on the Salop circle with the ones on Euclidean spaces by explaining that the key change was the existence of boundaries. We have demonstrated that periodic spaces will always impose non-concentrated equilibrium, and that concentrated equilibrium can only occur if there is only one median on the manifold. This illustrated the need for a unified framework to bring together the individual advancements of different communities of researchers.

Finally, we have shown that the model was highly versatile and able to study a wide class of manifolds. This has been illustrated via our generalized cylinder, where we have shown that we were able to characterize the equilibrium component-wise. This mathematical challenge is again of great economic interest: we have proved that on feature spaces that are made of both bounded and periodic features, firms can select minimum differentiation in bounded features depending on consumer rationality while differentiating in the periodic ones. 

More generally, as this model does not make heavy assumptions on the manifold it studies, we pave the way to new experiments, data-driven, where the manifold could be learned from empirical data, to predict and finally empirically challenge the Hotelling model. This could open new avenues for future research, especially in marketing or in spatial economics. 






