% Our theoretical analysis showed that AUSSM is capable of implementing group-based automata, thereby enabling maximal expressivity in diagonal SSMs. We further show that AUSSM, when interleaved with Mamba blocks, achieves maximal representational power for regular languages under the Krohn-Rhodes decomposition framework. To address the challenge of the quadratic scaling of adaptive SSMs in practice, we developed a separable convolution formulation and an optimized CUDA kernel for AUSSM enabling performance exceeding existing partially adaptive SSMs. Our experiments confirm that this implementation yields favorable time and memory complexity, approaching that of Mamba, while providing substantially higher modeling capacity.
