\section*{Introduction}
Quantum computing promises transformative advances in a variety of applications, from combinatorial optimization to ground-state preparation and dynamical simulation of many-body systems \cite{grover1996fast,ambainis2019quantum}. However, large-scale quantum circuits remain challenging to implement due to hardware limitations, gate errors, and limited qubit coherence \cite{preskill2018quantum}. One of the most urgent goals in the noisy intermediate-scale quantum (NISQ) era, and the subsequent early fault-tolerant era \cite{katabarwa2024early} (Early FTQC), is to maximize computational utility within these constraints by employing shallow circuits and error-mitigated designs \cite{nanda2024explorative}.

Among the many strategies proposed to enable practical applications under these conditions, hybrid quantum–classical algorithms, particularly variational quantum algorithms (VQAs)  \cite{peruzzo2014variational,mcardle2020quantum,cerezo2021variational}, stand out. VQAs combine parameterized quantum circuits (PQC) with classical optimization routines, using quantum circuits as ansatzes to solve problems that can be formulated within a variational framework. Despite initial enthusiasm, standard VQAs face at least three critical challenges. First, optimization challenges, such as barren plateaus \cite{cerezo2021cost, grant2019initialization, holmes2021barren, holmes2022connecting, wang2021noise}, where gradients vanish exponentially with circuit depth or system size, severely limit trainability. Second, measurement costs scale poorly with both qubit count and circuit parameters, placing heavy demands on quantum resources. Third, even hardware-efficient circuit designs may require long depths to represent certain wavefunctions, but decoherence makes such computations unreliable in practice \cite{fedorov2022vqe}.

Meanwhile, deep learning has demonstrated remarkable capabilities in learning complex functions and high-dimensional structures from large datasets. In fact, neural networks (NNs) have set new standards for computational efficiency in tasks like quantum simulation \cite{bluvstein2021controlling,lange2024architectures,pfau2024accurate,hermann2020deep,carleo2017solving}, which are also commonly associated with quantum computing. A natural question then arises: can quantum resources enhance NN-based variational methods and help push the computational boundary of what is achievable with current and near-future hardware? Previous works have extensively investigated and documented that NNs can efficiently model the wavefunction amplitudes (i.e., essentially the probability distribution), but struggle to capture intricate sign or phase structures in many-body systems \cite{szabo2020neural,westerhout2020generalization,nomura2017restricted,cai2018approximating,carleo2017solving,preskill2018quantum}. On the other hand, modeling the phase structure of an arbitrary wavefunction can be well approximated with some variations of parameterized Instantaneous Quantum Polynomial (IQP) circuit \cite{westerhout2023many,park2025complexity,pagano2020quantum,leontica2024exploring,diez2024connection,bremner2016average}. This observation motivates one way to combine PQC with NN to jointly model complex wavefunction within a hybrid variational framework. Importantly, such models may exhibit potential quantum advantage if continuous variable IQP (CV-IQP) operations exist for a single iteration \cite{douce2017continuous,verdon2019quantum}.

Numerous efforts have explored the combination of quantum computing with deep learning in various settings \cite{bennewitz2022neural,termanova2024tensor,zoufal2019quantum}. An especially relevant example is the variational quantum-neural hybrid eigensolver (VQNHE) \cite{zhang2022variational} algorithm, previously developed by one of us and collaborators. This method uses a neural network to post-process quantum states prepared by a shallow circuit, forming a hybrid quantum-neural ansatz that efficiently expands the accessible quantum state space. However, VQNHE still faces limitations in trainability and incurs non-trivial measurement overhead.

In light of these developments and challenges, we revisit how best to harness both quantum and classical resources within a variational framework, one that maximizes hybrid advantages while mitigating their downsides. To this end, we propose sign-VQNHE (sVQNHE), a hybrid algorithm that comprehensively assuages all the major challenges facing mainstream VQAs: expressiveness, measurement cost, trainability, and robustness (see Table \ref{tab:compare}). In short, the key innovations of sVQNHE lie in its principled separation of quantum and classical roles: the diagonal layers of the quantum circuit are tasked to model the phase structure (with much reduced measurement costs), while a non-negative neural network only has to learn the amplitude (or effectively, the probability) distribution for improved numerical stability. These components are coupled through an iterative amplitude-transfer mechanism that maps the neural state onto a shallow and classically simulatable quantum layer. Compared to the original VQNHE, this re-design of the hybrid quantum-neural framework not only optimizes expressivity with shallow circuits and substantially improves the measurement costs and the gradient-based convergence rate, but also inherently mitigates sampling noise and decoherence, yielding more robust solutions under realistic conditions (see Results).

%The key idea is to integrate a PQC composed of commuting diagonal gates, which efficiently encode the sign (or phase) information, with a classical neural network (restricted to non-negative outputs) primarily responsible for modeling the wavefunction amplitudes. Furthermore,

During the iterative, bidirectional feedback optimization loop (to train the PQC and NN parameters), if a single diagonal quantum layer cannot fully capture the target wavefunction’s sign structure, we extend the circuit depth by adding a layer of classically simulatable gates along with a new diagonal layer. The classical gate layer approximates the amplitude function learned by the NN by solving a tractable optimization problem, allowing the NN to guide amplitude refinement and assist in layer-wise circuit optimization without encountering barren plateaus. Importantly, because only the parameters of commuting diagonal gates are optimized on the quantum device, the required number of quantum measurements is dramatically reduced compared to the conventional non-commuting circuit ansatzes. In practice, at the last layer of the iterative, bidirectional feedback optimization procedure, one can choose to relax the constraint and allow the neural network to output complex values, thereby enhancing the expressiveness of the hybrid quantum-neural ansatz. Finally, our new algorithm improves the convergence behavior of the original VQNHE by incorporating a stable layer-by-layer training strategy that suppresses numerical instabilities.

This newly proposed iterative and bidirectional feedback loop enables efficient training, expressive state representations, and significant reductions in circuit depth and measurement cost. Our numerical experiments validate these advantages. For instance, in the 6-qubit J1–J2 model, sVQNHE reduces mean absolute error by $98.9\%$ and variance by $99.6\%$ relative to a baseline NN, while achieving nearly 19× faster convergence compared to a hardware-efficient VQE using similar quantum resources. In solving the MaxCut problem on 45-node Erdős–Rényi graphs, sVQNHE improves solution quality by $19\%$ and quantum resource efficiency by $85\%$ over a brickwork ansatz VQE baseline \cite{sciorilli2025towards}.

In summary, sVQNHE offers a scalable and practical path forward for tackling complex many-body quantum simulations and large-scale combinatorial optimization problems by fully leveraging the synergy between classical and quantum computation. As both quantum hardware and neural network models continue to advance, we anticipate that hybrid frameworks like sVQNHE will play an increasingly important role in achieving real-world quantum utility.

