\noindent\textbf{Algorithm Features}\\
Measurement Efficiency via Commuting Gates. The quantum circuit employs commuting diagonal gates (e.g., $R_z$, $R_{zz}$) in $W$ block. Unlike conventional PQCs with non-commuting gates, which incur a measurement cost of $\mathcal{O}(2d)$, commuting gates reduce this to $\mathcal{O}(1)$, significantly lowering the overhead compared to VQNHE. Detailed strategies are elaborated in the Methods section.

Expressivity Hierarchy. The gates $R_z$, $R_y$, and $R_{zz}$ suffice for universality, enabling circuits with adjustable $G_l$ and $W_l$ structures to span the full quantum space. Even with a fixed structure, simple $G$ layers (e.g., a single layer $R_y$) can effectively adjust the exploration space (see Proposition \ref{propLayer}) and enhance the ansatz beyond QAOA’s expressivity (see Theorem \ref{the:lie_ansatz}).


Barren Plateau Suppression via Neural Guidance. The neural network $F_{l-1}$ initializes each $G_l$ in a low-entropy submanifold near the prior layer’s amplitude distribution, confining quantum exploration to relevant regions. This gradual amplitude transfer mitigates hardware noise and guides ansatz adjustments, improving predictions from noisy samples. Compared to the adapt-VQE class\cite{tang2021qubit,grant2019initialization}, neural network-guided ansatz adjustments can introduce certain entanglement capabilities and do not require the introduction of more quantum measurement costs to adjust parameters. A bidirectional feedback loop connects the components: the quantum circuit refines phase information for neural training, while classical gradients steer the quantum ansatz toward physically meaningful amplitude distributions.

Orthogonality via Unidirectional Information Flow. Our algorithm enforces strict classical-to-quantum information flow by iteratively aligning the classically simulable $G_l$ (shallow quantum circuit blocks)  with the classical part $F_{l-1}$. This can be performed by different schemes, such as:\\
1. Frobenius norm minimization (global): Minimize the full matrix distance:  $|G_l - F_{l-1}|_F^2$.\\
2. Test state projection consistency (local): For a set of Haar-random test states $\{|\psi_{\text{test}}^k\rangle\}$, enforce consistency in the projected probability distribution
\begin{equation}
    P_G^k(s) = |\langle s|G_l|\psi_{\text{test}}^k\rangle|^2, \quad P_F^k(s) = |\langle s|F_{l-1}|\psi_{\text{test}}^k\rangle|^2
\end{equation}
by minimize expected KL divergence $\mathbb{E}_k\left[ \text{KL}(P_G^k \| P_F^k) \right] \to 0$. This test state sampling provides a weaker but practical consistency condition, avoiding direct optimization of $G_l $.

\noindent As classical information is continuously integrated into the quantum, the algorithm can asymptotically push $f_{l}(s) \to 1$. Unlike original VQNHE—where quantum updates corrupt the classical part, inducing parameter redundancy ($I(Q;C) \gg 0$) and oscillatory instabilities—our unidirectional design restricts mutual information $I(Q;C)$ to a layer-wise schedule, reduce redundancy, and improve robustness.

