\subsection*{Experimental Results}
To rigorously validate the accuracy and efficiency of our proposed algorithm for the ground-state preparation of a given Hamiltonian, we conducted systematic evaluations across four canonical manybody problems: the 1D J1-J2 model, the 2D Heisenberg model, the Transverse Field Ising Model (TFIM), and the Classical Ising model. These models were chosen to represent diverse physical systems with varying degrees of sign problem severity and ground-state complexity. We employed three methodologies, sVQNHE, VQE, and NNs, to benchmark their performance. Additionally, we integrated the encoding scheme proposed in Ref. \cite{sciorilli2025towards} to demonstrate the superiority of sVQNHE over conventional VQAs (such as QAOA and VQE) in solving the MaxCut problem, a prototypical combinatorial optimization task.  

Our experiments included a 6-qubit 1D J1-J2 model with $J1/J2 = 0.6$, a configuration known to exhibit a highly non-trivial ground-state phase structure \cite{viteritti2022accuracy}. This model serves as a representative case for systems with intricate sign signatures. We also tested a 9-qubit 2D Heisenberg model with periodic boundary conditions, which is known to incur severe sign-related challenges for numerical methods\cite{stoudenmire2012studying}. For comparison, we analyzed a 9-qubit 1D TFIM (solvable via Jordan-Wigner transform) and two 1D Ising models (6 and 12 qubits). Both TFIM and Ising models we chose are Sign-problem-free systems. Nevertheless, they still present non-trivial challenges in different ways. For example, they are relevant to the qubit-formulated combinatorial optimization problems.
We evaluated our algorithm under four scenarios to assess its theoretical advantages and practical robustness: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item Ideal Simulation: Quantified intrinsic accuracy, variability, and scalability under ideal conditions.   
    \item Real-World Simulation: Assessed robustness under realistic quantum conditions, combining depolarization-based gate errors (two-qubit: $0.005$; single-qubit: $0.0013$) with bitstring-sampling-based estimation of observables and gradients.
    %Finite Sampling Errors: Assessed noise immunity and sampling efficiency under practical resource constraints.  
    %\item Quantum Device Noise: Tested resilience against depolarizing noise (two-qubit: 0.005, single-qubit: 0.0013), mirroring real hardware conditions.  
    \item Scalability Under Finite Sampling: Demonstrated that sVQNHE’s advantage over existing methods\cite{sciorilli2025towards} can be sustained with system size. 
\end{itemize}
We emphasize that the results of all the so-called "non-ideal" experiments are based on the original sampling data (of bitstrings) from noisy quantum circuits, without implementing any quantum error mitigation procedures. As sVQNHE possesses an inherent and robust error mitigating capability \cite{zhang2023variational} due to the fact that the hybrid quantum-neural ansatz can be directly tuned with the classical NN module during the noisy optimization process.
