\subsubsection*{1. Ideal Simulation}
We conducted a systematic evaluation of the performance of sVQNHE and other methods (VQNHE, VQE, NN, QAOA) focusing on accuracy, variability, and measurement efficiency. All the calculations were done in statevector-type exact numerical computations.
For consistency, sVQNHE and VQNHE utilized the same non-negative real neural network component (MLP), while the NN method employed a real MLP with identical parameters and structure. The quantum components of sVQNHE and VQNHE were our single-layer sign ansatz and a hardware-efficient ansatz with a similar number of parameters, respectively. In Table \ref{tab:all_model}, these are denoted as sign-NN and hea-NN, respectively. The VQE method used a hardware-efficient ansatz with varying layers, such as HEA2 for two layers. Similarly, the QAOA method was tested with different layers, for instance, QAOA10 for ten layers. All methods initialized quantum circuit parameters using independent uniform distributions $U(-2\pi, 2\pi)$, optimized for the same number of steps, and averaged the estimated energy from 20 random initial points. The baseline algorithm in Table \ref{tab:all_model} is NN.


\noindent\textbf{\\ Simulating Frustrated Many-Body Systems}

For the 1D J1-J2 model, which features a non-trivial ground state, the sVQNHE method (sign-NN) demonstrated significant improvements over the NN method, the VQE method (HEA2), and the VQNHE method (hea-NN). Specifically, Table \ref{tab:all_model} shows that after 2000 optimization steps, the sign-NN method reduced the mean absolute error (MAE) by $98.9\%$ and the variance by $99.6\%$ for the 6-qubit J1-J2 model compared to the NN method. Additionally, Figure \ref{fig:J1-J2} illustrates the scaling results (from 6 qubits to 12 qubits) of sign-NN and NN with the same number of optimization steps to achieve the minimum error. The advantage of sign-NN increases with the number of qubits. In contrast, while hea-NN also reduced the MAE by $75.1\%$ compared to NN, its variance was larger than that of NN (variance: hea-NN > NN > sign-NN). Furthermore, due to the non-commutative structure of hea, the measurement amount for sign-NN was significantly smaller than that for hea-NN under similar parameter amounts. These three points—improved accuracy, reduced variance, and reduced measurement amount—demonstrate the superiority of sVQNHE over the original VQNHE algorithm. Regarding convergence speed, sign-NN significantly outperformed HEA2. To achieve the same error threshold, HEA2 required 12.6 times more optimization steps on average than sign-NN. In the most efficient cases, HEA2 needed 1676 steps to reach the same accuracy, while sign-NN only required 89 steps. Additionally, the probability of the sign-NN successfully reaching the target accuracy was 12 times higher than that of HEA2, highlighting its excellent reliability and efficiency (for details, please refer to the supplementary material). Similar results were observed in the Heisenberg model, further verifying the capability of sVQNHE to handle systems affected by the sign problem.
