\noindent\textbf{\\ Simulating Sign-Problem-Free Many-Body Systems}

Although the above results highlight the advantages of sVQNHE in sign-frustrated quantum models, the same architectural design is effective even in sign-free systems, where phase modeling is trivial for the ground state itself, but the ability to explore a broader Hilbert space (as defined by the PQC-generated phase strcutures) turns out to be beneficial for the optimization convergence in the numerical investigated presented in this subsection. More precisely, please see the last three rows of Table \ref{tab:all_model}.

In our numerical experiments on the Ising model, the sign-NN approach demonstrates substantial improvements over the NN in both error reduction and variance compression. At 9 qubits, sign-NN achieves a $41.6\%$ reduction in MAE compared to NN, indicating a significant enhancement in accuracy. This advantage becomes even more pronounced at 12 qubits, where sign-NN reduces MAE by $81.1\%$ relative to NN, showcasing its superior scalability with increasing system size. Similarly, in terms of variance, sign-NN outperforms NN by reducing variance by $45.5\%$ at 9 qubits. This gap widens dramatically at 12 qubits, with sign-NN achieving a $97.9\%$ reduction in variance compared to NN, highlighting its ability to deliver consistent and reliable results as the system scales. When benchmarked against the QAOA in the 9-qubit Ising model, sign-NN maintains its performance edge. A 5-layer QAOA configuration requires a comparable number of per-iteration measurements to sign-NN but yields a higher MAE, indicating lower accuracy. Extending QAOA to 10 layers improves its accuracy, yet it still falls short of sign-NN’s performance. This trend persists and becomes more evident at 12 qubits, where sign-NN’s scalability further amplifies its advantage over QAOA. The consistency of these results extends to the TFIM.
The superior performance of sign-NN can be attributed to its carefully designed ansatz, optimized with neural network guidance. Unlike QAOA, which uses $R_x$ gates and constrains all $Z$- and $X$-related gates within a layer to share a single parameter, the Sign Ansatz employs $R_{zz}$ and $R_y$ gates with independent parameters. This design, as supported by Theorem \ref{the:lie_ansatz}, generates a higher-dimensional Lie algebra ($\mathfrak{g}_1$) compared to QAOA’s ($\mathfrak{g}_2$), enabling sign-NN to explore a broader unitary space through multi-qubit $Z$-phase adjustments. The phase-focusing architecture of the Sign Ansatz, which leverages multi-qubit $Z$-gates to refine phase information, allows for efficient navigation of high-dimensional solution spaces. When combined with neural network optimization, this approach achieves high accuracy and low variance at a reduced computational cost, making sVQNHE particularly well-suited for large-scale quantum optimization problems.

%This enhanced expressibility allows us to explore more highly entangled state spaces in simulating adiabatic evolution, which corresponds to gradually simulating the ground state of the corresponding TFIM model, thereby ultimately obtaining higher-quality solutions.\cite{rajak2023quantum,albash2020validating} Furthermore, since the ground state from the previous evolution step serves as a good guide for the next step, and with additional guidance from the neural network, we estimate that the trainability issue is not more severe than that of the deep QAOA method, which can achieve a similar accessible unitary space size. 


