\subsubsection*{3. Scaling Under Sampling Errors}
Finally, we investigate whether the observed advantage of sVQNHE can be sustained as the system size increases under the real-world setting of extracting PQC state by sampling bitstrings on quantum circuits. An example is provided in Figure \ref{fig:sampling_scaling}. We evaluate the solution quality and measurement cost of the MaxCut problem on Erdős–Rényi random graphs with an edge connection probability of $0.3$ as the graph size increases. To establish this advantage, we compare sVQNHE against the Sign Ansatz VQE (sign-VQE) and the recently proposed brickwork-VQE \cite{sciorilli2025towards}, all evaluated under the same polynomial encoding model. This model encodes the $m$ binary variables of the MaxCut problem into the Pauli correlations across $k$ qubits, enabling the representation of $m = O(n^k)$ variables with only $n$ qubits, where $k > 1$ is an integer. Unlike the direct encoding schemes like QAOA, which require $m$ qubits—one per variable—this approach achieves polynomial compression in the qubit count. The desired solution of the combinatorial problem is now encoded in terms of a non-classical Hamiltonian. The compression ratio depends on the polynomial order $k$, allowing us to tackle larger MaxCut instances with fewer qubits. All methods are evaluated under a unified encoding framework with $k=2$ to ensure fairness.

To evaluate solution quality across varying scales, we conducted experiments with 1,200 measurement shots, averaging results over multiple trials. As shown in Figure \ref{fig:sampling_scaling}(Left), scaling sVQNHE parameters—through enhancements in the NN component or other optimizations—consistently widens its performance gap over competing methods. Specifically, sVQNHE, which employs NN-guided hierarchical optimization and NN post-processing, significantly outperforms sign-VQE and brickwork-VQE. Sign-VQE adopts the final PQC structure from sVQNHE (a two-layer sign ansatz with two diagonal and two non-diagonal layers) but omits the NN-guided optimization and post-processing used in sVQNHE. Brickwork-VQE, in contrast, utilizes a VQE with a brickwork ansatz, as detailed in \cite{sciorilli2025towards}. 
Figure \ref{fig:sampling_scaling}(Left) reveals that, without NN guidance and post-processing (sign-VQE vs. brickwork-VQE), the sign ansatz shows no clear advantage over the brickwork ansatz, with comparable solution quality and similar measurement costs. However, sVQNHE's hybrid approach - integrating hierarchical optimization guided by NN and NN post-processing to Sign Ansatz - yields a marked improvement. For instance, the performance gap between sVQNHE and baseline methods increases by approximately $6\% \approx (0.96-0.90)/0.90$ for 9-vertex graphs and $19\% \approx (1 - 0.84)/0.84 $ for 45-vertex graphs when using parameter set 2. Additionally, Figure \ref{fig:sampling_scaling}(Right) shows that sVQNHE significantly reduces single-step measurement consumption compared to both sign-VQE and brickwork-VQE. With parameter set 2, it achieves $86\% \approx (72 - 10)/72$ and $85\% \approx (306 - 46)/306$ improvements in quantum resource efficiency for 9- and 45-vertex systems, respectively. Additionally, in the Supplementary Information, we provide a preliminary comparison of resource consumption for larger instances, further highlighting sVQNHE's advantages in scalability and efficiency under large-scale conditions. This underscores its potential for large-scale quantum applications.

