\subsection*{Sampling Efficiency Verification}
This section presents a compelling argument for performing sampling from the quantum circuit component rather than the classical neural network component that describes the amplitude. Our objective is to verify the computational advantages in the main text argument. As illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:samp_nn}, we compare the convergence curve of our model with that shown in Figures 3 and 4 of the main text. The key difference is that the set of bitstrings $s$ here is obtained by sampling from the classical neural network component. Compared to the results in Figures 3 and 4 of the main text, we observe that performing sampling from the quantum circuit component is better; fewer shots can yield more stable iterations and faster convergence. This suggests that our quantum circuit can effectively sample from complex probability distributions that are intractable for classical computers, thereby providing a high-quality solution space that closer approximates the true solution space.
