\section{Properties of the Product Log Function}\label{AS:PL}

In this section we collect facts about the product log function $\plog{}$, defined as the inverse of $y e^y$ on $(-1,\infty)$. First, direct calculation using $\plog{z}e^{\plog{z}} = y$ shows that
\begin{equation}\label{E:plog_deriv}
\partial_z \plog{z} = \frac{\plog{z}}{z(1+\plog{z})}.
\end{equation}



Next, we state a lemma  used to provide gradient estimates when solving the PDE in \eqref{E:PDE_2}.

\begin{lemma}\label{L:plog_quad}
For $z\in\reals$ and $K>0$ we have $\plog{Ke^z}^2 + 2\plog{Ke^z}\leq 2K + z^2$ with equality if and only if $K=z$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof of Lemma \ref{L:plog_quad}]

Set $h(K,z) = 2k + z^2 - \plog{Ke^z}^2 - 2\plog{Ke^z}$. From \eqref{E:plog_deriv} we see that $\partial_z h(K,z) = 2(z - \plog{Ke^z})$ and $\partial_{zz} h(K,z) = 2/(1+\plog{Ke^z}) > 0$. Thus, for $K$ fixed, $h(K,z)$ has a unique minimum (in $z$) at $z = \plog{Ke^z}$ or equivalently when $z=K$, and when $z=K$ we have $h(K,z) =0$.

\end{proof}

